        <p>DR. J. COLLIS BROWNE'S CHLORODYNB.</p>
        <p>THE ORIGINAL AND ONLY GENUINE. fIifLORODYNE is admitted by the Profession to k../ be the moot wonderful and valuable remedy ever die- covered.</p>
        <p>CORODYNIZ is the best remedy known for Coughs, Consumption, Bronchitis, and Asthma. 011LORODYNE effectually checks and arrests thaw too cften fatal diseases.—Diptheria, Fever. Creep. te. 1110RODYNE acts like a charm in Diarrhoea, and is the only specific in Cholera and Dysentry. CIILORODYNE effect ually cuts short all attacks of Epilepsy. Hysteria, Palpitation, and Spasms. CLOHODYNE is the only palliaive in Neural- gia, Ilkeuinatam, Gm.:t, Cancer, Toothache, ezlingi tie,</p>
        <p>From pr. B. T. BoULTON &amp; CO., Horne...sale., We have made pretty extensive use of Chlorodyne in our practice lately, and look upon it as an excellent direct Seda- tive and Anti-spasmodic. It seems to allay pain and irrita- tion in whatever organ, arot trom whatever cause. It Induces a feeling of comfort and quietude not obtainable by any other remedy, and it seems to possess this gre ad- age over all other Sedatives ; that it leaves no IlltpleaSant after effects.</p>
        <p>Earl Russell communicated to the College of l'hystelaas that he received a despatch from her Majesty's Consul at Manilla, to the effect that Cholera kad been raging feartully, and that the ONLY remedy of any service was CI-ILO/16- Dnill."-6ee Lancet , let December, 1864.</p>
        <p>CAUTION.-BEWARE OF PIRACY AND IMIT- ATIONS.</p>
        <p>CArTIOY.—ViCs Chancellor Sir W. Paciz WOOD stated that Dr. J. COLL/8 BRoWNE. was, undoubtedly, the inventor of CHLORODYNE "and that the story of the defendant FREE- MAN, was deliberately untrue, which he regretted to sfq , sled been sworn to.—See Times. 13th July. 1864. , Sold in bottles at is. 9.1., and 4s. 6d. None is genuine without the words "li. J. COLLIS BROWNE'S CHLORODYNE "on the Government Stamp. Overwhelm- ing Medical testimony accompanies each bottle. SOLE MasurFAcruans.s.—J . T. DAVIINPORT 33 Great , Bloomsbury. London.</p>
        <p>KEATING'S COUGH LOZENGES. 94, Commerctal load, Peckham, July 12, 1889. " Drer Sir,— I am a poor hand at expressing my feelings, but I should Lite to thank you. Your lozenges have done wonders to relieving my terrible cough. Since I had the operation of • Tra,theotomy (the same as the late Emperor of Germany, end unlike him. thank God, I am still alive) per- formed at St.. Baitholomew's Hospital, no one could possibly have had a more violedt cough; it was so bad at times that it quite exhausteil me. The iv UM?, which was very copious and hard, has been soiteoed, and I have been able to get rid ot it without difficulty.—I am, sir, yours truly, J. HILL."</p>
        <p>ITE 0.1,Y UNRIVALLED. The qbove , a.. ,i or tself. From strict inquiry it appears that the benefit, front using Beating's Cough Lozenges is understateq. The oporatio.s ss as a specially severe one, and was rt•rformed by the specialist, Dr. H. T. Butlin, ot St. Bartfolome W'6 Hospital. :iince the operation the only means of relief is the use M these Lozenges. So successful are they that one affords immediate benefit, although from the nature of tie ease the throat irritation is intense.</p>
        <p>WEIWIT IN GOLD.</p>
        <p>• • -- - - under date Sept. 8, 1691, Air. llill writes : "I should I(ng since have been tisa:L but for your Lozenges—they are worth their weig4t in yo/d. I will gladly see and tell anyone what a splendid cough remedy they are. Keating's Lozenges are sold in tins, is. I.ld. each. TI e unriv,lled remedy for COUUHS, If OARSENIiss at,d. THROAT TROUBLES.</p>
