      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>'CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>'CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>The salary list at the Garrick when Mr. John Ilearrevives" Diplomacy" will be a pretty heavy one if all the company—a galaxy of" stars "—ar. paid at the same handsome sate as Mr. and Mrs Bancroft, who will receive between them a weekly salary of .£l5O. It is not only in a business sense that relations are to be established between the Bancrofts and the Hares, for a daughter of Mr. Bare is to be married shortly to the elder son of AIL Bancroft.</p>
        <p>There are, writes a correspondent, few surgeons with a bigger reputation in Germany than Dr. Von Esraarch, the professor at the University of Kiel, who recently celebrated his seventieth birthday. The famous professor has been a teacher of the medical faculty there for over thirty years, and it is said of him that during that period he has per- formed more than fourteen thousand operations. Dr. Von Esmarch is still a bale and hearty man, with a fund of good humour and a smooth kindly voice, while at the university with which he has been so long associated his popularity is great. His second wife is an aunt ot the German Lmpress and a Princess of Schleswig-Holstein.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Shaw, the senior member for Halifax, whose death creates a vacancy in that Radical borough, is a man who will be universally missed in the House of Commons. His geniality made him a favourite with all sections of the House ; and even its humble attendants will grieve for the loss of a kindly and courteous benefactor. Mr. iShaw,en- tered Parliament late in life. He was not a speaker, but he was a good voter, and always turned up on the Radical side. He had passed through the various municipal offices in Halifax before becom- ing her Parliamentary representative, and his mayoralty will long bo remembered for his sump- tuous banquets and genial hospitality as well as for the benefits it conferred on the town.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shaw was head of a great manufacturing firm, in which he is now represented by his nephew and son. The firm have been proverbial as good employers. In his younger days he lived at the works, but many years ago he took up house in the suburbs of Halifax, where the name of Allingate, his residence, soon became synonymous with hospi- tality and good cheer. Mrs. Shaw, who survives him, was the daughter of Mr. Rawson, who was treasurer of the Anti-Corn Law League, and she preserves many interesting reminiscences of the great leaders of that movement. The sympathy of a vast circle of friends will attend her in her bereavement.</p>
        <p>Some amusement will be caused by the announce- ment that the Marquis of Queensberry will give an address to women at the Princes' Hall on" Marriage and the Relation of the Sexes" Themarquia's own marital venture was not a success, but probably he considers his passage through the Divorce Court gives him the right to air his opinions. It.ces- tainly gives him an intimate knowledge of one of the possibilities of marriage. The present marquis —the eighth—ls a direct descendant of Sir Charles Douglas, a distant kinsman of 4' 01dQ.," the lam- ous boxing authority, who was at once Marquis and Duke of Queensberry. At his death the duke- dom went to the Duke of Buccleuch and the marquisate to Sir Charles Douglas.</p>
        <p>The children of the seventh marquis seem to bare inherited many of the eccentricities of "Old Q." Lord James Douglas, brother of the present marquis, and twin brother to Lady Florence Dixie, gained some notoriety two years ago by describing his wife on a census paper as a cross between a sweep and a" lunatic. A month later he commit- ted suicide. The interruption made by ,the mar- quis himself, at the performance of Lord Tennyson's /Promise of May," by rising in his stall and de- livering an harangue on agnosticism, is a matter of history, as is also his divorce in 1887. To complete the famtiy tale of eccentricity, his sister, Lady Gertrude Dangles, married a Chelsea baker. Almost one of the first subscriptions to the "Darkest England" fund was from the marquis, who sent A cheque for 1:100 "as a reverent agnostic, to our common cause of humanity." The originally large family estates have been much injured by the ex- cesses and extravagances of several generations. The heir to them is Viscount Drumlanrig. who was recently appointed private secretary to Lord Rose- bery. The marquis has two other sous and a daughter.</p>
        <p>With the death of Mr. Terence O'Brien, of Bel- fast, a very remarkable figure passed awa:ir from Irish public life. He was one of the men under police surveillance for many years, owing to his identiflation with the Fenian rebellion of 1867, and be subsequently became one of the leaders in ,Illster of the Home Rule movement initiated by Isaac Butt and Colonel Ring-Harman, WO of them .at that time prosounced Conservatives. He was a -.wan of rare literary attainments, being one of the test linguists in Ireland, whilst his enthusiasm on 'educational or coatroversial topics was unbounded. Ee was the most intimate friend of the late Mr. ;Joseph Biggar, who placed unlimited confidence in his judgment, and the two were almost inseparable eaispanions. Mr. O'Brien was a man of almost Ltiliputian stature, but doggedness was stamped on his countenance.</p>
        <p>Regent's Park Chapel, has secured in the Bev. B. 8. Grange. who has accepted a" call "to its pulpit', petthr-ps the most popular Nonconformist divine the west of England. Old Broadmead Chapel,which has heen for many years—ne fewer than 23 in fact —the scene of Mr. Grange's labours is pretty much the Tab.q•nacle of Bristal. It has a large regular</p>
        <p>eongregation, as well as be a favourite worship:- lug place with what may be called the irregular body of chapel-goers. For this latter class Mr, Grange's preaching had considerable attraction. He is arpepular preacher as opposed to the" fitly, - my brethren" type, is a fitient speaker on general matters;has a fund of anecode,humerous and other- wise, and makes a capital lecturer. Though trained in the rastors' College. he was one of the late Mr. Spurgeon! s chief opponents,in the " down-grade " controversy. His departure will be sincerely re- gretted by his Bristol congregation. Taymouth Castle, where the Marquis and Marchioness of Breadalbane are entertaining a party far the opening of the salmon angling season, is one of the, most complete of the Scotch baronial residences. ilt, is strikingly situated at the foot of shill that is forest-clad to the .summit, and stands on the site of the old house of Balloch, which dated from the sixteenth century. The castle was forty years in-building, the final addition being made when the west wing was added in 1842, on the occasion of the Queen's visit. -The mansion is ,of dark grey-coloured stone. It consists of a square central portion, with corner towers and a pavilion. 'lwo wings of considerablyless elevation jut out from the main edifice. Some of the apartments are very fine. In the breakfast room, which is a part of the original building, is hung the best existing collec- tion of the works of Jameson. of Aberdeen, who bas.heen called "the Scotch Vandyke:" The sal- moulfishing in the Tay is widely celebrated, and this Fear's prospects are said to be unusually good.</p>
        <p>Sir F..obert Gresley, who is shortly to be married to Lady Frances Spencer-Churchill, is the holder of one of the oldest barooetcies in the kingdom, that somewhat doubtful honour having been conferred in 1611 upon his erudite ancestor, George Gresley, the friend .of the Earl of Atundale and the patron of many literary persons of the period. Sir Robert —whose ",elairas of long descent" may vie with Chose of our bluest blooded families, for his for- bears were the hereditary standard bearers of the Dukes of Normany—is a rather boyish-looking baronet of six-and-twenty, tall and slight, with a, pale clean-shaven face, pleasant grey eyes, and dark! hair. He is the owner of an ample fortune, and a fine estate in Derbyshire, Drakelow, the home of the Gresleys since the fourteenth century, and their property Firice the Conquest. Sir Robert's future bride, Lady Frances Spencer Churchill, who is four years younger than himself, is the eldest daughter of the late Duke of Marlborough and the eldest sister of the new Duke. who attained his title and his majority almost simultaneously a abort time since.</p>
        <p>The vastness and the probable destination of the fortune of the late Mr. H. T. Coghlan has been com- mented upon by most of the London journals. Three wills were made at different times by the deceased gentleman, and three firms of solicitors have been anxious to find them, or at any rate one of them. But though the big house in Hyde-park- gardens and the boxes at the various bankers have been ransacked, no will has yet been discovered, and the chances are greatly in favor of Sir 11. D. Broughton and the three ether cousins of the de- cessed dividing nearly .£700.000 between them. Mr. c,--,:bian had been building up his fortune for more Visa s century, and bad done 80 by judicious or</p>
        <p>lucky investments. Ile was not always right, for some of his bonds are of about the same italue aa the paper they are printed on. He wis a believer in Turks and in them a large part of his fortune was made, for he bought at the time of the Russo- Turco war, when the public sold at _panic prices. 'Spanish securities were also much favored by Mr. Cogblan, who occupied the probably unique posi- tion of having a Spanish loan all to himself, having declined to convert when the proposal was made by the Spanish Government and accepted by all the ether holders. Mr. Coghlan's father, who left him the nucleus of his great fortune, was a general officer in the English Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>BELGIAN DRAUGHT HORSE.</head>
        <p>THE BELGIAN DRAUGHT HORSE.</p>
        <p>A report on Belgian draught horses, by the American Consul at Brussels, has lately been published by the Department of State at Washington. There are, it seems two pure breeds of horses in Belgium—the Ardennes horse, from the Valley of the Meuse, and the Frisian from the sea coast. The Brabancon horse is the issue of the two. These form the three main breeds of draught horses, and great care has been given to their breeding, and with the exception of the English shire horse, they are the largest draught horses in the world, being fine proportioned and with excellent legs and feet. The Ardennes horse is an excellent type of a light draught horse, and is perfectly adapted for mountainous regions. It is, however, rapidly disappearing, as the Luxemburg farmers prefer the heavy draught horse, and the intro- duction of large heavy stallions from Brabant and Hainaut has almost completely transformed the breed. 'For some years much attention has been devoted to the improvement of the Brabancon horse. The line of the back is now much straighter, the hind part longer, the neck and shoulders more proportionate, and the legs large and clean. Harnessed to the heavy carts of the county, which weigh about 3,000 pounds, it will pull on the level a weight of from 6,000 to 10,000 pounds, and will work from eight to 10 hours daily. It is considered the best draught breed in the kingdom, and is absorbing all the others. The recent exhibitions prove that there are now no distinctly pure breeds in sufficient numbers to form classes by themselves, and that for practical purposes there are only heavy and light draught horses. The special characteristics of the former are stature, form, strength, and breed. They have less bulk and strength than the Clydesdale or shire horse, but they are •better bred and have more energy. They have more bulk and strength than either the Perchsron or Boulan- nais horse, with quite as much energy. At one time the • Government maintained a number of stallions, but the system did not prove successful, and now horse-breeding is left to private enterprise, en...ouraged by a system of registration and prizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>CANAL CONSTRUCTION IN AMERICA.</head>
        <p>CANAL CONSTRUCTION IN AMERICA.</p>
        <p>The plans for the ship canals between Duluth,Chicago, and Buffalo have now been completed, and an idea can be formed of the magnitude of this undertaking. The work will be carried out in eight sections, the eighth and largest extending for some 28,500 feet. A depth of 21 feet is required for all except the sixth and seventh sections, where it will be 20 feet. The two latter are probably the most important sections. The sixth re- quires the removal of 950,009 cubic yards for the im- provement of the St. Clair Flats. The channel is to begin at the deep watetr of the St. Clair river above the St, Clair Flats Canal, with a bottom width of not more than 850 feet, thenee narrowing gradually to the canal for a distance of about 3,000 feet, and finally widening out into a channel 17,000 feet long, and not more than 800 feet broad at the bottom. The seventh section re- .quires the improvement of Grove Point Flats, and the removal of fro:n 1,200,000 to 2.900,000 cubic yards of earth. Work at the various sections is to begun on or .before the 15th of May, and it is to be hoped that it will be finished by the 20th of November. 1895. This it is expected will give a period of 200 working days. Con- gress has appropriated for the work at the commence- ment the sum of 375,000 dols., and has limited the cost of the,canal complete to 3.340,000 dols. Dredging con- tractors from all part's of the lakes have been for some time, past bidding on the different sections of the 20-foot channel project. The lowest figures are all below the engineer's estimates, so much so, that it is now posi- tively assured that the entire work will be put under contract for the maximum width of 800 feet. On the most important section the work at Grove Point Flats, which involves the removal of about 2,900.000 cubic feet of clay, gravel, and sand, the price asked by the successful contractors, 13reyman Brothers, of' Toledo,was 6 cents, a yard less than that of the next lowest bidder, which would he equal to about 175,000 dols.on the job. The Messrs. Breyman had dug boles all over the section before putting in a bid. They own the largest dredger in the United States and are in every way capablo of carrying out the contract. They have already a con- tract with11.00;000dols. with the United States Govern- ment for straight channel work at Toledo.—" Trans- port?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>SCISICZE4 IN THE CAPITAL. OF</head>
        <p>SCISICZE4 IN THE CAPITAL. OF</p>
        <p>SENEGAL.</p>
        <p>• - Perhaps the place where one can see most of the varied life of the capital of Senegal is on the Tont Faidherbe, between half-past five and half-past six of an evening. This bridge is more than. a quarteeof a mile in length, and, as already stated, is made of limits moored fore and aft. When a south-westerly sll comes up from the mouth of the river, the bridge sways from one side to the other with a short vie. k movement, making it difficult for, persons not accus- tomed ta board-ship to keep their footing, and some- times causing native children to suffer from mal-de- mer. When, however, the wind is in any other direc- tion the bridge is perfectly steady, and after the heat of the day one can sit in the recesses made for the purpose and enjoy a little fiesh air while watching the passers-by. There is sure to be a Moorish caravan. consisting perhaps of half-a-dozen cameis4 coming it with heavy burdens from the interior, the tail of one animal tied by a light card to a ring in the nose of thin succeeding one, the procession headed by a single Moor, with his short lithe step, holding the, cord attached to the foremost camel. Some four or five others: walk by the side or the end of the caravan, Then ccme a batch of young girls returning from/ washing clothes at some roadside well Where the wateri is cleaner though perhaps more brackish than the river. water. The clothes are contained in large calabashes,' carried on the head, and steadied with one or both, uplifted arms. la spite of the distance they havd walked, and the ft.tigtip of ,maintaining their arms in that elevated position, they seem cheerful enough,' laughing and chatting as they move along, though the. burden they carry prevents them , from tuitnlfie! their head to right or left. Here pass, too, plenty of Jeloff , ,men, laughing and talking at the top of their voices as, they walk along at a swinging pace, brandishing their: 'heavy walking sticks, while their long white robes* ap out behind them under the impulse of the stronel ,evening • breeze. Much more slowly approach some: half-dozen Joloff women who have been attending at festivity at Sor, and are decked out in their most' gorgeous attire. They walk abreast, and take posses- Egon of the bridge, looking with undisguised coatempt *tithe quiet-coloured garments of the Moorish women whom they meet. Soon a boy of 12, belonging to the poorer class, and wearing only the scantiest of loin clothe, staggers past under the weight of a freshly killed sheep from the slaughtering ground on the far- • ther side of the river. Senegal sheep are horned and' small, but they are too heavy for a young lad, and th. carcase with its gaping throat seems likely to drive the small black head which carries it down between the slender nformed shoulders.—From "Blackwood's Maga- zine.'</p>
        <p>QUITE TIi.U,D! Sufferers ,irom Gravel. Lumbigo, Piles, Pains in the Back, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaints. Diseases of Kidneys, Bladder, Urinary Organs, Stone, Gleet, Stric- ture, Sciatica, Rheumatism, and Gout, will find a positive cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box, and if not satisfied your money will be returned. Prise is lid. Of all chemists, or post free for 12 stamps from HOLD- ROYD'S Medical Hall, Cleckheaton, Yorks. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor, and a box will be sent next post.—Advt. John Shaw Trutnag, who stated that he was a native of Stockport, was arrested on Tuesday at Matlock on suspicion of murdering Mrs. Pearcey at Bromsgrove. It is stated that the axe with which the crime was com- mitted was stolen from a building in course of alteration at 2irmingharn. ADVICE To MorrrEns t—Are you broken in your rest by a sick child suffering with the pain of cutting teeth? Go at once to a chemist and get a bottle of Miss. WnsaLoW'S SooTxtrato B.VRUP. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is Perfectly harmless and pleasant to taste, it produces natural, quiet sleep, by relieving the child from Dahl, and the little cherub awakes "as bright as a button." It soothes the child, If softens the gums, allays all pain, relieves wind-, regulates the bowels, and is the best known remedy for dysentry arid diar- rlicea, whether arising from teething or other causes. Mrs. Winslow's soothing Syrup is sold by Medicine dealers every- where at is, lid. per bottle.—Adrt.</p>
