      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 21, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 21, 1893.</p>
        <p>THE FATAL " CAROUSAL " AT</p>
        <p>questioned by Mr. Swift, witness said now that he had heard the evidence, most probably if a post-Inortess examination could be made—and it could not be told without—they would find a crack in the part of the skull under which the brain rested.—Mr. Grimke-Bray- ton (from the Bench) : Would it be possible for him to have had nine or ten glasses of drink, as stated, and you to get only a gill of fluid from his stomach 2 Witness: It is hardly possible.—The Superintendent: Did you tell Police-constable Simpson that you never saw a clearer case of alcoholic poisoning in your life 2 Witness: Certainly not.—The Superintendent: You distinctly deny that you ever made such a statement 2 Witness: Yes.—The Superintendent: I have other evidence. Hay&amp; you seen it stated in the papers that you said so i—Witness : I saw it in the papers; they will publish 1. ny thin g .—The S u perinten dent : Be careful. I can call evidence if I want, that you made that state- ment. —Witness : I was very much surprised when I saw it. After a brief consultation, the Bench dismissed the case on both charges.</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTON SOLICITOR AND THE NEW FORM OF OATH.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court, nn Thursday, Mr. Jenkins, solicitor, made an application for a commitment under judgment summons, for non-payment of arrears due under a deed for the maintenance of an illegitimate child. Mr. Jenkins desired to be sworn as a witness according to the method allowed by the new Act of Parliament, namely with uplifted hand. Mr. Jenkins raised his right hand as he made the application.—The judge : Have you no religious belief ?—Mr. Jenkins : I simply make the request having regard to the epidemic now prevail- ing.—The Judge: lam afraid I cannot allow it.—Mr. Jenkins : Will you allow me to call attention to the case ?—His Honour: If you object to the oath that is , another thing, but if you do not object to it I think you must be sworn on thu Testament.—Mr. Jenkins asked permission to cite a case in which it was held that the form of oath be preferred was biading.—His Honour said he must call upon Mr. Jenkins to observe the usual form of kissing the book.—Mr. Jenkins then took the usual form of oath.</p>
        <p>THE DISEASES OF PLANTS. On Saturday evening, Mr. A. T. Gillanders, of High Legh, gave a lecture on the above subject in the school- room. The chair was occupied by the Hon. J. E. Cross, who, in introducing the lecturer' said he was pleased to see so large an audience, and he hoped they might have many more meetings of this kind. In the course -A his able and somewhat witty address, Mr. Gillanders referred to the potato disease, doubtless known to most of those present, and which was first noticed in the Isle of Wight, in 1844, and spread with alarming rapidity. It generally made its appearance towards the end of June. The disease showed its symptoms by pale spots on the leaves gradually assuming a brownish hue. With the aid of a mlcroscope, 2,370 of these small bodies could be found to the twelfth part of the square inch. A remedy had been tried, but it did not answer satis- factorily by turning the stem, or wurtzel, into the bot- tom of the drill so as to catch the rain, and wash the disease out. A better remedy was to get 20Ibs. copper, 201 b. lime, and mix with about 100 gallons of water, and spray the leaves with the mixture. The cost of treating an acre in this manner, including labour, was about 10s. The average additional yield of potatoes , was about 53i bushels per acre. He next dealt with " Rust "in wheat. It was computed that Australia lost £2,000,000 annually from this disease. lhe same disease affected the grass, &amp;c., by the waysides in this locality. It was supposed in Massachusetts to have a connection with the barberry : but in Australia, where the disease was most, rife, the barberry did not exist. Dr. Koch in one of his axioms laid it down " that by inoculation with a fresh subject, the same disease from which they were derived might be produced.' Anthrax was the first contagious disease in which the presence of the microbe was discovered. Tapeworm was proved to have had its origin in the pig before it entered the I human body. Possibly there might be a hint in that to the ladies to cook the bacon well, so as to prevent tape- worm. The lecturer also touched on mould, hop dis- ease, smut in corn, vine disease, and damping off of seedlings. A bed of mustard and cress suddenly began to show brown patches of decay. On pulling up one of these little plants it appeared that the stems were unable to bear the upper portion ; but here again the fungi (which dissolved outside, and did not enter by mechanical force) had been at work, and had been known to destroy a bed of cress in a quarter of an hour If sown at the back end of the year the disease refused to germinate until spring. A vote of thanks was accorded to MI. Gillanders for his lecture.</p>
        <p>TUE REV. E. S. RAVENSCROFT, OF RUNCORN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>GOLBORNR</head>
        <p>GOLBORNR</p>
        <p>POLICE PROCEERINGS.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY REVELATIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Newton Town Hall on Saturday, before Messrs. T. J. Gillespie (in the chairs, W. F. Gooch, and T. D. Grinmke-Drayton, John Parr, landlord of the Railway Hotel, Golborne, was indicted for two offences under the licensing laws—first for "permitting drunkenness on his premises," and the second "selling intoxicating liquor to a drunken person "—both being alleged tohave been committed on Sunday evening, the Ist inst. . The case arose out of the recent " fatal careers!' at Gol- borne," respecting which a coroner's 'jury returned a verdict of" Alcoholic poisoning "as to the death of James Connolly, a young Irishman, who, after drinking at the Railway Hotel, was said to have fallen down insensible in the bar,was carried to his lodgings by his companions, and died after being attended for several hours by a medical man. Mr. Superintendent Brassington prose- cuted on behalf of the police, and defendant was repre- sented by Mr. T. Swift, barrister, (instructed by Mr. H. J. Wisidows, solicitor, Leigh and Manchester). When the case was called, to the surprise of all in court, the witnesses for the prosecution did not appear. Mr. Superintendent Brassington said four witnesses had been served personally with summonses to attend, but for some reason he should get at subsequently they were not present. He, therefore, applied for an adjournment of the case, and he would also ask their worships to grant warrants against the witnesses for their appearance. Mr. Swift sail the witnesses alluded to were not policemen, and therefore in that sense it was not a police' case, but one that had arisen through an immature ver- dict of a coroner's jury. He ventured to think the case was so extraordinary as to surpass anything their wor- ships had ever heard of. His client, who had been connected with the liquor trade for many yeaes, volun- teered evidence at the inquest, and was prepared te repeat it before their worships. There , was only one other witness examined at the inquest, and it was the most extraordinary inquiry and the most extraordinary verdict he every read in his life. Be (Mr. Swift) had about a dozen witnesses, who were prepared to refute the evidence given at the inquest, and under the circum- stances he must ask the bench to dismiss the present summonses against his client. The superintendent of police was sot, entitled to ask for warrants against the witnesses: he had not been put to any expense in the matter, and if after the present summonses had been dismissed the superintendent, after fully considering the matter, decided to proceed with the case, he could apply to the magistrates for fresh summonses without e,ntail- ing any expense. Mr. Brassington said he should press his application. Mr. Greenall (magistrates' clerk) said the superinten- dent was not entitled to warrant?, as there had been no money paid to the witnesses in respect of their expenses. The Bench retired to consider the question of ad- journment, and as they were re-entering, the wit- nesses walked into court. The case was then proceeded with. Thomas Loydon,collier. of Golborne,examined by Mr. Brassingten, said the man James Connolly (now dead) was nineteen and a half years of age, and came to re- side with him in July last. On the night of New Year's Day, after visiting the Prince of Wales Hotel, and the Sir Charles Napier Hotel, witness, the deceased, and two other lodgers went to the Railway Hotel, kept by the defendant. They arrived there between half past eight i and nine o'clock n the evening. Witness paid for the first round of drink, deceased having rum. The second round was paid for by the deceased, who.subsequently asked the landlord for a New Year's glass. The land- lord thought he was entitled to one, and it was served by the barmaid. Altogether the deceased had about nine glasses of rum and one of sherry ;in the Railway Hotel. The landlord served some of it and the remainder</p>
        <p>was served by the barmaid. About a quarter to tea o'clock the deceased fell down on the floor in the bar. Witness helped to pick him up and take him home. After getting him home, he went for Dr. Part, who remained with the deceased until twenty minutes to two o'clock in the morning, when he died.—By Mr. Swift: They were all four perfectly sober when they entered the Railway Rotel. The landlord and the barmaids served deceased with the quantity stated, but witness had only three drinks in the house. Witness had to lay deceased down in the road while he went for assistance to carry him home. The roads were slippery, but deceased did not fall off a man's back several times. John Burke said he lodged with the last witness, and accompanied the men to the Railway Hotel on New -Year's night. While there deceased had nine glasses of rum and one of sherry. Witness had four pints of beer. Deceased fell behind the counter, and witness helped to carry him home. The cause of his falling in the bar was that he was drunk.—Mr. Swift: Were you drunk? —Witness : No sir. I had six pints of ale. Pressed further, witness said deceased had nine glasses of rum •; the servant served him with about four glasses and the landlord served him with live, but he only saw two paid for.—Mr. Swift : Do you mean to say that the land- _ .</p>
        <p>lord and the barmaid have the deceased drink that was not paid for P—Witness : Yes.—The Chairman : Do you mean that the nine glasses were not paid for at all? Witness : Only two were paid for—two rounds.—Mr. Swift: Do you really expect us, in our senses, to be- lieve that P—Witness : Well, the Landlord is there. By Mr. Brassington: The reason the drinks were given was they had no more money. John ,Belly, labourer, swore that deceased had eight or nine glasses of ruin, and, he thought, one of sherry. The landlord supplied about three, and the batmeid supplied the rest.—Mr. Brassington : Were the others paid for P—Witness : They were not ; they were given Ito Connolly.—By Mr. Swift :He got one of the free drinks.—Mr. Swift : Did you hear the landlord say to deceased," You have not been in this house before, and 1, you have a nice cheek to ask for a New Year's glass ?" 1 —Witness : I did, sir.—Mr. Swift : Did the barmaid give deceased a New year's glass P—Witness t She laid it on the counter, and I expect he supped it. (Laugh- ter.)—Mr. Brassington : ,Do you know who has paid for all this drink P—Witness : I do not.—The Clerk : Has it been paid for yet P—Witness : No. Police-constable Simpson, stationed at Golborne, said he saw the witnesses with deceased in the atreet about 9:55 on the Sunday night in question. The man Connolly appeared to be helplessly drunk.—Mr. Swift: Will you swear that he was not at the time suffering from °con- cussion of the brain P—Witness : No ; I did not examine him. Mr. Brassington : That is the case for the police. . For the defence, Mr. Swift laid stress on the impro- bability of the landlord, after complaining about dc- ceased asking for a New Year's gift, giving him four or, five glasses, as stated by the witnesses. Such a state- ment was most extraordinary. The most extraordinary part of the affair, however, was that, although Dr. Part attended the man, he was not called at the inquest. Only one witness was examined, and how a jury of ex- perts, could arrive at such a conclusion as to the cause of death was a most mysterious thing, and surpassed his comprehension. Dr. Part would tell their worships that " alcohol poisoning" was not the cause of death at all ; that it was due to compression of the. brain, cased by injuries received from falling upon hi i head it] the street, when being taken home. The coroner's jury exonerated the publican from all blame, and he (Mr. Swift) thought it would be unwise to Convict his client ppon such feeble evidence as that of the three witnesses. John Parr, the defendant, said he had made careful inquiry, and found that deceased had not more than six glasses in house altogether. When he saw him alew minutes before leaving, the man appeared all right.— By Mr. Brassington : He admitted to the coroner that deceased had five or six glasses in his house. Lucy Bolding, barmaid, said the four men stood in the vaults from the time they entered to leaving. She served Connolly with only three glasses herself, and she , saw no signs of drunkenness in any of the men. She never saw Connolly fall in the bar.—By the Superin- tendent : A gentleman who was present only paid for one round to her knowledge. She was in and out of the bar, and they very busy. She knew she only served Connolly with three drinks, but she could not remember how many others had. , Mary Glover, another barmaid, deposed to serving deceased with two glasses of rum only. He showed no signs of drunkenness. Elias Clayton, collier, said he saw the men drinking together. He left the Railway Hotel about 9-25 p.m., when the men showed no signs of oeing the worse for drink. lie lived about two miwutes' walk from the Railway Betel, and as he was taking his coat off in the . .</p>
        <p>house tis wife called his attention to someone groan- ing in the street. Goitrg outside, he saw Connolly down against a wall, and the man Burke standing over him. Connolly's head was on the ground, and Burke tried to lift him up three or four times. Every time be tried to lift him he let him go, and the man's head " bumped " against the wall.—By Mr. Swift :—The roads were slippery P—Witness : Like glass.—Mr. Swift: Is it true that deceased had nine or ten glasses of drink in the Railway Hotel P—Witness : No, it is all non- sense.—The Magistrates' Clerk ; Is it true he had six ? —Witness : No. 1 say four or fire and I won't exceed that. . _ .</p>
        <p>—io"hn Painter said he was present at the Railway Hotel, and would swear deceased had only five glasses, Dr. Herbert Part, practising at Golborne, deposed to being called to see the deceased at Loydon's house about 10.20 on the night in question. He was in the kitchen lying an a bench near the bed. Be was blue in the face,in a clammy perspiration, and his limbs ,were helpless. Witness applied the stomach pump and got about a gill of fluid from him. He washed the stomach with warm and cold water, and tried a battery in order to revive him, but death ensued about twenty minutes to two in the morning.—Mr. Swift: Do you think it possible that death was caused by alcohol ? Considering the evidence as to the quaatity he had, cer- tainly not. I was told that he had a large quantity of drink, and when I reported the case I said there should be au inquiry. No such inquiry was held. Further</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>CRISIS IN THE GLASS BOTTLE TRADE.</head>
        <p>CRISIS IN THE GLASS BOTTLE TRADE.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL CONFERENCE AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>GREAT REDUCTIONS IN WAGES.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon, a national conference of delegates in connection with the Glass Bottle Makers' Trade Protection Association was held at St. Helens, to consider not only the condition of Lancashire bottle makers,but the present depressed state of the bottle trade generally, which has resulted in great reductions in wages, and many being thrown out of employment and upon the funds of the societies. Delegates were present from all those parts of Great Britain and Ireland in which glass bottles are manufac- tured. The conference was opened at the Royal Hotel. but on account of the large attendance of delegates and local members, an adjournment was made to the Prirai- tive Methodist Schools, in Kirkland-st. Here the pro- ceedings were conducted with closed doors, anoi con- tinued for seven hours. At the close the officials in- formed the Press that it had been decided to give no in- formation whatever of what had been done or what it was intended to do. We understand that no definite resolution was come to, but that the conference ad- journed for a week. With regard to St. Helens, which forms nearly the whole of the Lancashire distr,ict, notices of 3s. per week and Is. per gross off overwork were served some few weeks back, and the men, acting under the duly considered decision of the executive com- mittee of the Lancashire district, worked on that reduc- tion last week. They, however, considered that though a reduction might be necessary, the one proposed was far too large. In some cases it is said to amount to about 15s. per week, but to the majority of the men it averages 7e. per week. In the St. Helens district there are 150 apprentices and 420 journeymen, making a total of 570 members. Every workman is a member of the association. At, the end of June there were thirty-one members out of work,and they received donation money of 10s. per week, but during the trade depression that number had increased to about 150. We understand that the object of the conference was not to bring about a strike, but to decide upon a course of action by which masters and men could consider the best means of meet- ing the special difficulties which have arisen from the depressed state of trade during the past year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>KEIR HARDIE ON LIBERALS AND THE LABOUR PARTY.</head>
        <p>KEIR HARDIE ON LIBERALS AND THE LABOUR PARTY.</p>
        <p>On Monday night, Mr. Koir Hardie, M.P., lectured to a largo audience at Colne. Theton. member said it was surprising to find Labour organisations all over the the country giving prominence to such questions as an Improved system of government, extension of the fran- chise to:every adult, payment of members of Parliament by the State, reform of the registration laws, abolition of the House of Lords, and disestablishment of the Church, but whose programme eoutained scarcely a single item of what they who belonged to the Labour party considered labour questions. The political reforms he had indicated, good though they might be them- selves, were only means to an end. Whilst he was in favour of all those political reforms, he was much more in favour of abolishing landlord and capitalist. He found himself in conflict with those true and trusted friends of labour who thought these objects would be best accomplished by working in and through the Liberal party. It was because he believed the Labour party was destined to be a separate party, and in the very near future the dominant party in the , State that he went hopefully and joyfully forward to consecrate his life to the service of the independent Labour devement. Referring to pending Parliamen- tary elections, he said that when the Halifax election was over the independent Labour party in the House of Commons would be one stronger. In Burnley the Liberals had distinetly ignored the Labour party in selecting a candidate. He trusted the Labour party would return the compliment by ignoring the Liberal selection, and selecting a candidate of their own. In Huddersfield, net enly bad the Liberals ignored the Labour party, but had literally courted destruction by flying in the face of the Labour party and selecting a candidate who was chiefly known throughout Yorkshire by his hostility to the Labour movement—a man who had said, so he was told, that he would rather lose the election than vote for an Eight Hours' Bill for minors, a man who refused to recognise Trades Union rules In his printing office, where, he (Mr. Keir Hardie) was told, he printed a newspaper. That was the man whom the Liberals of Huddersfield had selected to represent labour in the House of Commons, and those were the men who came and said they were the friends of labour. He trusted that at Huddersfield the Labour party would select an independent labour candidate ; and if the elec- tion resulted in loss both to the Liberal and the Labour parties the responsibility would be on the shoulders of those men who were false to the cause of the democracy in selecting a candidate such as he bad dw cribed. - ' '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>LIBERATOR FAILURE. THE MISSING BALFOUR. A sumnuous HOME IN THE COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>THE LIBERATOR FAILURE. THE MISSING BALFOUR. A sumnuous HOME IN THE COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>- - • It is stated that Mr. J. S. Balfour's movements during the few days previous to his departure from England have been ascertained by thoso interested in his where- abouts. Mr. Balfour was at Burcot his seat in Oxford- shire, when the fact was announced that he had applied for the Chiltern Hundreds. This intimation was pub- lished on December 11, and on thesame evening Messrs. Hobbs and Wright were arrested. The news did not become known until about eleven o'clock on the follow- ing morning, and it was immediately telegraphed by a friend of Mr. Balfbur to meet him at Burcot. The important informotion came upon him with startling suddenness, and he lost no time in determining to leave Burcot for London. He journeyed to town on the Tuesday afternoon, made the necessary arrangements for a prolonged stay out of ,the country, and after executing a power of attorney on the Thursday, left London the same day: The Tress Association has ascer- tained that Mr. Balfour made his application for the Chiltern Hundreds at least a month before the announce- ment to this effect was issued to the press, and about two months after the failure of the Liberator, and the allied group of companies. The application was granted in the ordinary course, but it has not been gazetted,and the question has been raised whether his appointment to an office of profit under the Crown has thus taken effect The Press Association, however, has reason to believe that the Chancellor of the Exchequer exercised his undoubted right in returning an affirmative answer to Mr. Balfour's request, and that from that moment the seat for Burnley became vacant, and a new writ will be moved for on the meeting of Parliament.</p>
        <p>A Burnley correspondent states that up to the crash amongst the Balfour group of companies he was re - forded somewhat :as a model representative in many respects, although- his visits to the town were almost entirely confined to two or three times a year, when he either addressed his constituents or presided at some religious or semi-religious function. He was most ap- proachable, and almost invariably lent a willing ear to the appeals made to him on behalf of the numerous local institutions, to which he was a most generous con- tributor—notably the cricket and football clubs, the hospital, and the Mechanics' Institution. He teas con- sequently very much in request, and appeared to take i great interest n helping, both by his presence and his purse, all the agencies for good in the town. Two or three years ago, at a meeting of the Baptist Congrega- tional Union, at Burnley, he made an eloquent speech of the necessities of universities for the training of ministers of the Gospel. Some time before the last election at Burnley he caused some thousand copies of a London paper containing a cartoon of himself with a complimentary notice to be circulated throughout the town. It was almost the only portrait of him which the electors possessed, and is now to be found in very few houses. On two or three occasions the Liberal Executive and others have been invited to Burcot, and have returned to Burnley with glowing accounts of the member's boundless hospitality. There are very few people in Burnley who made any investments in the Liberator, but there is a small and poor community of Baptists a few miles from the town who are reported to have lost about £l,OOO in that society. Some interesting details have (says the Exchange Telegraph Company) transpired with reference to the debtor's property at Burcot, where he has an estate 35 acres in extent, with two large houses and outbuildings. The houses are elaborately furnished, and there has been found a good deal of silver plate, bearing the monogram of the debtor. There is also a large quantity of cham- pagne and other wines. The plate has been locked up in a safe placed in careful custody, and the key handed to the Official Receiver. Miss Bishop, Mr. Spencer Bal- four's housekeeper, lives a short distance from the estate. Her house is also sumptuously furnished.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA is absolutely pure.—The Public Ana- lysts have submitted CADBURY'S COCOA GO close tests, and the result has always proved its great value as a sustain- ing and nourishing Leverage.--44,5.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>RECEPTION ON HIS RETURN TO hIS MISSION</head>
        <p>RECEPTION ON HIS RETURN TO hIS MISSION</p>
        <p>FIELD.</p>
        <p>The following extracts from the "Northern Post," Harriarnith, Natal, will be read with pleasure by many Rancornians :—On Tuesday last the Rev. S. H. Ravenscroft, minister of the Wesleyan Church here, returned from his recent trip to England. During his stay in England, Mr. Re venscroft travel- led about a good deal, and attended the great Wes- leyan Conference which was held in Bradford in the early part of August. He also invited many of the relatives and friends ot members of his congre- gation. Mr. Ravenscroft left for England on the 16th of last June, and was due back again on the 16th of next month, so it it will be seen he has returned before his time. This we believe was due in a great measure to the cold weather. Mr. Ravenscroft em- barked in the Roslin Castle, which vessel broke down after getting out to sea, when she had to return again to Southampton. Mr. Ravenscroft speaks highly of the accommodation provided for the passengers until they coulel re-embark on board of the Ilawartieu Castle. This steamer left at 3 p.m. on Thursday, the 27th of October. Mr. Ravenscroft not having been well for a few days, and not having fully recovered, he kept his cabin for a stort time, when he was much better. On Monday, October 31st, the vessel anchored off Maderia at 11 a.m.,1 where he went on shore and visited several places of interest. Mr. Ravenscroft complains of the public beggars of Funchal, who, he says, refused to be shaken' off—he being of the cloth, perhaps made them more importunate than they are with others. He, however, complains very strongly against the Portuguese Govern- ment ..for allowing lepers to go about and beg like other unfortunates. Picture," he says, your feel- ings, if you can, if you should find yourself face to face with hideous, loathsome lepers." The Hawarden Castle left Madeira again at 4.20 p.m., and the incidents of the rest of the voyage were much as usual. ,Very severe weather, however' was experienced ,wher! they were within two days of the Cape. On Saturday night, the 12th inst., a fiercer gale from the south-east prevailed. The ship pitched heavily, the propeller com- ing out of the water at almost every alterdatk revolu- tion, but as the Sunday wore on it kubsided again, and Capetown was reached at 3.30 p.m. on Monday, the 14th. During the voyage the ministers on board— there being no less than five—held a number of services, Mr. Ravenscroft conducting one or two of them. 'The voyage up the coast was a pleasant one, and Durban was reached on Monday afternoon at 3.30 p.m., and Mr. Ravenscroft arrived home, as stated above, on Tuesday morning. We tender Mr. Ravenscroft a hearty welcome back to ,Harrismith, and are pleased to find that he has improved in health, and trust that his reverend labotlrs will be abundantly blessed.</p>
        <p>In a later publication t P ,same journal contains an interesting account of the public welcome accorded to the rev, gentleman on his return, and in a leaderette says, "The public welcome given to the Rev. S. H. Ravenscroft on Wednesday night was, as the chairman described it, a very hearty one ; and was in fact one of the most pleasant gatherings it has been our pleasure to attend for a long time. In extending the welcome to Mr. Ravenscroft, Mr. McKechnic said it was only a formal gathering ; but, pared:, xical as it may sound, there was no formality about it, and it would better be described as a free-and-easy one. Everyone seemed to vie with each other as to who could be the most sociable; and certainly such gatherings make people better acquainted with each other, and tend very strongly to create that sociability which should exist in every community. Mr. Ravenscroft's account of his trip to the home land was a very interesting one, especi- ally that part of it where he spoke of visiting the rela- tives and friends of members of his congregation. He has a keen sense of the humorous, and knows well how to introduce it into his remarks ; and this point added a sweet spiciness to his address on Wednesday night. Mr. Ravenscroft spoke for over half-an-hour, and so interest- ing was he that if he had gone on for more than double that length of time he would have been gladly listened to. But it is always better to be short and sweet than to be too long on such occasions; and we have no doubt that, if any one wishes to know more about his trip to the old country, he will be very pleased to answer any questions they nsay put t 6 him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>• THE LATE HON. W. H. CROSS.</head>
        <p>• THE LATE HON. W. H. CROSS.</p>
        <p>The following letters have been received from Lord Cross and the Boy. Mrs. Cross, in reply to the vote Of condolence which was passed by the mem- bers of the Newton Polling district Conservative Club, on the death of the Hon. W. H. Cross. Old Breadgate, Broughton-in-Furness, January 12, 1893. Mr. Haselden,—l am very grateful to you, and the members of the Newton Polling district Conservative Club for your most kind sympathy with me and my family in our terrible sorrow, and for the high tribute of praise to my dear husband, which we value highly.— Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>MARY CROSS. Eccles Iligg, Broughton-in-Furness, January 10, 1893. My Dear Sir,-1 gladly send my thanks to the mem- bers of the Newton Polling district Conservative Club, for remembering old friendship, and for the resolution of condolence, which is most grateful to us.—Always yours faithfully, CROSS. Mr. Thos. Llaselden.</p>
