      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>DARKWOOD.</head>
        <p>DARKWOOD.</p>
        <p>A TALE OF LONEMOOR.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. HARRIETT LEWIS, Author of "The Sundered Hearts," "The Bailiff's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>"The Double Life," &amp;e.</head>
        <p>"The Double Life," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER V. THE SEARCH ENDED.</p>
        <p>The old housekeeper spread an instant alarm throughout the mansion. Servants with lanterns were dispatched to search the moor, through the wild, white drifts and raging storms, for the wandering girl. The butler took an active part in the eager quest. Mrs. Quillet, wild with terror and excitement, rushed out upen the lawn, nearly Losing herself in the snow, and then hurried in again, gasping for breath. As she paused in the lower hall for a moment, after closing the door to arrange her loosened grey hair and disordered garments, hearing the faint wail of the deserted baby upstairs, the door of the sitting room opened, and the old squire looked out, demanding angrily the cause of so much com- motion. 46 Oh, sir!" cried Mrs. Quillet, in a breathless I sort of way. "She's gone! She's gone." The squire's face grew suddenly pale, then furiously red. "Who's gone ?" he demanded harshly. "She—Miss Glare! Gone into this awful storm to her death. Heaven have pity on her. Oh, my poor lamb!' The squire's face grew dark as a thundercloud. The lightning of anger leaped from his stormy eyes. "Hush !" he commanded. "If you speak that name again you will leave my house. So the vagrant woman is gone? It is as well. Gone to , her death? Heaven grant it I" He retreated within the sitting room abruptly, and slammed the door. I Mrs. Quillet sighed moaningly and toiled up the stairs to the wailing child. As she sat down before the fire, in a low nursing chair with the '-aby in her arms, she muttered— "Once he worshipped Miss Clare. Now be would let her die in this pitiless storti and not lift one finger to her rescue. He is glad she is out in 1 all this wild snow to-night. And she will die, my I poor darling—my innocent nursling! For she is innocent. I know that she is pure and innocent in heart and soul, in spite of her father's curses, in spite of his disowning her, in spite of the lack of wedding ring, in spite of this baby, in spite of everything ; I would believe her even if angels should testify against her. Oh ! what is this mys- tery—this awful mystery? They will find her and bring her in, and then I shall persuade her to tell me _ .</p>
        <p>She rang the bell and ordered the cook to pre- pare coffee and other hot drinks. She paced the floor, the baby in her arms, and peered from the windows, and waited in a horrible suspense that was sickening to bear. "She will ne chilled to death," moaned the old woman. "She will stagger and fall and be covered by those awful drifts ! Or she will lie down and die ! Oh, Heaven, take care of her !" The hours toiled wearily on. The servants came and went,</p>
        <p>Mounted grooms joined in the wild search for the "vagrant woman." Not one of those who sought foi her, with the exception of the old butler, knew that the half crazed young wanderer was in reality the Miss Clare Markham who was supposed to be dead—not one of them even suspected that the girl who was now lost in the night, the dark- ness, aIL-.1 the whirling snow-drifts was in truth the delicately-nurtered heiress of Lonemoor, who had once been the belle of the county, the pride of her father's heart, and who was now pop:lady believed to be sleeping her "last long sleep" under foreign</p>
        <p>But the, search was all in vain. The wind increased in ...fury, raging like a loosened demon, piling snow Itgarnet the fences, the terraces, and every obstruction ; the flakes tell carelessly; the air was filled with the wild 'White whirl. The roads and paths that led over the moors were indiscern- ible. One vast whiteness reigned everywhere uppn the earth and in the air. The men came in one by one, chilled and dis- heartened. The cook administered to tkem the hot drinks she had prepared. By morning the men w. re aIL in, and not one of them had found the wandering girl. The old squire did not show his face that night, but his light burned steadily in his sitting-room, and the housekeeper, stealing now and then down the stairs, heard his ceaseless tread to and fzu, and knew that his night too was sleepless. When morning came—a grey, snowy morning white pall covered the moor, the park, the fields, and the gardens of Lonemoor. Fences and shrubs were blotted out. The marble vases on the terrace were bidden. A vast white desolation, reminding them of the Arctic scenes, was spread far and wide. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The men, well mounted, went forth again at daybreak to renew their search upon the trackless moor. Mrs. worn and trembling, with swollen eyes and pale complexion, carried- in her master's breakfast, as was her wont. He was st,mdinz at a window, looking out as she entered. Wn she had deposited the tray upon a table, he turned an 4 the old woman started back at tilt sight of his face. . He had grown years older dnritig the past night. He was greyer, more haggard, and far more stern. There was a fierceness in his sunken, burn- ing eyes that stilled the exclamation that rose to Mrs. (billet's lips. Shi had always been in awe of hir master, but a new fear entered now in her regard towards him. Well," he said, in a voice she scarcely recog- nised, yet it was lees harsh and dictatorial than usual, " is she found ?"</p>
        <p>If Not yet," answered the housekeeper, putting her apron to her eyes. "They have gone out to. look again !" • , The squire turned abruptly again to the window. " She nuis;. be dead by this," :aid the old woman, brokenly, "The awful exposu: e.—the storm— the deadly cold—would have killed one so tenderly reared. She is dead" •</p>
        <p>The old quire made a fierce gesture to her to depart, end she withdrew: All that, ay he'remained shnt up in his sitting room, pacing the floor, orwatching from the window for the hue-bringing of the daughter whom he had cursed.</p>
        <p>And all that day he men scrvan.s sear hod, and the snow fell ceaselessly; and a gloom hem; like a pall over tie mansion, of Lonetnoor. Mrs. Quillet nursed the little 611ild tenderly. and 'Watched at the window,,, and started nervously at every sound, and the lolk of terror and despair deepened on her old face 'aonr I,y hour alit! Aiihuto by tote. " When night came on again, with still that thick white veil dropping steadily down over the face of. nature, the last vestige of hope lied from the heart of the old housekeeper, anu an awful dread and anguish took its place. , She expected the return pf the servants with a ghastly burden, and made ready for its reception. The servants came `stitiggling in, chilled ,and muttering at the trouble they had taken for a "wandering beggar." They had searched the trackless waste far and wide, explored drifts, had ' visited Danford-bridge railway station and Peni- stone, and every hamlet en the moors within a radius of twelve miles, going singly and in pairs, and they had found no trace of the lost girl. Those heavy snows seemed to have blotted her out of existence. "She was so lately ill," said the old butler, coming up to the lonely,chamber in which his wife kept her vigils over the month-old baby to make his report, so weakly still ,that she•could never have borne one hour of the ciill, the wind and the storm. She's dead, Maria, under some snowdrift. Poor, delicate young creature ! Who that saw her a twelvemonth or more ago in her youth, and beauty, and pride would have dreamed that she Would ever hay i a fate like this ?" Mrs. Quillet'inears fell on the innocent baby face that lay against her breast.</p>
        <p>- "My poor lamb! My bonnie nursling 1" she said moaningly. "Oh, John, John, it is awful !"</p>
        <p>"Yes. She wandered on. I can see just how It all ended," said the butler. "She staggered on through the drifts for an hour or more, and then she stumbled into some hollow and fell. She did not rise again, although she may have made two or three efforts. Then the snow fell upon her and covered her, and her grave is hidden from our search."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Quillet sobbed despairingly. The old butler wiped his eyes.</p>
        <p>"It is as well so," he said. "This world held no refuge for her after all that had happened. Her own father had disowned and cursed her. She was home- less, friendless, accursed. Let us hope, Maria, that there is room for her in heaven."</p>
        <p>"And that heaven will be merciful to her to whom her own father was merciless," said the housekeeper. .!.!_When the snows disannear wo sh3ll find her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>ilia the butler, "unless he lost her way and tell into the moor ,am. In that case she may never be Found. How does the master take it ?"</p>
        <p>"I don't know. He looks fiercer and more et•rible thin ever, but he asks no questions. He is not eaten a morsel to-day ; and that reminds me that I must take in his dinner to him."</p>
        <p>_ Mrs. Quillet laid the now sleeping infant in its oradle—the cradle that had rocked the lost heiress :cventeen or eighteen years hence—and hurried below. She carried in the squire's dinner on a tray, as had been her custom of late. Her master sat in an armchair, still by the window. The fire had gone ant, and the room was very cold. A single candle burned dimly in a remote corner. - -</p>
        <p>Mrs. Quillet sat down the tray, and uttered pro- fuse apologies for the cheerless condition of the room. Trembling with apprehension, she rushed to. the grate to stir up any possibly lingering embers; but the squire's voice, hard and metallic, bade her let the fire alone. "I want no tire," he said. "Where is Quillet ?"' "Upstairs, sir. He is tired, sir, and chilled through; but I'll call him, sir." "Let him alone. Are the servants all in?" " All in, sir." The squire asked no more questions. The old woman's face told him that the search had been unsuccessful. His face grew a shade graver, if that were possible, and he motioned her to take away the tray as if the sight of it sickened him. Mrs. Quillet withdrew with her burden, and the old squire was left again to his darkness, cold, and loneliness. The old housekeeper noticed thathe did not retire to his room that night. The next day, and the next, the servants con- tinued their explorations of the moor. The sno* gradually ceased to fall; but the air was crisp and cold, and the snow that lay on the ground did not melt. The secret of the lost girl's fate remained a ' secret which no exertion could solve. It was in December that the ill-fated heiress had gone forth in storm and terror. In January a three days' snow was added to the one we have described, and while that still lay unmelted another occurred in February. March was stormy and blustering. It was not until early in April that the tardy spring made bare OW wide, far-stretching moors, and that the searchers' finally resumed their work under the orders and directions of Quillet, for it must be understood that the squire had never spoken one word save those we have narrated of the lost girl and her fate. It hod beea a strange hard winter to Squire Markham. He had abandoned his old sitting-room,. and taken up his quarters in the gloomy library, whose windows commanded a wide view of the moor. He had no fire in the library during the winter, although the season was unusually severe. Some of the servants whispered among themselves that the master was "growing a miser ;" but the butler and housekeeper alone knew of the worm that gnawed secretly at the old man's heart. It was the third day of April when the little party of mounted men, with the old butler at their head, rode out of the stable yard at lonem ror, separated at the gates, and hurried upon their quest. The squire saw them depart; but his impassive countenance might have been carved of wood or stone for any sign of emution or interest he showed. Yet he paced his floor for hours, and his meals were not touched.</p>
        <p>The servants returned singly, having been unsuc- cessful, at intervals throughout the day—all, except the butler and two others. These rode in about nightfad at a funeral pace, followed by an under- taker's waggon. The squire, staring from his window, saw that the waggon held s. box, whose ho' rible contents he could only too well surmise. Mrs. Quillet ran down to the stableyard, and her husband drew her aside, and said to her, in an agitated whisper— " She's found at last. We've brought her home, Maria." " Where did you find her ?" "Five miles this side of Penistone. She had fallen into a deep hollow, where the snow had covered her all winter. It was an awful sight. We left her there, and hurried to Penistone for the coroner and undertaker. They've had an inquest, and I thought it best to bring her here."</p>
        <p>"Are you sure it is our young lady ? Are you, very sure, Johnr "I could swear to it. Her clothes were al, soaked to rottenness. She was a horrible sight to look -upon after all these mouths; but it is our young mistress, Maria. I'll swear to it. Her long black hair was s reaming all about her in ropes. 1 am sick when I think of it. You must not see her, or the sight will haunt you to your death. What are we to do Will the master let her be buried 52 she ought? He cursed her to her death. Will her give her bpdy decent burial ?" "You said there was an inquest. Did any on°. suspect who she was ?" " Ne one. They believed her a wandering, crazed vagrant, who was sheltered at Lonemoor, gave birth to a child here, and in her madness wandered forth again to die." - • lllll see 1-.ll' Master," said Mrs. Quillet. " Let' the waggon stand until I hear what he has to Bah." 'She hurried into the house and to the libfadi The squire turned his cold, hard gaze upon her, Ana with a faltering tongue and streaming eyes she told her story. "What shall we do with her ?" she asked. "Shall we take her to the great hall or to the dead room ?" "You said, I believe, that her real identity re- mained unsuspected by the coroner, the undertakers and their gaping crew ?" "Yes, master ; John took good care of that. " Wo y should Quillet bring to Lonemoor the bodY of a vagrant ?" demanded the squire harshly. "The woman was nothing to me. Take her body hence' It cannot rest under my roof to night." " Oh, master—' " Go !" Mrs. Quillet slowly withdrew, returning to beg husband. "His heart is utterly hardened, John,'' eke whispered. "He will no give her decent buf,ial. He says she must be taken away immediately. The butler's face grew stern. 'I I "She shall not lie in a pauper's grave will ° have a single sovereign !" he exclaimed. " se° her buried decent,y, Maria. And may heavert for- give the squire 1" The butler mounted beside the undertaker, and the waggon set out upon its return joarney 0 Penis tone. Quillet returned the next day about noon, io tbe market waggon which his wife had sent to fetish him. "She's buried, Maria," he said, when the twos were in an upper room together. "I had a clergY,-, man to say a prayer over her. Her life is endeo, poor, misguided, unhappy young creature. l_Tri the life of her child is scarcely begun. heaven the child is weak and sickly. She soon follow her mother, and the tragedy Will b° ended."</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
