      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. WILLIAM ARMITAGE, J.P.</p>
        <p>THE FUNERAL</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mr. Wm. Armitage, J. P. twit place on Saturday morning at the Parish %Ural, Bowdon. By the family of the deceased a eriali had been expressed to have the interment onnducted with all the privacy, possible, and their invitations were confined to near relatives, a few friends, and others closely identified with the busi- helm life of Mr. Armitage. Intimations of the in- tention of public bodies to be represented, however, catue from so many sources that the original wishes Were necessarily set aside, and the funeral was of an essentially public character. It had been intended ro hold a service at Townfleld House, and to convey the bodY direct to the grave at Bowdon, but in deference to Widespread wish a departure was made, and it was determined to bold the service at the Bowdon Downs Congregational Church, so as to permit of as large a number as possible taking part. In Altrincham public regard was betokened by the closed shutters and the drawn blinds. All the shops in Church-st. and Market- Place and many of those in George-st..were closed, and in Dunham-road the veiled windows were silent evid- ences of respect. A brief service was held at Townfield House before the body was removed. At half-past 11, ti3e remains were tenderly borne from the room in Which they lay by the six sons of Mr. Armitage, and bY them affectionately placed in the waiting hearse. The procession was headed by several members of the Cheshire Constabulary under the command of Super- intendent Walker and Sergeant Christian. The came the followin. members of the Altrincham Local Board : —"Messrs. G. Bowen (chairman). J. Steen, J. Gott. R. Cratley, 3. Arnold, and J. Stokoe (clerk.) Mr. T. B. Parkes also accompanied the members,of the Board. The oarri age of Mr. Coward came next, containing the Rev. r. Mackennal, the Rev. Thomas Willis (of the Gros- eenor-street Congregational Church),the Rev.—Thomas, rand Dr. Cox (the deceased's medical attendant). The 'arse was drawn by four fine black Belgian horses. Its glass sides permitted a view of the coffin. The desire of the family, that no flowers should be sent, had been TerY generally respected, and it accounted for the very ow wreaths that were to be seen: The coffin, which had been made by Mr. H. Ormson, Bowdon, was of Polished oak, the sides of which were handsomely pan- !Wiled. It was finished with very massive brass mount- and on an inlaid plate was the inscription William Armitage, born June 29th. 1815; died Jan- uary 11th, 1893." A handsome floral device in violets a'ld white flowers from the family of the deceased almost entirely covered the lid. The other wreaths Which found a place in the hearse were from the em- Ployes of Messrs. Armitage and Rigby, Warrington ; the Tembers of the Altrincham Reform Club ; Mr. and Mrs. di P. Milne, Lyndhurst, Birkdale ; and John and Grace e'lles, Bryn Celyn, Llanfairfechan. On each side of the nrse walked the six sons of the deceased and some of ;no near relatives, who acted as carriers and pall nearers. It was impossible not to be impressed by this aitnple and touching act of reverent affection. On the I:ght of the hearse were Mr. Ziba Armitage, Mr. William r,rnlirege, Mr. S. Rigby Armitage (sons), Mr. Jesse iworth (son-in-law), Mr. W. Rigby, Mr. Marshall Y (nephews), and Mr. A. H. Hawerth. On the left i".!re Professor Armitage, Mr. G. Faulkner Armitage, r. J. Fred Armitage (sons), Mr. Z. A. Ward, Mr. W. r. Carver (son-in-law), Mr. J. Rigby (nephew), and f_r Alfred Simpson. A long line of private carriages 6°eceeded. The following is the order observed, with '412. occupants of the carriages : Ha rownfield brougham.—Mrs. Armitage and Mrs. Jesse worth. Tow nfield carriage.—Mrs.Bulley, Mrs.oswald Carver, Loo' Ziba Armitage, Miss E. Armitage, 4nd Miss Phyllis</p>
        <p>urMs.r; Jesse Havcorth's carriage.—Mrs. E. Armitage, Arrru . W• Armitage, Mrs. G. F. Armithge, and Mrs. S. R. tage Mc. Abraham Haworth's carriage.—Mrs. J. Frederick Arna end. age, Mrs. Rigby, Mrs. Brinsley, and Mrs. Towns- -O.M,iSs Thompson's carriage, Heald Bank.—Mrs. W. :sigoY, Mrs. M. Rigby, Mrs. Arthur Haworth, Mrs. Her- -155 Rnett and Machel Armitage. Mr. Edward Galloway's carriage.—Miss C. Armitage and Master Godfrey Armitage. Mr. C. Behrens' carriage.—Miss Dorothy Armitage, I Miss Winifred Armitage, Master J. B. Armitage, and . Master Noel Armitage. Mr. Alfred Simpson's carriage.—Mr. Melland, Mr. J. Mills, Mrs. T. Carver, and Mr. W. E. Melland. Mr. J. A. Morris's carriage.—Mr. George Beaumont, Mr. Joseph Thompson, and Mr. T. R. Bulley. Mr. Arthur Haworth's carriage.—Mr. L. Simpson, the Rev. John Yonge, and Professor Wilkins. Mr. W. J. Crossley's carriage.—Mr. John Wilkinson, Ir. J. H. Seddon, and Mr. R. E. Phillips, deacons of the G 4 rosvenor-st. Congregational Church. Mr. Hutchings' carriage.—Mr. G. H. Adshead, Mr. E. nal:ward, Mr. D. Baillie, and Mr. C. H. Bibbed, mana- gers of departments. Mr. A. W. Mi;!s' carriage.—Messrs. S. Butler and W. t.larvey, London ; Mr. L. G. Meller, London; and Mr. T. nillshaw, managers of departments. Mr. W. E. Melland's carriage, Moorfield, Withington ; -4. Gee, Mr. Staniforth, and Mr. Noles. Rylands's carriage, Longford Hall, Stretford; 'lr. W. Wilkinson and Mr. Frank A. Jones, deacons of t'osvenor-street Church. Mr. R. P. Gill's carriage.—Mr. R. P. Gill, J.P. Oldham Hall carriage.—Mr. Bulkeley Allen, J.P. .„Mr. Johnson Ferguson's carriage.—Mr. J. E. John- ,-"n-Ferguson M.P. for Loughborough Division of 'Leicester. Dr. Sidebotham's carriage.—Dr. Sidebotham, C.C., '4ld Mr, T. W. Killick, J.P., C.C. b, Three carriages containing the servants of Townfield iiinse followed, and the private carriages of Mr. Jamas A id, Mr. H. T. Gaddum, J.P., Mr. W. H. Johnson, Mr. ."larn J.P., and Mr. R. H. Jovnson, J.P., brought 1115,t, he rear. ▪ he following deputations were also present : ~,London Missionary Society.—Dr. Hodgson and Mr. uavoid Lee. Lancashire and Cheshire Chapel Building Society.— ;-',l3°. William Hewgill, M.A., Farnworth ; Rev. Dr. `nodrich, Mr. J. C. Needham, and Mr. Reuben Spen- ggS, J.p. rtheshire Congregational Union.—Mr. Joseph Wood, 4lsirman, and Mr. J. S. Wood. kLancashire Congregational Union.—Mr. W. Crosfield, ;Rev. T. Willis and the Rev. John Yonge, War- -oRton•, Mr. E. B. Dawson, and Mr. W. Murray Fraser. vvcliffe Congregational Church, Warrington.—Mr. J. garland. „linked Kingdom Alliance.—Messrs. James Boyd and rt Whitworth. liFiorth Cheshire Rural Mission.—Messrs. William Henry Barratt, Edgar C. Mills, E. W. Wollaston, ,H. Boydell, Johnson, and A. C. O'kell. ,kaneashire and Yorkshire Bank.—Mr. John Thomp- , "'"o, director. and Mr. J. H. S. Ceompton, manager. , e.C'o,...ekhedge Mills. Warrington.—Messrs. David Bailie, „..tt• Hibbert, P. Winskill, and John Lingard. tienshaw's Blind Asylum—Mr. John Grantham, Mr. J°llll S. Mayson, Mr. John Galloway, Mellor, and Mr. tires W. Rattray, secretary, representing the Asylum ard. Mr. 11. Aldridge, master ; Mrs. Bryning, Imatron's assistant; Mr. David Deakin, and four blind tits, representing the institution, Old Trafford. 43ewton Night School.—Mr. Samuel Murgatroyd. at?irosvenor-street Congregational Church.—Mr. John afield, Mr. Robert Ruth, Mr. Henry Matthews, J.P., r. John Hermon. .t.ancashireCollege.—Professor Scott, D.D. traughton Park Church.—Mr. S. Pearson. ppdlanchester, Salford and District Congregational Lay ,i,eachers' Society.—Mr. G. Bellhouse, honorary genre- -'rY, and Mr. Samuel Heath, financial secretary. It k'Y be added that of this society the deceased was I,:Terly organist and treasurer. bflanchester and Salford Congregational Ministers' and Wacons' Associatlon.—Rev. J. W. Kiddie, Mr. J. C. , o,,rbury, and Mr. G. N. Ford. del'ancashire County Union and Ministers' Provi- alt society—Mr. Dawson, Lancaster, treasurer. This m„ee was formerly held by the decased. lx,"oYal Insurance Company—Mr. Richard Wright, local , 7,l:lager. "I ueen's Insurance Company—Mr. Matthew Irving. ' AJkrrnitage and Rigby, Portland-street—Messrs. G. H. 41eaci, T. Willshaw, W. Gee, S. Butler, H. Lomas, W. al dden, Mather, E. Midwood, heads of departments; rts°, Messrs. Roberts, Brough, Wardleworth, Gaskell, Caunee, Hollingswerth, Finley, Fish, Smith, Earnshaw, &amp;c. ~,f,rtnitage and Rigby. Rodney-st., Mill.—Mr. E. Hay, d (manager), and Mr. T. Caunce. Should also be stated that the deaconesses of the • ~don Downs Congregational Church, were present. C. Davies represented the Rev. R. M. Davies, Old- 4ervua! Who was unable to take his expected part in the • the body was conveyed into the church the organist 1,11.31tt1e played "I know that my Redeemer livethy sunn,nYam !` Come let us join our friends above" was opeibi! after which the Rev. Dr. Mackennal read the ir sentences of the burial service, and at its close aekennall delivered an address.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>----- THURSDA.Y. Before Judge Wynne Ffoulkes. WARRANTS TORN TO PIECES AT EARLESTOWN.—Mr. Grice, head bailiff of the court, applied on behalf of the High Bailiff for the lame of four fresh commitment war- rants in place of four which had been destroyed at Earlestown, and handed up the remains of the docu- ments to the Judge.—Bailiff Gardener said he was at Earlestown on the 31st ult., and was standing under a gas lamp with the four warrants in his hand, five or six men came past and they got hold of them and tore them up. He stuck to the top of them.—His Honour asked how it happened that the top part of some was torn and the under part of others. The officer must have had the warrants topsy-turvy, one warrant was torn one way and one another.—The bailiff: They were all torn at once.—The Judge said he must refuse to grant the request, first because he had not the power to do so and secondly because he thought there had been some carelessness on the part of the officer. JOSEPH DAINTETH v. JOSEPH SYLVESTER.—The plaintiff of 156, Manchester-road, for whom Mr. Davies appeared, sued the defendant, a gardener,also of Manchester-road, who was represented by Mr. Browne, for £4 Os. 6d. The plaintiff and his witnesses, William Hind, plasterer, 12, Manchester-road, and Thomas Williams, broker, gave evidence, and the case for the plaintiff was that he claimed the amount for three days work and for pur- chases made, less £1 paid on account, but the defend- ant said the work was a contract, and repudiated the claim, stating that only £2 had been due and he paid £1 at this as the plaintiff said he was pressed for money but refused to pay the other until he finished his contract by doing the spouting and stagin,g, which work the plaintiff admitted he had not done. The judge gave a verdict for the plaintiff, the debt to be discharged by a first payment of 155., and Bs. a month thereafter. WOOLEY V. CARLILE.—THE CLAIM FOR ALLEGED OTERDRIVING.—In this case which had been twice previously adjourned; in which the plaintiff, Thomas Wooley, 13, Mill-lane, Warrington, sued Edward Carlile, butcher, Market-street, Warrington, for recovery of £22 103. for alleged over- driving of his pony, Mr. Browne now addressed the court for the defence. He denied that there was any trespass by the defendant in entering the plaintiff's trap and there was nothing to show that the animal's death resulted from his conduct. There had been no post mortem examination, and the pony had not been seen by a veterinary surgeon when it was ill, and there was no evidence as to the real cause of death. He contended that if there was any negligence there was contributory negligence on the part of the plaintiff's son, who had driven the pony steaming into Sankey.—Mr. Davies replied on behalf of the plaintiff. lie contended that the defendant wrongfully deprived the plaintiff of his pony by frightening the lad Woolev out of the trap in order that they might get into it, remarking that the defendant and his companion Wicks, who took posses- sion of the trap admitted that the lad had said something about having to wait for his brother at Rainhill, who VMS behind with some beasts, as an objection to giving up the trap. As to the horse being seen by certain landlords at Rain- hill and other places not to be steaming, it was the over- driving that was done afterwards that was injurious to the pony. As to Woolley's son having contributed to the horse's death by overdriving it, he had explained to the witness Whittle that it was through its usage by the defendant that it was in that state.—The Judge con- sidered the evidence as to the cause of the pony's death, did not prove that it was caused by the defendant. As to the trespass he thought the plaintiff had made out his case ; and that the evidence of the boy as to the defend- ant wrongfully getting possession of the trap was true, but the evidence did not prove any damage by him, that was permanent or more than slight, to the pony. A certain amount of injury had he thought been proved. His Honour gave a verdict for the plaintiff for £5 with costs, to be paid at the rate of 10s. a month, but on the objection of Mr. Brown, declined to alio* the veterinary surgeon's expenses, as his evidence was as to the point which had not been proved by the plaintiff. THOMAS MORRIS T. SAMUEL JOHN FRANCIS.—CLAIM FOR • HUNDRED GEESE.—The plaintiff, a poulterer, of Heath Side, Warrington, sued the defendant, a Widnes, fish and poultry dealer; for £33 155., the value of 104 geese supplied by the plaintiff to the defendant. Mr. Browne appeared for the plaintiff, and Dr. Comins, instructed by Mr. J. W. Knowles, of Widnes, for the defence.—Mr. Browne said the defendant carried on a goose club, and bought the geese on December 21st to supply a certain number of members of the club for Christmas Day. The geese were purchased by the plain- tiff from one of the largest dealers in Manchester, and were all dressed ,weighed ,and examined one by one,by the plaintiff, and a lot of them were opened to satisfy the defendant's wife, who said they were most beautiful geese, and took them away. The day before Christmas Day a telegram was received from the defendant,stating that the geese had all been seized by the Widnes Corpora- tion, and condemned as unfit for food, and he repudiated the claim. Mr. Browne contended that in any case,the defendant could no more act as he had done, than he (Mr. Browne could purchase a cocoa nut at a shop after examining it, and take it back three days after as bad. There was no warranty of the goods. and the defendant , took them at his own risk, but he was prepared to prove that the geese were absolutely good..—The Judge: We don't want evidence as to whether they were goodor bad if there was no warranty. Mary Ann Morris, the plaintiff's wife gave evidence corroborating Mr. Browne's statement. She said the plaintiff's wife wanted at first to buy 200 geese, but witness could not supply that num- ber.—ln reply to Dr. Comins she said she had charged id. a lb. more than she had formerly charged the defend- ant for geese, telling his wife she would do so because they would bo specially good .—Susannah Sheldon, mar- ried daughter of the plaintiff and the plaintiff gave con- firmatory evidence. He stated that on receipt of the telegram he went to the defendant's house at Widnes and he found several women there dressing French geese, and the defendant brought a goose out of the yard and said this was one of the bad geese, but witness saw that it was not one of his, not being large enough, and told the defendant so.—The case was adjourned till next court nay, February 2nd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>EXTENSIVE GIFTS TO THE PLYMOUTH</head>
        <p>EXTENSIVE GIFTS TO THE PLYMOUTH</p>
        <p>BRETHREN. ACTION TO RECOVER £140,000. The trial was cemmenced in the Chancery Division on Thursday, of an action by which Mr. S. H. Morley, and Mr. Arnold Morley, M.P., as executors of their late brothers, Mr. Henry Morley, seek to recover from three brothers named Longhman and their brother-in- law, Mr. Sleeman, the sum of £140,000. The Longh- mans were formerly clergymen of the Church of Eng- land, but subsequently joined the sect known as the Plymouth Brethren. The deceased belonged also to the ' same sect, and resided for some years with the Longh- mans, who acted as his religious and business advisers. Altogether they received from him the sums amounting to £140,000, which they said were voluntary gifts, but the plaintiffs allege that the gifts were not good in law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>BRISTOL RIOTS.</head>
        <p>THE BRISTOL RIOTS.</p>
        <p>MR. B. TILLETT COMMITTED FOR TRIAL. The hearing of the charge against Ben Tillett of hav- ing incited dockers to riot was resumed at Bristol on Thursday. The Chief Constable said he had an inter- view with a deputation of the dockers on December 21st, when they refused to take another route except the projected one through the centre of the town. Other police witnesses alleged that on December 23rd violent language was used, and that the conduct of the proces- sionista was riotous. Superintendent Croker spoke of numerous assaults on non-Unionists by Unionists. Evidence for the prosecution was concluded, and Mr. Wansborough, on behalf of the police, applied for the defendant's committal, and after hearing the defendant's counsel, the Bench decided that there was sufficient evi- dence on which to commit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>"NO CRINOLINE LEAGUE."</head>
        <p>THE "NO CRINOLINE LEAGUE."</p>
        <p>THOUSANDS OF RESPONSES.</p>
        <p>- - Mrs. Stannard ("John Strange Winter") writes that she has enrolled 5,265 members of the No Crinoline League since Tuesday week, and adds :—Many corres- pondents naturally consider that Her Royal Highness the Princess of Wales should be asked to declare at once against the threatened fashion. lam asking for an in- dilation of her views and intentions, and I am confident that they will be entirely favourable to this crusade. Others imaeine that the danger is more imaginary than real. This -delusion will be assiduously fostered until the crinolines are . ready. No one really behind the scenes will venture to deny my assertion that certain firms are at this moment specially busy manufacturing crinoline materials and accompaniments. This league will, r hope, stop or modify this activity—if the protest is not already too late. We may be sure that,when once huge stacks have been prepared for the" ready-made "trade, the most desperate effort will be made to force the fash- ion upon us. Our tastes cannot be expected to carry weight with capitalists and speculators busy with their investments. As a matter of fact, more than the thin edge of the wedge has already been driven home. Last week the Regent-st. firm which boasts of selling more dresses than any other in England, was showing dresses containing no less than six half-hoops of steel. From that to the crinoline in all its dangerous vulgarity is but a step. But if I rightly understand the temper of sen- sible women at this moment, it is a step which will not be taken by the vast majority. It is, perhaps, only !Inman that Lady Harberton' having missed the su- preme moment for securing favourable notice of the society she leads, should reproach me for not agitating for "general" dress reform, but that she should go fur- ther and actually approve of the crinoline will, I think, Cause endless regret and amazement. A Dress Reform Association which does not object to the crinoline need never hope to enrol 5,265 members in a week, nor, for the matter of that, in a generation.</p>
        <p>KAY'S COMPOUND, for Coughs and Colds. Asthma and Bronchitis are relieved by it, 94d. 134 d. Ac.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 21, 1893</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 21, 1893</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LITERARY AND PHILO-</p>
        <p>SOPIIICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>THE PL ACE OP GEC K .E 913.0</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a most masterly paper on " The place of Greek in education" was read by the Rev. A. Gibbon Smith, M.A., before the above- named society. The president, Mr. Robert Davies, was in the chair. There was a fair'attendance. The rev, gentleman contended that a classical education was far from being a hindrance to mathe- matical and scientific studies but was their natural pre- cursor, and trained the mind for them. He would not dis- cuss the question whether what was called a commercial education should be put in rivalry with a classical edu- cation, because, properly speaking, there was no such thing as a commercial education, as that term was understood. Education meant a drawing out and ex- panding of the mental faculties, a learning how to learn. What people called a commercialeducation was not an education but a training for a commercial pursuit. Such subjects as book-keeping and shorthand were not education, and could not be called so except by a mis- nomer. They were instruments not for developing the powers of the mind, but for winding up, as it were, a commercial machine. A boy who learnt how to learn could learn anything, an a boy who had had a good classical education woul4l learn shorthand and book- keeping better than under other conditions. The true demdcracy, the democracy of the new Jerusalem—for there was a new Jerusalem—was the democracy of education—education religious and intellectual. The old classical education went a long way towards realis- ing that ideal. In these days classical education was being more and mere confined to the great public schools and schools which followed their type, acces- sible only to the children of wealthy parents, while OD the other side of the ever-widening chasm higher grade Board schoels and othsr forms of elementary teaching were supposed to supply all the intellectual food required by children of a less favoured lot. It was because it required long sustained effort, unre- mitting attention because its acquisition was a matter of years and not of months, that classical learning stood first among instruments of education. It was precisely that process of mental training which hardened the muscles of the mind, and it did so by a system of uni- form graduation. It was possible to make shows of unreal knowledge and lucky guesses in some subjects, but not in classics. Now the very habit of getting "things up" was of UM utmost practical value in after life, and we English people had a keen eye for practical value. Many years ago the German educationalists came to the conclusion that the old gymnasium system, which corresponded closely to our Grammar Schools' was too narrow. SO the State set ups number of Realschulen throughout the country, in which Greek had no place, ,Latin a subordinate place. What were the results ? Germany had seen a whole generation brought up under that system, and it was generally admitted that the Realschulen were not a success—that it had produced inferior results to the old gymnasium. France had allowed classical studies to sink into decay. There was scarcely one eminent French classical scholar at the pre- sent time. What did we see? Was there a proportion- ately larger number of mathematicians, or a surpass.- ingly great harvest of scientific discoverers ? Nothing of the kind. Mr. Smith said they were asked some times to look at the English of John Bright, who knew no Greek, but he was a genius, and geniuses triumphed over all disadvantages. The rev, gentleman implied that since the classics had become the educa- tional heritage of the few in this country conversation and letter writing had deteriorated. And yet they were told that education was making gigantic progress! In conclusion, the rev, gentleman said they were told that the Renaissaince Caine after a period of prolonged literary sterility. If it was the debtiny of the English race (and he prayed it might not be) that they were to i pass through another age of literary sterility, n which chemistry and biology would have banished all other atudies, let them at any rate hope that there would suc- ceed to it another and yet fairer Renaissaance, which ,would give to the many and not merely to the few a noble culture and an eager enthusiasm for learning, a culture which Would rescue the sons of England from dev•-,ting both their bodies and their souls to material cares, as if man's life were made to revolve in an endless circle of identity, like an imprisoned squirrel in a cage. (Applause.) The Csamitesi made some observations on the paper. He said he did not think for a moment that anyone present considered Mr. Smith exaggerated the effect of the knowledge of Greek upon English literature and conversation,upon English fancy and imagination. One of the pleasures of his (the Chairman's) life had been his knowledge Greek; and he could not conceive of any- one thoroughly understanding scientifit terms without this knowledge. He said he cared comparatively little about Euclid or algebra but congratulated himself on having had the opportunity of learning Greek.—The Rev. L. J. T. Darwell moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Smith, eulogising highly the paper.—Mr. C. S. Burgess seconded. and Messrs. R. Dobson and J. R. Young (the secretary) supported the proposition. All the four gentlemen made remarks on the subject of the paper.— The motion was carried, and acknowledged by Mr. Smith, who replied to some criticisms upon his paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>HOW TO RETAIN BEAUTY.</head>
        <p>HOW TO RETAIN BEAUTY.</p>
        <p>The physical beauty of women should last until they are far past 50 says a writer' in " Siftings." Nor does beauty reach its zenith under the age of 35 or 40. Mien of Troy comes upon the stage at the age of 40. Aspasia wa 536 when married to the Pericles and she was a brilliant figure 30 years thereafter. Cleopatra was past 30 when she met Antony. Diane de Poictiers was 36 when she won the heart of Henry IL The King was half her age, but his devotion never changed. Anne of Austria was 38 when described as the most beautiful woman in 2urope. Mdme. de Maintenon was 43 when united to Louis, Catherine of Russia 33 when she seized the throne she occupied for 35 years. Mdlle. Mar was "lost beautiful at 45, apd Mdme. Recamier between the ages of 35 and 55. The most lasting and intense passion is not inspired by two decade beauties. The old saw about sweet 16 is exploded by the truer knowledge that the highest beauty does not dwell in immaturity. For beauty does not mean alone the fashion of form and colouring, as found in the waxen doll, The dew of youth and a complexion of roses sometimes combine in a face that is unmoving and unresponsive, as though lacking utterly the life spark. A woman's best and richest years are from 26 to 40. It is arrant error for any woman to regard herself as " passe " at an earlier</p>
        <p>day. In the course of years, however, a time arrives when the coming of age must be recognised, when the muscles begin to relax, the skin to lose its polish, and roundness and softness give place to angles. Contentment and good humour will still outrival all medical inventions as a preservative of youth. A woman, beautiful in all else, but wanting mirth, will grow old, sour, thin and sallow, while the merry, fun-loving woman will be fresh and sweet, despite life's happenings and sorrows. Every person should sleep alone, and all physicians sup- port this injunction. A buoyant girl will be rendered irritable, ill, and imbecile by sleeping with a sullen' morose woman. This matter is understood in Oriental countries far better than in Western nations. Every girl is recommended to make a study of beauty ; but beauty of the real kind, not the fictitious type, and cramped figure, gaudy apparely and liberal use of paints, dyes,and washes. True beauty rests on plain living and high thinking, on blood, bearing, and brains. It is in one sense a relative thing. To dip far into philosophy on the subject, is not necessary. The difference between genuine and meretricious beauty is well understood by our race according to its own standards. Temperance, purity, and exercise create an external beauty indicat- ing interior excellence, An expansive head conveys the idea of mental excellence and a bad skin that of impure blood. But perfect beauty according to art standards is not essential, nor is born regularity of features. Beauty is due to grace and actlyity ; to the vital system, the trunk, digestion, flesh texture, tint ; the vigour of brain, emotion, and expression. Here is a bread field for self- interpretation and improvement. Every woman should stand often before her mirrors in critical judgment and earnest investigation of personal advantages and detects as one means of correcting grimaces, distorted attitudes, I and awkward motions. A woman who has lost peace and happiness is indeed prodigal if she sends her face after them. The friction of a hand dipped in soft water will often banish pallor from cheek and temple. To be definite, this application of massage to the face should not be downward, but upward and outward, bearing in mind the labours of the day. The sensitive muscles of the mouth especially are to be smoothed from the daily strain. The gospel of relaxing, of " letting go " of one's self at times, is essential to facial well-being. The nervous system, like the violin, must not be kept always at - concert pitch. All this requires effort, but beauty is not to be attained on other terms. The greatest attainable excellence of the body should be developed and maintained harmon- iously. Beauty means harmony, balance, and mental fire of sensibility, as well as bodily fascination. banish fretting, trivial perturbation, scowling, whining, wail- ing, excessive laughter, and pointless smiling. The cul- ture of beauty must be a life-long noble purpose. In the first place, health is all important. Flesh, texture, and tint, for example, depend upon it. A complexion lack- ing lustre plumpness, and elasticity, shows a lack some- where in 'the vital or nutritive system. A mild diet, gentle temperature, even digestion, open-air exercise, sleep, and a tranquil mind pertain to loveliness. Mistakes of diet begin usually in childhood, Often a girl sits down to a potato and pickle, several cups of strong tea, pies, cakes, ices and fiery condiments. If meat be on the bill of fare there is a chance that it has been spoiled in the cooking. As a result, when the girl is 20 her eyes are dull, teeth yellow, gums pale, lips wan, flesh flaccid, and skin unyielding. Recourse is had to padding, face washes, stains and belladonna. The habits of life are unaltered. ilefare there can be an improvement a change must be made and firmly per- sisted in. The diet, while generous, must be temperate. Peppered soups, stews, game pates, ragouts, and spices are not good for the complexion. What is termed the epicurean woman will have before she is 30 a blotched face and flabby flesh. Women of nervous and sanguine temperament should restrict themselves to a diet of eggs, milk, bread, fruit, light broths, and crustacea. Erated and natural mineral spring waters should be the daily beverage. All alcoholic drinks and opiates produce a general unhealthy action of the skin, as shown in dry- ness, pimples, blotches, and discolourations. The beautiful woman who takes malt and spirituous liquors daily conspires against her beauty. A lady who con- sulted a physician concerning her red nose confessed ; that her nightly tipple was whisky and water. "Leave out the water," said the doctor, " and your nose will soon be purple." Tonics containing iron, phosphoric • acid, and other drugs are often feund harmful to the complexion. • It is now generally understood that most of the dis- • corntort suffered by the human race is due to errors in food. Judgment should be used as to food and occupa- tion, and gluttonous satisfaction of the appetite 'always avoided. If the complexion has become thick, red, and pimply, the remedy is not in the use of drugs or decoc- tions. Paints and enamels render the face dry and chalky, and would destroy a velvet bloom if it existed. A great deal of beauty at low cost can be obtained through the plentiful use of rainwater, sunlight, and i open air exercise. Frequent bathing s a healthful luxury, especially with the addition of sea salt. Bodily exercise should be carried on temperately, its aim being facile muscles, supple joints, and pliant limbs—in a word, physical beauty. Excessive gymnastics make the joints prominent. How many woman know how to walk? Observe them in the street or entering a draw- ing-room—even the better classes. One shambles, an- other slouches, as if her shoes were down at the heels. But most of them drive, straining every muscle in their bodies, ploughing along with strenuous effort like a ship in a high sea and facing head winds. Five minutes' in- struction in stage walk, properly observed, would rectify every bit of this. A perceptible swing of the body should be manifest with every step, that is, advance all of one side at the same time, with a slight turn right and left of the shoulders as the corresponding foot is projected. A long step—not a stride—easy, unhurried, the leg thrown forward from the hip. This is the secret of the ideal gait. The highest beauty is the beauty of expression, and the cultivation of this requires the crushing out of envy, hatred, malice, and all low motives and passions.</p>
        <p>KAY'S Com Pram% a demulcent anodyne expectorant for Coughs and Colds, Slid., 141. Of all Chemiets.—Adve.</p>
        <p>EMIGRATION.—Intending emigran eto New York, Boston Philadelphia, San Francisco, Canada, China, Japan, Aue. tralla, or New Zealand, should take tt ..kets (lowest rates) at the " Examiner " Office, by anion, American. Australian, Oriental, and other lines.--lohn Walker, agent,"Examiner Office. Bewsev-st.. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
