      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>RECORDER OF LIVERPOOL ON THE HORRORS OF PENAL SERVITUDE.</head>
        <p>THE RECORDER OF LIVERPOOL ON THE HORRORS OF PENAL SERVITUDE.</p>
        <p>--- At the opening of the Liverpool City Sessions on Wed- nesday morning the Recorder (Mr. C. H. riopwood, Q.C.), addressing the grand Jury, described to some ex- tent what penal servitude meant. It was important that judges and the public should realise by means of trustworthy information its dreadful nature. It was not sufficient to visit a prison two or three times and see the ingenuity with which the art of imprisonment had been projected. The majority of visitors cut short re- flection, but if they gave reins to their imagination they would brood over the long months or years of hopeless detention where for 23 hours of each day no ray of sun- light came, and where there was none of that variety so necessary to healthy life of both mind and body—deten- tion without amusement, without the greetings of friendship, always the same monotony of dulness from day to day, month to month, year to year. If there were any outside to send in writing a word or a thought after the poor wretch, or go to visit him, ingenuity in torture prescribed that it should be only once in six months, and under censorship. Some trifling breach of rule might forfeit this inestimable and sole solace of the prisoner's lot. An enlightened judge once essayed the tread-wheel to realise the punishment h 3 inflicted, but he required to be assisted from it in a state of exhaus- tion. They could not expect Home Secretaries and judges to qualify themselves for discrimination in punishment by serving a month, or even a week, of im- prisonment ; but many knew how grievous it was to be confined to the house or a sick bed for a month, and in the mind's eye they could scan the dreary passing away of years of the life of men, to come out at the end broken, deteriorated, if not made vile, cowardly from watching to conciliate the caprice or temper of their keepers, timorous as the beaten hound, merciless, for they had received no mercy, helpless against tempta- tion, and schooled to prey upon others. Intelligent and • capable administrators declared them incorrigible, but there was more than a trace of hypocrisy in their lamen- tations. For the causes their thoughtlessness or their ignorance or presumption was responsible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>RACE WAR IN ALABAMA.</head>
        <p>THE RACE WAR IN ALABAMA.</p>
        <p>A NEGRO LYNCHED</p>
        <p>ISTaw YORX, THURSDAY.—A white girl of thirteen,was delivered of a negro child a few days ago at Alabama City. Being closely questioned, she named the father. A party of whites seized the man and lynched him,pour- ing a volley of bullets into the body.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARS' CONVENTION.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARS' CONVENTION.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of No. 1 ConventiOn, S.W. Lancashire, WaB held on Saturday, in the New Jerusalem schoolroom, Wigan, when there was a , good attendance of deputies and representatives from lodges and temples in Warrington, Earles- town, St. Helens, Ashton, Hindley, and Wigan. The 1 session opened at 4 p.m., Bro. W. Pilkingtort, C.Co., 1 of Hindley, presiding in the temporary absence of 1 Bro. Q.M. Sergeant _Roberts, C.C.T., who arrived I later. The usual committees for reporting to the Press, and to examine credentials of members were ! appointed, after which the reports of officers were read. Bro. R. Keen, C. sec., gave a very brief but , interesting report of the state of several of the lodges. Whilst some had been able to give a good account of , their stewardship, there was one or two who were not i doing so well, and needed some little stimulus. Owing to his business, he had been prevented from visiting , lodges as he was wont, or would like to do, for which he must ask the Convention to excuse him. There I was great need for more earnest efforts, and he trusted ,the Convention meeting that day would , help to push forward the work in hand. Bro. John Vint, C.S.J.T., of Ashton, gave a very in- interesting account .of the juvenile section. The holi- days at Christmas had to some extent interfered with the meetings, still the accounts to hand were very favour- , able, especially from the temples located in Hindley, Wigan, and Earlestown. The Mayflower " Temple at St. Helens, was without a head, the superintendent having removed to Widnes. He had several suggestions ' for united meetings, the temples which would be sub- mitted later on, and he hoped the result would be very beneficial to the junior section. Owing to the continued indisposition of Bro. Brockbank, C. Trea., the financial report was held over to next session. Bro. J. Lindon and R. Thompson, of St. Helens, were appointed audi- tors. After the adoption of the various reports, Bro. Vint read a letter from Bro. A. Newell, D.S.J.T., of Liverpool, suggesting united meetings of temples. 1. Hindley and Wigan ;2. St. Helens and Earlestown ;3. Warrington and Widnes ; and asking for suitable dates when he would s rrange to attend and give special black board lessons to the children. The matter was agreed to, and referred to the juvenile committee to arrange. A letter was read from Bro. Morris Jones, D.C.T., who should have been present, but had been seriously ill and quite unable to leave home. It was resolved to forward to him the sincere sympathy of the Convention. The next session (annual) was fixed to be held at Earlestown on Saturday, February 11th.— A very interesting paper was read by Bro. R. Kean, C. , Sec.—Subject" The duty of the Subordinate Ledge and its members," in whieh he offered some very useful advice. Discussion followed, thanks being tendered to , Bro. Kean for his efforts. The same compliment was paid the Wigan friends for their arrangements and a . very encouraging session was closed at 6.20 p.m. . A splendidly laid tea was partaken of by over 100 members and friends, and a public meeting was after- wards held, presided over by Bro. Q. M. Sergeant , Roberts, C.C.T., of Warrington. Short practical ad- dresses were given by the Chairman, and Bros. George ' Clarke, Earlestown; R. Skaife, St. Helens ; and J. . Whitehead of Wigan; and a most interesting programme of songs, recitations, &amp;c., was rendered in a very satis- factory manner oy various members and friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ALLEGED I:IO3ASTEAD POISONING</head>
        <p>THE ALLEGED I:IO3ASTEAD POISONING</p>
        <p>PLOT.</p>
        <p>HOW THE NON-UNIONISTS WERE MADE ILL.</p>
        <p>PITTSBURG, JAN. 16:—The trial of Hugh Dempsey, the labour leader, and of the men Davidson and Beatty, who are charged with the poisoning of non-unionist workmen at Homestead, was continued here to-day. Several witnesses who were called testified to the illness of the men, and various medical men described their treatment of these men.. Patrick Gallagher, the self-confessed poisoner,gavehis testimony in a calm manner. He said : I know Dempsey, pointing to the prisoner in the dock. II saw him last August in the hall of the Knights of Labour, when both Beatty and Davidson were with me. Beatty addressed Dempsey and said, "These are the two men." Demesey then turned to Davidson and me, and said You know what we want." We replied that we knew a little about it. Dempsey said, "We want to make the men in Homestead Milk sick." Beatty suggested the use of croton oil, and Davidson and I re- plied that we would use it. Dempsey then said that powders hpd been used in breaking the street car strike at Chicago, and that, if we would use these powders at Homestead, the drug would make the men ill and break the strike in ten days. He, moreover, guaranteed the payment of 50 dollars to each of us and told us to get work at the mill. I first met Beatty on the previous day when I was talking with a man named McKenna Beatty accosted us and asked if we wanted to go to. Homestead to do a special job, which he explained was te dose the men working there, and said there were two or three dollars a day in it for ' us. McKenna bad got a job on board a boat, and I was told Beatty would try to get another man to take kis place. Davidson and I .met Beatty the next morning, when we went to see Dempsey. I got a job as second cook at the mill on August 30th. Before entering on my duties Dempsey told me that he would furnish me with the powders to put in the food, and stated that they were often used to weaken prize-fighters, that it was impossible to analyse them, and that a Chicago man was to famish them. I got the first three packets of powder on September 7th or Bth from Dempsey, who intimated to me how I was to use them. The powder was of a yellow hue. On the following day I put the first packet into thirty gallons of soup, and on the second and third day I put the two remaining packets into thirty gallons of tea. I then got nine packets of the powder from Dempsey. Davidson used some of them in the tea and gave packets to Alex. McCord, another cook at the mill. I then got six more packets of powder from Dempsey, two of which I used, and gave Davidson the remaining four. I left work on September 21, and Davidson went to work the next day. I took a little of the dosed soup, and it made me ill. Dempsey asked me if there were many Of the hands ill at the mill, and I told him there were quite a number on the sick list. When I again went to work I saw Beatty in the mill. • Dempsey told me he did not want any one besides Beatty, Davidson, and myself to know that he was mixed up in the scheme. In the meantime two other cooks, who had gone in at the instance of Beatty and Dempsey, were at work. They had also got six other powders from Dempsey. Dempsey used one packet and put the others into the table drawer, from which they disappeared. I left work on Oct. 18, up to which date I got 30 powders from Dempsey, but never received any money direct from him. The testimony of this witness, however, went to show that he was paid indirectly by Dempsey, who also en- dorsed Davidson's bill—" 0.K." Dempsey, he said, sug- gested to him that if compelled he should go into court and swear point blank that he did not know him (Dempsey). Dempsey similarly would swear that he had never seen Gallagher. The witness, on being cross- examined, said that Dempsey told him that a fool had seen him who wanted to go to Homestead and kill the men. Davidson and he (witness) refused to have any- thing to do with the scheme if it were dangerous to life.</p>
        <p>THE RIXTON EXPLOSION.</p>
        <p>- THE ADJOURNED INQUEST. At the Latchford Hospital on Thursday afternoon Mr. Yates, coroner, held the adjourned inquest con— cerning the death of the man Green who was killed by the explosion at Rixton, last week. Col. Fords H.M. Inspector of Explosives was present, and the Ship Canal Company were represented by Mr. A. Newmen, from the office of Messrs. Grundy and Kershaw, solicitors to the Ship Canal Company, and. Mr. Griffiths, manager of the section. The Coroner explained that Hendicott, one of the witnesses, was unable to leave the hospital yet to give his evidence. What he had to say as the foreman would be rather important, and, therefore, he thought it would be advisable that his evidence should be taken at the bedside. He thought the explanations which Mr. New- man and Col. Ford had to make would assist greatly in elucidating the facts of the case. ' Mr. Newman said he had been to visit the scene of the explosion at Rixton, and had procured certain facts which he had no doubt would greatly assist them in understanding how the accident occurred. He then ex- plained that the pontoon to which the accident had occurred, was a flat-bottomed boat, and measured Mt. 6in. between the deck and the bottom, and it drew la inches of water. Right through from the deck to the bottom, were seven holes, through which long pipes were pushed down to the bed of the river. Then through these holes they had their drill s which worked the seven holes in the rocky bottom. The drills were taken up and the charges of tonite, which were placed in cartridges, were dropped down through the pipes into the holes in the rocks which just held them. On the occasion of the accident there were 14 men oa the pontoon. The seven holes were drilled and the charges, consisting of three cartridges each, were pushed down the pipes. The car- tridges contained tonite. Before applying the fuse the pipes which had to be lifted from the bottom of the river were tested and found to be clear. The fuse was applied, and the pontoon removed to a distance. Six of the charges exploded all right, but the seventh by some means went off beneath the pontoon, with the sad re- sult already known. William Potter, a" powder monkey," to the explod- ing party said he had been engaged in carrying the explosives. They commenced blasting the river bed on Saturday and continued until Monday morning last week. In the course of his evidence he said he saw Handicott, the foreman, place three cartridges in each tulle. He could not say whether all the pipes had been loosened before Handicott applied the fuse, but he thought so. When the fuse was ignited they removed the boat to the bank some 60 yards away, and then six of the charge went off, and whilst wondering what had become of the seventh it suddenly went off beneath the pontoon. He had leaped upon the bank before this, and saw that in the explosion a beam which was shot up struck the de- ceased in the left side. Deceased had been engaged to bore holes in the rock. He could not say exactly wheth2r three cartridges had been placed in the seventh charge. The Foreman of the Jury:—You would know how many you brought from the stores ?—I bought a box—they are weighed in pounds, not counted. •Colonel Arthur Ford. H.M. Inspector of Explosives, next gave evidence. He said he had visited the scene of the accident and particularly examined the pontoon. He had also had conversation with the foreman who charged the holes. Mr. Griffiths, the manager of the section of the canal,had also given him every facility for getting a correct opinion of how the affair happened. Every- thing that he had seen and heard pointed to the fact that the explosion occurred not beneath the pontoon. and at the bottom of the river, but in one of the pipes down which the charges were forced. There was a pipe now in court in which the explosion had certainly taken place. It had originally been about 12 feet long, but quite a foot had been shattered off the end. This was a plain indication of his theory that the explosion had taken place in the pipe, and he certainly considered that one of the cartridges had gone off there. That showed that the cartridge had either been design- edly pulled back into the pipe, or it had not been pro- perly driven down. However, he dismissed all suspicion of design and felt satisfied that the cartridge had never been in the hole. He accounted for that in this way : Three cartridges were not safe as a charge because the first two were always dropped down the pipe,and thus, as had probably happened in that case, one of them had stuck half way down the hole, and when the third was put down it would remain in the pipe. There was a chance of it dropping out when thc pipe was lifted, but in this case it had evidently remairhd in the pipe and exploded there. It seemed to him that using the three cartridges for a charge was a kind of makeshift, because there were long cartridges made for the purpose. There was always danger of a similar accident occurring when more than one cartridge was used in a charge. The Coroner : Do not explosives Acts deal with that? —No. They merely regulate the manufacture of explo- sives, but when an accident in their use occurs like this an inspector is always sent down to enquire into it, and to give advice or take any other measures. Mr. Newman: But I don't see exactly where the danger of having three cartridges comes in ?—Colonel i Ford : But there s a danger,and a great one too. There' have been many accidents in using a charge of that character in mining. There is a danger of them slip- ping into the bed of the river and exploding when struck by the delver of a dredger. Mr. Newman : I am led to believe that the cartridges are not particularly dangerous, I believe that if they are placed in a fire they will not explode ?—But they most certainly will. It placed in the fire quietly and touched they might—in exceptional cases—burn quietly without exploding. But that would be an exception. Further evidence was given by Thomas Brown and Edwin Goodwin, both of whom were engaged on the pontoon. Their evidence corroborated Mr. Newman's statement. The Coroner, who had with some of the jurymen visited the injured man Handicott, read that witness's statement. His story was in every detail similar to that told by other witnesses. Handicott had lighted the fuses of all the seven charges; an he could only account for the explosion by the fact that the tonite got buried i the pontoon in the bed of the river. The Coroner (to Colonel Ford) : Do you think that anyone is censurable for the accident, either by neglect or carelessness P—No, there is no doubt that it has been a simple accident. The man with the lapping rod might have been more careful, because I am inclined to believe that had he been regarding his measurement carefully in lapping that seventh charge, he would have discovered the dislodgment of one of the cartridges which has caused the accident. Then,too,l think that it would be advisable for the company to use long cartridges in future, when doing any such operation. After some consideration, the jury returned a verdict of 'Accidental death."</p>
        <p>3 .777,0 q</p>
