      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>" SIIMMAT WRUNG SUMWHEER t "</head>
        <p>" SIIMMAT WRUNG SUMWHEER t "</p>
        <p>Aw've getten buzzin's in my yed, An' wen aw clutter sawt o' bed, Aw feel ez if my wits wur fled— Tiler's summat wrung sumwheer ! Aw wander wot aw've bin abeawt ; An' wen aw wakken wi a sheaut, Be bang'd if aw con find it eawt— Tiler's automat wrung sumwheer ! Sumtimes aw go to th' Club at neet, An' whether its browt on wi' freet, Ther's summat alias darken's th' leet— Summat wrung sumwheer I At times" Baur William "keeps me late, An' wen aw get inside my gate, Tole not be puzzle't gyouse my fate— Theis summat wrung sumwheer ! Just neaw an' then BAV'M in th' bad books, An' get sum very sarious looks, Throo nowt but watcbin' " Peter's " flooks I Ther's summat wrung surnwheer ! An' wen, sumtimes, aw caunt resist The pleasure ov a gam' at whist, Aw'm welly sartin to be miss'd— Summat wrung sumwheer ! If off to Nyowton aw thud trot, An' Bashaw happens t' he on th' spot, Aw'm beaun't be late an' catch it hot— Sum mat wrung sumwheer ! An' if th' owd station mester's theer, To gammon me wi' bi-., good cheer, He'll only lowf at wot aw fear— Summut wrung sumwheer ! He'll tak' me fer a tarn on th' ice, An' wi' a chuckle say it's nice, Wen brokken shocders are to splice I Summat wrung sumwheer If aw feel tired an' strowl to th' "Bull," An' tak' sum physic coz aw'm dull, My bally's sure to get too full— Summat wrung sumwheer ! An' if aw travel to th' " Legh Arms," Wheer th' popper mester lends his charms, Aw connot Berry War's alarms ! SUMMut wrung sumwheer ! But, lads, ez eaur owd craytur's vex'd, Amr'll mend my ways an' change my text,— (Yole find it soon, but not ith' next)— To nowt wrung nowheer ! TUNI. 0' PEGS.</p>
        <p>Rose Bonk, January 20. 1893. PUBLIC NOTICE.---I.oLn.esad ttUNDREDS of people Of both sexes with wrecked and debilitated constitut;ons are yearly restored to sound and robust health again by Messrs. Wilkinson, 3 and 4, Fitzallau Square, Sheffield. Write to them for advice free when suffering from weakness, loss of flesh, appetite, disturbed rest, pimples, blotches, indiges- tion, pallid skin, nervousness, and general debility. Posi- tive cure for all Blood aud Skin Dis,a,es, acquired or consti- tutional. Sixty years' experienc. One address (as above) for over 30 years. The" A. 1 Mixture," 4s. 91. the case. The Magic Mirror in envelope, four stamps. Sufferers disap- pointed with Elsctric Belts should write to us at once.—Advt.</p>
        <p>-</p>
