      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>TERRIBLE WEAPON.</head>
        <p>A TERRIBLE WEAPON.</p>
        <p>2,000 SHOTS A MINUTE.</p>
        <p>HAUTFORD, CONN., WEDNESDAY.-It is reported that Dr. Gatling is attracting the attention of the American naval and military authorities by his researches in ele:i- tricity,which have culminated in his inventing a method ,by which electricity can be applied to firearms. He claims that his new gun will be superior to existing Gatling guns and mitrailleuses, or any other machine machine guns, and will fire 2,000 shots a minute. The power is derived from an electric motor which is attached by a belt to the mechanism of the gun. Toe cost of firing will be reduced to a minimum. The invention will shortly be tested ny the 13..iard of Ordnance ()nicer&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>OLDHAM AND THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>The voting papers regarding the proposed loan of. £250,000 by the Corporation of Oldham to the Manches- ter Ship Canal Companywere counted on Tuesda y,and the result was made known on Tuesday evening,as follows For the loan 9,08&amp;; against, 7,451 ; majority for, 2,354.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FALL FROM A BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>FALL FROM A BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>A man named John McCarthy while working on the bridge which is in course of construction on the Ship Canal at Stockton Heath, fell below, a depth of several feet, and was seriously injured about the face. He waa taken to the hospital at Latchford, where his injuries were attended to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>COMING HOHE RULE BILL</head>
        <p>THE COMING HOHE RULE BILL</p>
        <p>MR. LABOUCHERE'S VIEWS. Writing to the "Times," Mr.Labouchere says:— Your Faris correspondent sends to you a prr4;.l of a conversation that I recently had with the representative of the" Temps' in London, which leads me to tie consi elusion that this gentleman did not vita catch the tenor of my observations.</p>
        <p>As regards Home Rule, I did not, of ccurse, say that there were only three Home Rulers in the world—Mr. Gladstone, Mr. Morley, and myself. I said that, as there were no stronger Home Rulers, except myself, than Mr. Gladstone and Mr. Morley in Parliament, it is probable that a Cabinct in which the one is Prime Minis- ter and the other Irish Secretary will submit to the House of Commons a satisfactory Home Rule Bill. Asked whether I thought that it would be carried in the House of Commons, I said that this was probable, but that there were many Liberals, in the Cabinet and out of it, who would say, if not carried owing to above twenty Irish M.P.'s voting against it, "We have done all that we can for you ; now English reforms must have prece- dence." Assuming that the bill is one that ought to satisfy Ireland, I confess that I should he amongst those Liberals.</p>
        <p>--- Mr. Labouchere, discussing the Irish question in Troth," says :—I lay down three requirements, with- out which no Home Rule Bill will be acceptable :-1. It must be a final settlement. 2. It must be accepted as a final settlement by the great body of Irish Nationalists. 2. It met relieve the Imperial Parliament of the in- cubus' of Irish debates, so as to have a clear field for 13ritish issues. Any bill that does not fulfil these require- ments is a mere intrigue.</p>
        <p>At Leicester on Wednesday night, the Attorney General (Sir Charles Russell, MP.,) addressed a crowd- ed meeting of Liberals, and dealt chiefly with Irish affairs and the attitude of the House ot Lords. The i coming Home Rule Bill would, he said, differ n one leading point from that of 1886, which contemplated the exclusion of the Irish members.</p>
        <p>Dr. Tanner, M.P., was present on Wednesday evening at a large meeting of Irishmen at Stockport, at which a resolution was carried expressing confidence in the Liberal Government and in their'power to pass such a measure of Home Rule as woilld bring contentment and peace to the Irish people. lii a speeeh Dr. Tanner pre- dicted that the new Home Rule Bill would be in all respects more acceptable than the one of 1886.</p>
        <p>• •</p>
        <p>c-‘ ..E.4 PP S' S</p>
      </div>
