      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JANUARY 21, 1893.</p>
        <p>The National: THE meetingr, of the National Libe- Liheral ral Federation, which was opened at</p>
        <p>Federation. Liverpool, on Thursday, is a gather. ing of more than usual importance,. and seeing that a Liberal Cabinet holds the reigns of Govern- ment, it is not at all unlikely that its discussion° and decisions may influence and mould legislati°l2' The fifteenth annual report of the Federation, contains not only a digest of recent political hie" tory, but also enlarges upon the reforms of the future. A perusal of it shows haw ample is els field of beneficial legislation that lies immediateli in front of the Liberal party. Home Rule stands first, but after that there are many important questions ripe for treatment. An analysis of the voting at the last election, which is given in ther, report, is interesting and suggestive. The recorclee' votes show a total majority of 224,665 in faWrir, of Home Rule, as compared with a majority °' 77,754 against it in 1886. The twenty-five Vb.. erals who sit for metropolitan constituencies rePre- sent an average of 7,174 votes per seat, and thB thirty-two Tories and Liberal Unionists who were successful at the poll an average of 6,665; in English boroughs, the sixty-three Libel's: members who were returned after a contest election represent an average of 9,824 votes es compared with 7,272 for the eigty-eight ists " returned. In the English counties tee, Ininety-nine Liberal members who won contests° elections are credited with an average of votes per seat; that of the one hundred an" twenty Tories and Dissentients was 8,017. the grand total for the United Kingdom, _Athe, Liberal candidates secured 4,005 votes against 3,781 3,781 recorded for their Tory and Diss,en; tient Liberal opponent&amp; The thorough-go3° Liberalism of Wales turned a representation of twenty-three Liberals and seven TorieS. into a representation of twenty-eight Liberals aan two Tories, and this, the Federation holds, justi- fies them in putting Disestablishment for Wi'des into the forefront of the Liberal programm°. result of the election petitions is glanced at, an the Committee exiiress the opinion that the portance of the decision of the Hesharn case Cell hardly be over-estimated, as it established that candidature may begin eighteen months prior to an election, and that Primrose League or servative Association entertainments, aided by the candidate or his election agent, though given, twelve months before the election, are coffer' and illegal. The differences between a Liberal and a Toll government does not consist alone in the leglBlB- - that is carried out or formulated. In the administration alone there is a Wide scope f°r differences and contrasts, and in this respect Oa: present government, brief though its career 118 yet been, already compares favourably with Yr. predecessor. By administrative reforms in 110 land the operation of the Coercion Act has beeto suspended, a Commission appointed to inquire in the case of evicted tenants, and the clemency ete the Crown has been extended to the Gweedore prisoners. In England, Trafalgar Square haS,onci again, been thrown open to public meetings, 01°7 measures have been taken to secure a fuller benefit r from factory legislation. The state of the Poe. is to be inquired into by a Royal Courre.'s" sion ; and certainly not the least indicatioa of a more liberal and enlightened administratioo is furnished by the efforts of the EducationDePer merit to secure to parents the benefits of free education. In the appointment of magistrates ' vast change has come over the methods of adraiiii- istration, and this is evidenced by the considerab 0 number of workingmen who had been placed 111)011 the bench in boroughs. In this connection aPO test is made by the Federation against the inellner</p>
        <p>, ; . ' ' ii, ';.'..:,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>jo which the Liberals have been excluded from the county magistracy ; and, as an example, it is Mentioned that in Leicestershire there are only eight Liberals out of one hundred and seventy-six county magistrates, although the county returns four Liberals and only one Tory to Parliament. The Federation does excellent service in bringing to light all injustices and anomalies of this des- wiption, and if it succeeds in lessening the pre- Iforainance of Tories and game-preservers on country benches it will have done a beneficial Work</p>
        <p>THE show-men who purvey The Vaa- l:Yellers. amusements of a varied character at I" fairs," are not always regarded With a favourable eye. The ordinary stay-at-home ,hcalseholder is disposed to look upon the mode of afe of his migratory fellow citizen as anything but re€Pectable; and there is a tendency in some quarters t° keep the showman at a distance, as was evid- eljeed by the resolution passed at the last meeting `a the Warrington Town Council in favour of abol- ng the "fair." Yet we are told by a writer iu Manchester contemporary that as a class the 1111, °w-people are not poor. They live in rude cora- 40rt and are much better off than the average llorkingmen in the town. The van-child has generally plenty to eat. The showman is, usually, 4, capitalist in a small way. The' owner of a dobby-horse " and cycle apparatus assessed its 'clue at £1,500, this sum including the wagons "id traction engines, and he said it cost him £2O a "Leek in fuel and labour to work it. The manager a " fit-up " asserted that he paid in wages £25 'Week to a company of eleven, and that the leenery, properties, booths and dresses were Worth £6OO. A circus stud was valued at ~.. 4400, the eight human performers receiving £l5 a :Oak, whilst in the weekly expenses of a menagerie, ;co food for the animals alone was put at £4O. roaY, indeed, be safely conceded that a fair 'flount of money is required even to " run " a 'rooting gallery or an " Aunt Sally." We are told, farther, that the houses of some of the wealthier lan3w-men are really miniature palaces on wheels, With the luxuries of a Pullman-car on a small IN% The van is divided by curtains into draw- ing-room, bedroom, and kitchen, the walls are covered With pictures and mirrors, and in some ears% there is a small library—and it is remarkable to relate—even a piano. It is,however,only the com- Paratively wealthy proprietors who own vans of tkis description. The ordinary showman inhabits vehicle of a much more modest description, but iii nearly all cases there are many ingenious de- 'ices fer economising the meagre space. One of the most serious evils of the van-dweller's mode of living is the almost entire ignorance in which tke children grow up. With a view to remedying !Ilia to some extent, the Van-Dwellers' Protection A'sague have made a novel proposal. It is, that certificated teachers should travel through the .c.'°untry in caravans in order to instruct 'he children as opportunity offered. It is La. id that van - children are quick to learn. A. Part from school learning their education ranch more varied than that of either lae ordinary town or country children. We are toM that "most van-children are much Inserter !la. au the average of their age. They have seen ZnaZ world, and read the green fields and facts of ch ry oZtelike a book. A van-child knows town and life equally well. From his early years , i 8 a Practical botanist and an ardent student of natural history in the "leafy glades." He does not need to be taught—and this we can believe nuply--anah elementary arithmetic as how kany pennies there are in a shilling. Under a 4ehoolteaster his progress, we are told, would be rapid, despite the fragmentary character of his studies.</p>
        <p>One of the most interesting articles in this wlonth's magazine is the account which appears ill the " Idler" of a day spent with Mr. JoHN Th:Taits, the democratic M.P. for Battersea. To those people, who sedulously cherish and propa- gate the notion that a workingman leader of Workingmen is, almost of necessity, a blatant loud-tongued and ignorant " agitator " or "dema- gogue,'. the article will be rather of the nature of 0 revelation. Mr. Buxom is educated—self-ed- heated, we should say,—and in his struggle for kilowledge he could many a time have said, in the l'ords of Erasmus," As soon as I get any money I shall buy books—and. then I shall buy some ' Clothes." The books in his library, as he told his liSiter, were bought with scores of meals; and 4alleek's "Is life worth living ?" represented a ri.'ree battle in his mind as to whether it should be the book or a pair ,of boots, and the book won the ;143'• Like Mr. GLADSTONE, and many another 000 k-lover, he prides himself upon the arrange- andb the classification of his volumes. He ;oasts, too, of having the best collection of social- 'atio pamphlets in England. In dealing with 4°oial themes, Mr. BURNS referred to the love of betting which has, as he says, become the work- -111:14.8 curse. He holds that the explanation Or the great prevalence of this vice is monotony of the modern workman's occupa- t,rl m "Machine industry tends to de-individua- 8° a man in these days. In the old. days of , 'reeee and Rome, and mediaeval England, the reverse was the case; painting, sculpture, the high ,eooditions of the crafts, brought out all a man's 'lldividuality, his best points." "Man," he con- ti!llued, "is a pleasure-loving animal, and must get tsio sport if not in his work. I have come to l'alt that the more the artisan of to-day has to ,'"'l7rlc, the more he bets." There is a good deal in t '4B opinion of Mr. BURNS which deserves the C • ellsideration of social reformers. SKATING MATCHES AT ST. HELENS. titt Carr Mill Dam, near St. Helens, on Monday Lit?theon, another important match between 'red n3riand, the champion quarter-mile, of Sefton, to 'Pavid Roberts, of lace, near Wigan, £lO a-side Place, the distance being 160 yards. About a 6 t "sand persons had assembled. The betting was Iv so 2on Litherland. Both competitors got away thtll at the rtart, but Litherland, gradually getting la lead, won easily by three yards. The time was etc; sees. Both men used the ordinary wooden 4ek turned-up skates. A match between: two St. tl eleas skaters named J. Mason and J.Cunliffe, over v 4 eourse of 3'20 yards, for £3 a-side, was won by '1 former. ' btrite`aPeTs and MSC:ILEUMS made and fitted free of charge 1111:8G6ALE.—d, and W. DUTTON Sankey-st.—Adv 0 t. k" ,RINOLINE QUESTION AND THE IRON TRADE. retn'irde thirty years ago, one of our correspondents Itacilu us, fortunes were made in the hoop-iron ful °by the introduction of the exceedingly grace- crinoline ; and, our correspondent un- aptly suggests, "if 'John Strange Winter' could theindur:eu to withdraw her denunciation against konremoured revival of an honourable fashion, , the e.Y might still be minted in certain branches of , It._ jrop trade." Probably Mrs. Stannard has not. , 1 fill'iold the question from the point of view of a boe peing imparted to the languisbing native hon- k ei.iliPo?mlustry by the recall to public favour of the tit" "fte• If she have not, and she should some- list. 8 peruse the technical journals, she may, at the "-)t offending a good proportion of her large t.." Of ternale admirers, be indaced to reconsider ots:__t IN, however, evidently, a firmly-grounded 4"040a. --Iron. ',</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>FIRE AT RUNCORN. On Tuesday night a wooden structure used as a car- penter's shop at the Docks, was burnt d own. The damage is estimated at about £4O. CARDINAL LOGUE. The Irish party at Runcorn on Tuesday night author- ised a message of congratulation to be sent to his Eminence Dr. Logue, on his election to the Cardinslate. JORKPII PRlTCHARD.Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- at.. and 4, Bridge-at.. Runcorn.—Adst. Sr. JOHN'S BAND OF HOPE, RUNCORN. An entertainment was given by the me mbers on Wednesday evening, when a large audience assembled. Songs, glees, duets, and recitations, wero given, and the chairman, Mr. James Riaardson, delivered an address. When in want of a. chaste and elegant wedding or birthday present go to PRITCHARD% 51, High-fgt.—Advt. ALBERT-ROAD BAND OF HOPE WIDNES. A public meeting in connection with this society was held on Tuesday evening, when an address was delivered by Mr. John Travers. During the evening, songs reci- tations, &amp;c., were given. The chair was occupied by Mr. T. Lawrinson.</p>
        <p>- - TIE ASSAULT ON A WIDNES PUBLICAN. On Wednesday, at the Liverpool County Quarter Sessions, Joseph Bradbury was charged with doing grievous bodily harm to Samuel Hurst, landlord of the Commercial Hotel, Appleton, by kicking him and break- ing his arm. Prisoner was sentenced to six months' im- prisonment with hard labour. JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- st.. and 4, Bridge st.. Runcorn.—Advt. WESTON BIBLE AND 'MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS. At the usual weekly meeting of this class held on Monday night last, Mr. Joseph Parker of Runcorn, an old member of the class, read a paper on" The Two Combatants." There was a good attendance of the members and at the close a hearty vote of thanks was passed to the essayist. BURGLARY AT WIDNES. During Tuesday night. burglars effected an entrance into the living premises of the shop of Mr. F. H. Pank- burst, tailor, &amp;e., Victoria-road, by way of the kitchen window, but they proved unsuccessful in gaining ad- mittance to the office or the shop, because of the safe manner in which these places were secured. The marau- ders obtained very little booty. • PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test forso years, andquality and first-class workmanship are still guaranteed.—Advt. FUNERAL OF AN OLD RUNCORNIAN. On Wednesday afternoon, the remains of Mr. William Croley, of Penketh-lane, Runcorn, were interred in the Runcorn Cemetery. The deceased, who was 82 years of age, died somewhat suddenly on Saturday. For many years he carried on business as a boot and shoe dealer in Bridge-St. and High-st. He was well-known throughout the town. The funeral service was con- ducted by the Rev. Dr. Preston. If you wantyour watch repaired by skilful Workmen. take It to PRITCHARD'S.—Advt. MARCH OUT OF THE RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS. On Friday evening last week, the F and G Companies of the 2nd V.B. Cheshire Regiment, took' their first march-out of the season. There was a good muster. The men were accompanied by their new band, the members of which 'played in capital style a series of martial music. A novel feature was the use for the first time of the bugle band, which promises to be a great success. The officers present were Captain G. Ashton, Chaplain-Captain Dr. Preston, and Sergeant- Instructor Player. JosISPV,PRITCHARD keeps spectacles to stilt all sights from a shilling a pair.—Advt. ODDFELLOW'S TIM PARTY AND BALL AT RUNCORN. This popular annual gathering in connection with the Loyal Wright's Pride Past Grand Lodge, 1.0.0., M.A., was held in Runcorn Public Hall on Thursday evening. About 150 persons sat down to tea, but this number was largely augmented at the ball afterwards, when Bro. Woods, quadrille band played for dancing. Bro. William Wright and George Burrows were efficient M.C.'s. Mr. J. Oates, of the Fleetwood Arms, was the caterer. If you want a lucky wedding ring go to 51, Hzen-sr.,where you will get a present with it.—. Advt. THEFT FROM WIDNES MARKET. At the Widnes Police Court, on Monday, before the [ Mayor (Alderman F. H. Gossage) and Mr. H. Wade Deacon, Elizabeth Harveston, Major Cross-street. was charged with stealing a shawl from a stall in the Wid- nes market, the property of A. Walker, Manchester. Police Constable Almond stated that about nine o'clock on Saturday night he was on duty in the market, when he saw defendant take the shawl produced from Mr. Walker's stall. tie took her into custody, when she offered to give him a shilling to let her off.—Defendant pleaded guilty, and was fined 53., or seven days in default. Both PRITCHARD'S Shops are under personal supervislion.aul customers may rely upon receivingeveryattention.—Advt. ROBBERY 15Y BOYS AT WIDNES. On Monday at Widnes Police Court, before the Mayor (Alderman F. H. Gossage) and Mr. H. Wade Deacon, six boys named Thomas Kelly, John Fox, Patrick Fox, William Spencer, James Connony,and Thomas Connolly, all residing at West Bank, Widnes, and whose ages ranged from fourteen to sixteen years, were charged with stealing £3, the moneys of Themes Cooney, Peel- street. The prosecutor lodged at the holm, and the prisoner Kelly also lived there. On Monday Cooney put five sovereigns into his box and locked it, and when he opened the box on Wednesday £3 of the money was mitsing. The prisoners pleaded guilty. The had spent the money in contectionery,rcigarettes, and visits to the theatre. Kelly, the brothers Fox, and Thomas Connolly were convicted of breaking and entering on the let a December last, and they were now committed te gaol for ten days, to be followed by four years' detention in a reformatory ; the other two prisoners were fined 10s. Go to PRITCHARD'S for the best five guinea watch a work- ingman can have.—Advt. INSANITARY PROPERTY AT RueCorix. Luke Holmes, auctioneer, High-st., was summoned to Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday to answer a charge under the Public Health Act, of being the owner of defective and insanitary property in Bridge-st.—Mr. Lake, clerk to the Board of Improvement Commis- sioners, said the case was before the Court a month ago, when Mr. Burton appeared for the defendant, and the case was adjourned for a month in order that the altera- tions required might be made. The alterations had been partially carried out the delay in completing them pro- bably arising from the frost. He asked the Bench to make an order in accordance with the notice issued by the Commissioners for the nuisances to be remedied within a month.—James Percival, nuisance inspector, proved the service of the notices, which required water closets to be substituted for the ordinary closets and the ashpits to be concreted and covered at numbers 42, 44 and 46 Bridge-st.—ln reply to the Bench, Mr. Lake said it was quite necessary that these alterations should be made; nothing but water closets were safe. There, had I been typhoid fever in one or two of the- houses.-,The order asked for-was made. JOSEPH PRITCHARD has the best stock of ladies' silver and gold watches in the town.—Advt.</p>
        <p>RIVAL BUTCHERS AT RUNCORN. At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, before Mr. C. Reynolds and Dr. Steele, George Tams, butcher, Church- , street and Regent-street, was summoned for assaulting Thomas Clough,of Regent-street. There was also a cross- summons charging Gough with assaulting Tams.—Mr. R. D. Maddock appeared for Clough, and Mr. E. W. Humphreys for Tams. The men are rival butchers, hay. in shops opposite each other in Regent-street, and it was alleged by Gough that on Saturday night the seventh inst., at 10.30 p.m., Tams crossed the road to dough's shop, and holding up his hands he said" The moon has changed since you married Susan." Clough asked Tams what he meant, and Tams made no reply, but swung his left hand round and struck Clough on the mouth. Clough resented this, and struck Tams, who not being sober, fell down on the pavement; bruising his face in doing so. Mr. Maddock informed the Bench that Tams was constantly making jeering and mocking remarks, and generally made himself a nuisance, and the summons was taken out as much to stop that nuis- ance as well as for the assault. He suggested that a small fine should be inflicted upon Tams, who should also be b.und over. Clough was also willing to be bound over as well, as he only desired to conduct his business quietly.—Clough, Charles Watson, Jessie Owen, P.C. Gregory, and John Purse gave evidence. The case for Tams was that the remark ahout Susan was an idiotic byword, having no reference to Mrs. Clough, or, any other individual, and that Tams gave no provoca- tion whatever. It was denied that he struck Clough, but that the latter gave Tams a blow on the face, which knocked him down and injured his nose, and gave him a black eye. It was pointed out that Clough had been fined three times for assaults on Tams and his assistants, and on the last occasion he was bound over to keep the peace for six months. Mr. Humphreys said a fine was powerless to prevent Clough, who was a hot-tempered little fighting butcher from committing these assaults, and he asked that he should be punished by imprison- ment. —Tams was called, and evidence was given by George Blood, Robert Evans and John Dyson. The Bench hound over the defendants to keep the peace for six months, each to pay his own costa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 21, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 21, 1893.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN MUSICAL SCCIETY.</p>
        <p>In connection with this Society a ballad and instru- mental concert will be given in the Runcorn Public Hall on Thursday evening next. Clever principals have been engaged, and the choir will render several part songs. Full particulars are advertised.</p>
        <p>THE REV. R. J. WARD. The Rev. R. J. Ward, late Congregational minis- ter, of S. Helens, has been taking part in the great decennial Missionary conference in Bombay, at- tended by upwards of 600 missionaries of various denominations from all parts of India. He will shortly leave for his sphere of labour at Madras. Among the subjects discussed at the Conference were " Work among the oppressed classes and the masses," " Social and legal rights of Christians marriage and divorce," "The native Church in India—its organisation and self-support." A num- ber of native teachers took part in the proceedings, and Mr. Ward in a letter says how greatly he has been struck with their " most effective English." ALL HOUSICHOLD LINFIf, hemmed and marked, free of charge.—J. and W,Durroxl Sankey-t.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Ti HOME Inorr TRADE. Although practically ,little change has taken place. in the English iron and steel trades, there is a slight hardening tendency in prices of crude material in one at two districts. The Glasgow war- rant market is more animated, but the business done is generally on account cif the cornering of the bears. Scotch makers' iron is somewhat irregular in price, and quiet. and Cleveland pig is a shade lower. Finished-iron is still in limited request ; but a slightly brisker demand is experienced for tinplates, and in steel rather more activity is re- ported. Old materials are, however, depressed. Shipbuilders and engineers do not exhibit much change. The coal trade is fairly active.—lron. TORTOISE STOVES.—GARLAND, Ironmonger, Warrington , and Ilarlestown.--ildr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>PRESENTATION TO A RUNCORN FOOTBALLER. In recognition of the services of Mr: W. Faulkner to the Runcorn Football Club and Cheshire County, a num- ber of his admirers have recently . presented that fine player with a piece of furniture for his house. Mr. J. Leathwood made the presentation on behalf of the sub- scribers. MILTON CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH. In this place of worship on Sunday the Rev. J. W. Chisholm, of Halifax, conducted special services and preached two eloquent sermons to large congregations. On Monday evening Mr. Chisholm delivered his popular lecture on "Funny Folk" to a good audi- ence. Alderman Sadler presided. BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH RUNCORN. Another "people's service" was held on Sunday evening, when Miss Heatherington sang" The Better Land," and Mr. Branch " Res;gnation." Mr. R. W. Pierpoint performed." The Lost Chord "on his cornet, and Mr. C. LeCouteur played the sole" Gloria "on the organ. The Rev. John Simpson delivered an address,on "True Religion." There was a large congregation.</p>
        <p>WIDNES PRIMROSE LEAGUE. A soiree and dance in connection with this Society WRS held in the Conservative Hall, on Thursday evening. Proceedings commenced with a tea, of which about 100 persons partook, and afterwards an entertainment was given by a number of local vocalists. Dancing was in- dulged in until a late hour. Mr. Gilhart, and Mr. T. C. hdvvards-Moss scut letters of apology for non- attendance. A WIDNES RUNNER'S TRAINING BILL. At Widnes County Court,yesterday, before his Honour Judge Shand, Mrs. Ann Jane Laws, wife of the proprie- tor of the Railway and Commercial Hotel, executrix under the will of Mary Anderson, deceased, sued a man named Win. White, for the sum of £2l, balance of an account for board and lodging for Samuel Morris, a pro- fessional runner, and well-known footballer. Mr. C. ff. Lewin appeared for the plaintiff, and filed a consent signed by the defendant, who agreed to pay the debt at the rate of £2 per month. 'FUNERAL OF A RUNCORN FOOTBALLER. The mortal remains of Mr. Jno. Stubbs, of Dale-st., Runcorn, were interred in the cemetery on Sunday after- noon, The deceased had been a promirent member of the Football Club for many years, and his body was fol- lowed to the grave by representatives of that body, members of the Liberal Club, members of the United Methodist Free Church, the scholars of the class formerly taught by the deceased, and many relatives and friends. The fumeral service was conducted by the Rey. J. Mar- tin. Numerous beautiful wreaths were placed on the grave.</p>
        <p>A BABY BURNT TO DEATH AT WIDNES. Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at Widnes Accident lloSpital, on the body of the infant child of George 'Henry Stowe, grocer, Mersey-road, who had died in that institution en Friday night from the effects of burns. The mother lcft the child in its cradle near the fire, and went to see her husband in his shop, leaving a girl four years of age in charge of the baby. In a few minutes the girl ran to a neighbour and told her the baby was on fire: The neighbour was so " upset " that she could do nothing to save the child, but call the mother, who quickly extinguished the flames by wrapping the baby in a shawl. The little one was dreadfully burnt about the face and arms.—A verdict of " Accidental death "was returned.</p>
        <p>- - SMALLPDX AT RUNCORN. This disease appears to be speedily spreading in the town. On Thursday there were seven patients in the Hospital, and to-day the Runcorn Commissioners will be asked to consider a proposal of the Health Committee to increase the accommodation at that Institution. A man removed from Runcorn to the Workhouse on Saturday, as a fit subject for the hospital, has since developed the disease. Tnere are also cases of smallpox at Weston,. Acton Grange, and Frodsham Marsh, and the Ship Canal Company have given the Rural Sanitary Authority per- mission to erect two temporary hospitals on their land at points the Authority consider suitable. The Health Committee of Runcorn have issued a placard urging all persons over the age of 13 to be re-vaccinated. Jo:wen PRITCHARD IS prepared to wind and keep in repair,. prirsts clocks from 105. per annum.—. Advt. THE UNEMPLOYED IN WIDNES. It will be remembered that the plant for the manufac- ture of caustic soda was stopped on the 29th Decembsr, in Wideee, because of the accumulations of stock. The period of stoppage was for an 'indefinite period," ac- cording to the order of the United Alkali Syndicate, and it is pleasing to find that there iknow a definite inten- tion to re-commence operations. N 0.2 works at Messrs. Mort, Liddell, and Co.'s, are re-started at the beginning of the week, finding employment for about a hundred men,and leaving still about 900 who have "got no work to do." It is intended to set in operation the plaint at Widnes Alkali Works, and Messrs. Golding, Davis, and Co.'s in a week, and the Liver Works, where about 500 are employed in the manufacture of caustic soda, will will be re-opened a week later. As chemical labourers are not a very provident class of the community, this enforced idleness at the present severe season has caused considerable distress amongst them. A WIDNES MERCHANT AND HIS WAREHOUSE.</p>
        <p>At Widnes County Court, yesterday, before His Honour Judge Shand, Richard Titchett, of Hough Green, sued Richard White, metal merchant, Widnes, for El damage to 73 windows in a warehouse occupied by the defendant. The defendant made a counter claim for £5, for inconvenience of tenancy, caused by alter- ations made to the property without his consent. The defence to the claim was that the windows had been broken through the dilapidated condition of the ware- house, and the Judge held that to a certain extent this was correct, and gave a verdict for the plaintiff for 10s. With regard to the counter claim his Honour expressed his belief that Mr. White had • agreed to continue his tenancy on the old terms, at the same time understand- ing that the specified alterations were to he made. Judgment was therefore given for the plaintiff on the 'counter claim with costs.—Mr. Knowles appeared for the defeclant awl Mr. Lewin for the plaintiff. JOSITH PRITCHARD'S sale shop IS 51,11ign-st.. ms workshop 4, Bridge-st.—Advt. EXTRAORDINARY DROWNING FATALITY' AT RUNCORN. About 3-45 on Thursday afternoon, P.C. Proctor went In search of a boatman named James Kershaw, to serve a SIIITIDIODS upon him for being drunk and refusing to quit licensed premises, on the previous day. Tne con- stable found Kershaw standing on the canal bank at Top Locks, but the man refused to take the summons, which was eventually pushed inside his waistcoat. Having thus completed his errand, Proctor was walking away, when he heard a splash, and seeing that Kershaw was struggling in the water, he at once pulled him out. The man was insensible, and Proctor being a member of St. John's Ambulance Association, tried every means to restore animation, but failed,and Dr. Horsley. of Halton, who was passing at the time, was called, and he at once pronounced the man to be in a dying condition.. He expired a few moments later. It is stated that Kershaw was subject to fits, and it is surmised that the excite- ment of having the summons served upon him threw him into a fit. He was not in the water more than a minute. The deceased was 47 years of age, and had charge of the narrow boats Ant and Bee, belonging to Messrs. Simpson, Davies, and Co. A BOATMAN DROWNED AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning. at Runcorn Police Court, Mr. S. E. Worsley, coroner, an inquiry into the death of Joseph Henry Evans, 16 years of age, of 7, Clarence-st., Runcorn.—Thos. Helebv, captain of the flat Derby, said on Saturday morning Evans was engaged in" shafting " the boat of which he was " hand " from the Albert Dock to the Fenton Dock, when his hook slipped and he fell in- to the water. Witness called to some men who were on the opposite side of the Dock. Witness could SWilllt but he could not have reached the deceased in time to save him. Ropes were thrown, but they did not reach de- ceased, who sank in two or three minutes after falling into the water. He did not see any means to restore life after the body had been recovered, fitteen minutes after Evans fell into the water.—The Coroner expressed as- tonishment that none of the people who came to the side of the Dock seemed to have the courage to go into the water, and try and rescue him. How many could swim he did not know. If any of them had been able to swim and had gone to the youth's assistance, he thought his life would have been saved. As to no means for restor- ing life having been used, the Coroner said it could not be too generally knewn that even if a person was to all ' appearance dead, every possible means of restoring life should be used. There had been wonderful cases in which the lives of those apparently drowned had been saved, and therefore no effort should be spared in such cases.—Mr. Shaw, Missionary, said he was an example of what had been done in the shape of restcration after 20 minutes' immersion in the water during severe weather. He suggested that life buoys should be placed in positions at the Docks, where they could be quickly obtained in case of accident.—Mr. J. K. Diggs promised that attention should be given to the suggestion.—A ver- dict Of "Accidentally drowned" was returned. REMARKABLE CONDUCT OF A SMALLPDX PATIENT. As we have already intimated one or two cases of smallpox have broken out at Stockton Heath. On Sunday morning about three o'clock a man living in Bostock-st., who is suffering from the disease,left the Louse during the temporary absence of his nurse; and wearing only a night-dress, wan- dered about the village for some time. About four o'clock he was discovered in the churchyard, his feet having been badly cut by glass and stone. A horse rug and a top coat were placed over the sufferer, and he was taken back to his home. He had been seen by several persons ..passing along the rood, who were quite startled at the strange sight. —Another account states :—The pcior was found in a nude condition, near Suet- ton Church, on Sunday morning. He and been suffering from , smallpox, which had deranged his mind, and during the short absence of his—wife, ha suddenly jumped out of bed and left the house in the early part of the morning, simply clothed in a sheet. In this condition he had been wandering about all night. When found, the poor fellow was nearly frozen to death, the night being bitterly cold He was provided with some apparel and nourishment, and taken to his home as quickly as possible by P.C. Brett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>7111.NGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>7111.NGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>Our congrtitulationa are offered to Mr. and Mrs. Meadowcroft, of Runcorn, on their being privileged to recently celebrate their" Silver wedding." May they both live to attain the age when they can cele- brate their" Golden Wedding."</p>
        <p>The offer made by Mr.T. Sutton Timmis to Wid- nee School Board, at the meeting of that body, on Thursday, is another instance of that gentleman's generous large-hearteduess, and of his deep and practical interest in education. There can be little doubt that the School Board will accept the trust he wishes to impose upon them, because it is weighted with no conditions they can feel it bur- densome to fulfil. "The Timmis Grants" is the natural and fitting title. Tnat the poor students should receive the benefit is a laudable and proper desire, and no one, except perhaps the Vicar of Runcorn, would object to the clause in the deed referring to schools or colleges where religious tests are applied. Mr. Timmis does not look upon the ' deed he has prepared as final ;he is willing to re- ceive and adopt suggestions from the Board on the subject ; which is to receive the consideration of the General Purposes Committee and the Board before it is sealed, signed, and delivered.</p>
        <p>The gift to the town is a noble conclusion to Mr. Timmis'a connection with the School Board, which had existed for se many years. He had, rendered in- valuable aid to the cause of education, as chairman of the Board, for nearly twelve years, and the feel- ings of regret to which expression was universally given at his retirement in October will be intensi- , fled by his last act of noble generosity in the cause ,of education In the'days to come many a youth of poor parents will have reason to thank Mr. Tim- mis for enabling him to take the first step along the path that led to intellectual cultnre and, may be, to fame and fortune.</p>
        <p>The ink of the foregoing paragraphs had scarcely dried ere news arrived of another generous endow- ment of edueation to Widnes, 'Mr. Alderman Gas* sage, the mayor, has Intimated that he will supple- ment the scheme of his partner in business, by founding a scholarship of £6O a year at Univetsity College, 'Liverpool, for the benefit of the scholars of , Widnes. It is understood that this scheme is to be worked in unison with the offer of Mr.Timmis, and the presumption is that the holders of tbe free scholarships at Farnworth Grammar. School, will compete for the higher prize, and the successful student will be enabled by the Gossage scholarship to carry on his studies at , University College. Doubtless Alderman C4ossage will definitely isiake known his intentions in the course of a few days. The gratitude of Widnesians is'due to both gentle. men for their generous munificence in the cause of education in the past and in the present.</p>
        <p>It will not be pleasant news to the ratepayers of Widnes that the precept for £5,000, ordered to be served upon the Town Council by the School Board on Tuesday, will mean an increase of a little over a penny in the .£ on the rates. For some years past the amount taken from the rates for School Board purposes, has not exceeded £4,500, and the increase on the present occasion would not seem likely to be a permanent one. It appears that the fee grant from the Education Department is paid in quarterly instalments, of which the Board only received three during the financial year, thereby accounting for a Ideficit of £4OO in the accounts of the School Man- agtiment Committee. Fr the present year the four instalments will be received, and it may be cenfidently anticipated that the School Board rate will fall to its normal level in 1894. What is not so easily understood is how a mistake could be made in estimating for so fixed an expenditure as salaries.</p>
        <p>From a return submitted to the same Board, the cost of education in Widnes and the results obtained therefrom compare very favourably with any town In the kingdon. The income per scholar in average attendance last year was 13s. ljd. in school pence and 9s. lid, from the rates. The expenditure per scholar was .£2 is. 11411., of which .£1 16s. nd. was for salaries. The grant paid per scholar by Govern- ment was .£1 Os. 6d. There are only four towns where a higher grant was obtained, viz : Bolton, .£1 is. ; Leicester, Li Is. 21.; Brighton, ..£1 Os. ; and Oldham, .21 Os. 61d. In each of these instances, however, the expenditure per scholar was much in excess of Widnes School Board. In Bolton it was £2 Os. 51L; Leicester, ..C2 7s. Old.; Mghton, £2 B§. 5d,; and Oldham, £2 Bs. 3d. In Widnes it was £2 is. hid. There is only one town of the list of 22 submitted, where the amount received from the rates was less than in Widnes, and that was Hull, where the rate amounted to 3s. 30. per scholar, and the expenditure was also low, being £1 us. 41lid The standard of education seemed to be on a similar scale, for the grant paid per scholar was only 175.8 d. Widnesians may con- gratulate themrelves that they have the highest efficency in their Board Schools' at a moderate cost.</p>
        <p>The Vicar of Runcorn took advantage of the Paribh Church tea party on Monday to deliver a long address with a view to showing the importance and necessity of keeping in existence the denomin- ational day schools. Argument was conspicuous by its absence in the oration ; the chief effort was made to induce the audience to believe that the teaching of distinct religious doctrine in the day schools is essential to the prosperity of the country and that any interference with them was a breach of religious liberty. The rev, gentleman made no attempt to prove the fermer,and lamentably failed to establish any foundation for the latter state- ment. Another important purpose of the speech was to arouse the enthusiasm of his hearers to such a pitch as to cause them to open their purses in aid nearly a third of the scholars in attendance are g a school where now eTnhlaartgthe e v. gentleman's views of religious liberty should be peculiar not sur- prising. Ile belongs to a church which has been No fo a n ciOnofj oe re mt s f dominant for centuries, and enjoyed privileges and liberties denied to other sects even to the last half century, and which at the present time derives a large proportion of its princely revenue from the state. The common notion is that the Government grants mode to elementary schools are for teaching reading, writing, arithmetic, and kindred sub- jects and not for inculcating a knowledge of the scriptures as set forth in the cate- chism of any creed or sect, but it is such men as the Bishop of Chester and Canon Wood who wish to perpetuate the present system of de- voting the public funds to what is in a great measure neither more nor less than a proselytising mission. Where would have been religious liberty had the 'Bishop of Chester succeeded in carrying his propo- sals at Moulton I There was not even the semblance of liberty or of fairness in the bishop's scheme.</p>
        <p>Who is there that is not acquainted with the advertisements for masters and mistresses who are ineligible unless of a certain faith, and who must take the organ at church, teach in the Sunday</p>
        <p>School, and perform other duties, for a salary that is provided out of the public funds for teaching the elements of an ordinary education? And men- tion of the Sunday School reminds us that the Vicar was wholly silent on this subject. Is there not such an institution at the National Schools? Are; the lessons given there so powerless to incul- cate a knowledge of religion as taught in the Prayer Book and the 39 Articles, that the whole fabric will crumble away if the . duty of enforcing the lesons there to be learned is not imposed upon often unwilling masters and mistresses, while the ministers of religion stand idly looking on? The Vicar would seem to desire to set up a religious inquisition;at least he laments the fact that teachers, when appointed by School Boards, are not ques- tioned as to their religious principles, but the teachers, through their union, which is yearly growing more powerful, are urging that an inquiry Into their religious faith shall not be permitted at i ill; that their fitness for a situation shall be judged by their educational attainments, and not by the fact that they are Churchmen, Wesleyans, or Unita- rians, or of any of the multifarious sects into which the religious world is split up. How different is their views of religious liberty from the Vicar's; and theirs is certain to prevail in the end. Religion ought to be a matter for the conscience, and not a subject of inquiry by bigoted clerics or prying school managers.</p>
        <p>The Vicar'sdescription of the effects of " denom- inational religion" isone which would not induce the unprejudiced observer, or the " nothingarian " to send his children to a denominational school. Religion is so hot, says the Vicar, that to mention it amongst the educated, and cultured members of a School Board would lead them to quarrelling. We beg the rev. Canon's pardon—to quote bim lit- erally, religion is not mentioned by these gentle- men, because it is too hot and they don't want to quarrel with one another. We presume, to use a homely illustration, the Canon was measuring a peck out of his own bushel; it would be a pity, if true, were all religions so " hot." We shall not have religious liberty until the teaching of creeds and catechisms is relegated to the Sunday School and the House,to paid clerics and voluntary " lay " teachers, and the public elementary scheols are so freed from denomination- alism that all sects and all classes can freely enter without being even under the disagreeable neces- sity of putting in force the conscience clause.</p>
        <p>Is a gratifying fact that Mr. F. Wiswall.Chair- man of Runcorn Commissioners, has succeeded in forming a committee of local gentlemen with the object of carrying out the scheme for erecting a Technical Institute. This Committee held a meet- ing, and has appointed Mr. Wiswall, Mr. P. Speak- man, C.C., and Mr. Taylor, to endeavour to secure donations from gentlemen residing in the town and neighbourhood. A better selection for the pur- pose could not have been made. May success at- tend their efforte.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>IIiISH NATIONAL MEETING AT</head>
        <p>IIiISH NATIONAL MEETING AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>SPEECH BY DR. TANNER M.P.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Michael Devitt Branch of the Irish National League of Great Britain a public meeting was held in the Union Hall, on Tuesday night. The room was crowded, Mr. J. W. Boyle, president of the branch occupied the chair, and be was supported by Dr. Tanner, M.P., Alder- man Williamson, (Widnes) ; Councillor Gough, (Widnes) ; Messrs. T. Lea, Thos. Byrne, John Clark, Wm. Dempsey,Ed ward Kennedy, John Withington, and Patrick Murphy, (district _organiser)._</p>
        <p>The Chairinan'diev"i -attention to Elie work" the Michaei Devitt Branch had accomplished during its existence, and said the league was a political organisation, open to all Irishmen irrespective of their theological opinions all that was required was for the meinberts to accept the belief in the restoration of Irish rights. The Eng- lish people and the Irish people were never joined to- gether in one brotherhood forming the democracy, and they had every confidence that a satisfactory measure of Homfallule would be passed by Mr. Gladstone, and the Radical party. It was hinted that the House of Lerds would put their feet down upon the measure, but it would be advisable for the noble Lords to keep their feet in the proper place, else their pedestal might be knocked from under them. (Applause.)—Alder man Williamson proposed "That we await the approach- ing Parliamentary Session with the utmost hope and confidence • having reliance not alone in the integrity of the Radical Government, and Mr. Gladstone but in their power to carry a ineasuie of Home Rule which will bring peace and contentment to the Irish people." Home limle he said must take precedence of any English re- fins and he could not see why Mr. Gladstone with a . majority of 40 at his back, should not carry his Bill. The only obstacle in the way was the House of Lords, but, if they bad understood aright, Mr. OlecW.olle' latest utterances, he was preparing a measure of Home Rule for the House of Lords which would land them in a worse pickle than ever the Irish party were landed in. (Applause.) Mr. The. Byrne seconded the resolution, and said they had implicit faith in Mr. Gladstone's ability to pass his measure. He attributed the faith that there was a Home Rule Bill at all to the Irish National League, and he hoped that every Irishman in that room would be- come a member of the League that night. (Applause.) Dr. Tanner, M.P., who had a hearty receptieu, dealt at some length with the Clanricarde evictions, stating that Lord Clanricarde was Lerd Salisbury's standard bearer in Ireland, and he characterised Lord Salisbury as a " ruffian " He had been taken to task for hay- ing used that expression at Birkenhead on the previous night, but he did not like nailing. a ruffian, and the Irish members could put up with rebukes provided that they were doing their duty. And their duty lay with the democracy of England. Scot- land, Ireland and Wales. If the Englishmen were helping Irishmen at the present time, Irishmen were grateful and would help the Englishmen to pase the groat reforms which they had needed " One man one vote "was foremost, and then poor Maclure and others would soon go from the House of Commons. (Laughter.) He could point out scores upoe scores of men who simply went into the House of Commons to make an in- decent livelihood out of it. Scores of the great Conser- vatives who talked about the welfare of the people,were all the time paying more attention to their company making and pocketing fees as directors of great com- panies. (Applause.) Tile great motto of the Irish and English ought to be " Advance Demos "—let the people I advance. They wanted workingmen in Parliament— true workingmen. no frauds, men who would really pro- mote the interests of the workingman, (Applause.) He wished those present could sem the conduct of some of the Tories in the House. They called the Irish murderers, but in a few days a little more light would be thrown upon the dynamite outrage in Dublin. (Applause.) All the Tory money was not spent in beer—(applause)— L 5,000 went in one way and £3,000 in another, to try and create a split in the Irish party. The Tories failed in that, and then like the cads they went in for bigotry, intolerance and set up the cry of clerical intimidation. But in spite of all the tactics of the Tory party they would within the next few days have the Queen's speech, and a fortnight later the Home Rule Bill would be introduced into Parliament. A majority of 40 if it were properly worked, was quite as ...God as a majority •of 2. They in Runcorn had a good member in his friend Mr. Brunner. (Applause.) Intensley, whole-hearted, he was always, with his kindliness of manner, with his open purse. trying to do his best for the people whom he served as a member of Parliament. (Applause.) Mr. Brunner had made up his mind that he was always going to he on the spot, and plenty of men like Mr. Brunner would be always on the spot; everyone of the Irish representatives would be constantly there, and the English whips told him there would be abetter attendance in the corning Parliament than ever there was before. (Applause.) No Home Rule Bill would be satisfactory which did not meet with the approbation of the Irish people, but the Irish people loved Gladstone and John Morley, and absolutely trusted the great Liberal Leaders, and they. hoped be- fore very long to see Ireland a nation once again, to see Irishmen able to livA as free men in their own land—to live fairly and honestly, not in furtherence of separation but in furtherance of combination. (Applause.) The resolution was carried unanimously, and a vote of thanks was accorded to Dr. Tanner, on the motion of Mr. Withington, seconded by Mr. Dempsy. A vote of thanks was also accorded to the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>FRODSHA.II.,</head>
        <p>FRODSHA.II.,</p>
        <p>PROPOSED COURT 1101JSE.—A Sub-committee of the Cheshire Standing Joint Committee have recommended the erection, of a Court-house with other offices at the Frodsham Police Station at a cost of £925. WESLEYAN Scrioor..—There was a good attendence on Monday evening to hear an entertainment of song, recitation, and speech from Mr. H. Taylor (police court missionary, Warrington), Mr. Guest, J.P., presided and spoke very highly of the valuable work done in the police courts and homes of the people by the missionaries. Mr. Taylor then gave several numerous temperance songs and recitations, and an address full of witty say- ings and anecdotes, which served to drive home useful lessons. The audience by frequent applause showed how much they enjoyed the various items. At the close a very hearty vote of thanks was given to Mr. Taylor and a cordial invitation to come again soon. The Doxology closed a very happy evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday. Mr. H. Wade Deacon presided and there were present the Revs. Jas. Clarke, and G. H. Garrett, Messrs. B. Brown, D. Lewis, C. B. Barlow, S. Quinn, C. Flynn, G. H. Danby (clerk.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>DISTRICT EDIIC TION.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT EDIIC TION.</p>
        <p>The district EdiTcsaTo-u--Collni.t.tee're. potted that dur- ing the month of Decembef (three weeks) there were 15 applications for school fees, which were recommended to be paid. 150 parents were summoned before the Committee for contravention of the Education Act, and 37 were prosecuted before the magistrates, 35 were fined and two children were sent to industrial schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>SCIIGOL MANAGEMENT.</head>
        <p>SCIIGOL MANAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>On the recommendation of the School Management Committee, the resignation of Miss Latchford, certified assistant, senior girls department, of the Simms Cross Schools, was accepted, and Miss Motley was appointed in her place at a salary of £65 per annum.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee recommended for payment accounts amounting to £670 6s. The receipts for the 'month amounted to £389195.6d.</p>
        <p>THE ESTIMATES. The Finance Committee submitted their estimate of the expenditure of the Board for the year ending Sep- tember 29th, 1893, as follows :—District Education Committee, £l,llB ; Sites and Building Committee, £425 10s. ; Finance Committee, £2,360; balance overdrawn at the bank, £495 18s. 10d, ; total, £5,066 21. 11d. The Committee recommended that a precept be served upon the Town Council of Widnes, for the sum of £5,000 being the estimated amount of the deficiency in the sezool fund up to the 29th of SepteLmber, 1893. The Clerk stated last year the estimates tatalkd £4,478 and a precept was issued for £4,500. Last year the School Management Committee estimated their expenture at £874 whereas they actually spent £1,577, having under- estimated their requirements by £703. The only ex- planation was that nearly £4OO was not received until November, too late to be placed in last year's accounts, and the salaries exceeded what was estimated. The Chairman observed that during this year four fee grants would be received, and it was probable that the require- ment would again be reduced to the usual figure of £4,soo.—The estimates were approved and the recom- mendation passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>COST OF TEACHING.</head>
        <p>THE COST OF TEACHING.</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the Finance Committee submitted a return showing that during the past year the net cost of teaching per hesd of children was 3s. 6p., in Simms Cross School ; Us. 2,td. in Warrington-road school, and Bs. Sid., in West Bank school, giving an average of 7s. nd. per head. The gross cost cl teaching per head at Simms Cross was £1 19s. Bd. ; Warrington-road £2 (4. 8/d. ; and West Bank £2 3s. ; average £2 2s. ed. It was stated that tbose figures compared very favourably with any school board in England and Wales. GENEROUS GIFT BY MR. TIMMS. GRANTS FOR SCHOLARSHIPS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the following letter :—Widnes, Janu- ary 17, 1893, Dear Mr. Deacon,—,As owing to the with- drawal of subscriptions, the Council of Education will not be able to continue the grants of free scholarships in connection with the elementary schools and as I recognised these grants to be the best part of the scheme lam wishful that they should be continued. To accom- plish this lam willing to hand over to trustees £2,580 of consols the interest of which will provide means to carry out the scholarship scheme on the existing lines. The School Board I consider the best possible Board of , Trustees, and on hear jug that it will accept the Trust, I will complete the deed and hand over the consols. Enclosed is a draft deed for the approval of the Board, as it is not drawn by a lawyer, it may require amend- ment so as to be in legal form. lam also open to receive suggestions from the Board not interfering with the general scope of the proposal. I remain yours truly T. Sutton Tim mis—The Chairman Widnes School Board.' The draft deed, which was read, sets forth that the fund is for the purpose of enabling Widnes School Board to make money grants in respect of boys who win the free scholarships at the Farnworth Grammar School. The grants shall be called the "Timmis Grants." The grants shall be made only to parents or guardians of boys who have been educated in one of the public elernentare schools within the township of Widnes, and shall by given in connection with the existing free scholarships of which there are six, each tenable for three years). The grants shall be made as follows, viz., £9 per annum payable monthly to the parent or guardian of each boy who wins a free scholarship, and £1 per annum to the headmaster of the Grammar School for books for each boy. As the income from the consols will probably be in excess of the amount required, the surplus is to ac- cumulate until £35 has been reached, and the School Board in determining how it should be expended, are to bear in mind that the object of the donor is to assist the most capable boys who are just leaving public elemen- tary schools, and whose parents or guardians need help, to obtain a higher education. The School Board are to refuse to award the grants in respect of any boy if in the opinion of the Board the parents or guardians do not need them. Should Farnworth Grammar School cease to exist, or all or any of the free scholarships be discontinued, the School Board shall deal with the in- come as though it were surplus income previously re- ferred to, provided always that no grant shall made in connection with any school or college requiring religious tests of any kind.—The Chairman proposed that the best thanhs of the Board be given to Mr.Timmis for his gene- rous offer, and Mr. Brown seconded the motion, which was carried by acclamation.—On the proposititri Mr. (Brown, seconded by the Chairman, the offer and draft deed wcreferre red to the General Purposes Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH TEA PARTY. TIIE VICAR ON RELIGIOUS EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH TEA PARTY. TIIE VICAR ON RELIGIOUS EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>The annual Congregational tea party was held in the National Schools on Monday evening. About 350 persons sat down to tea. The donors of tables were: the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, Mrs. Charles 11 azleh urst (two), Dr. MacLennan, Mrs. Henry White, the Misses Smith (Stanley Villas), Miss Savage, Messrs. P. Speakman, CO., J. E. Meredith, John Littler Walter Timmins, E. Gregory, W. Bate, W. Crosby, Geo. Moss, T. Nicholls. R. Abel, Isaac Sneakman,J. Leicester W. Broadhurst, W. Sutton, J. H. Hamilton' A. Jordan, E. Imison, W. Whittaker, A. Salkeld, H. Briscoe, W. R. Wass, J. J. W. Potter, J. Bridgewater, C. R. H. Harrison, J. Johnson. and A. Buck. Half tables were contributed by the Rev. J. Holden, Mesdames Gorst, Mason. and Glover ; Messrs. W. H. Rogerson, S. Shone, IL Hampson, J. Ball, M. Barker, and E. Newton. During an interval in the programme the vicar, who presided, delivered a long speech on denominational education. Be said it was a very serious question he wished to pit, namely. why did they like to keep up these denominational, and particularly their Church schools. Of course if they could not get any satisfactory answer to the question he should say, " Let us not trouble may more about it.' But he believed that a very satisfactory answer indeed could be given. People often said that denominational and Church schools were much cheaper to a town than Board schools. But he was not going to say that that night, although he believed it was perfectly true. If they asked him if he wanted to keep up denominational schools to save their pockets, he would say no. The great reason he had, and he believed most thinking Christian people agreed with him, for wishing denomina- tional schools and especially church schools, to flourish was that they were very essential to the religious pros- perity of the country. He was going to say something tor which the Bishop and himself had been taken to task, and he was going to maintain that what his lordship had said, and what he (the chairman) was saying, was founded on common sense, and it was that they not simply wanted to maintain and improve the religious condition of the people, but that they desired to main- tain religious freedom. (Applause.) 'Religious freedom was really the question at issue; it was the whole gist of the matter. Let them suppose for one moment that all the denominational schools of Runcorn were closed, and in place of then they had a large extension of Board schools. At the present moment, out of every ten children who were being educated in the town, five attended the Church of England schools, three the other denominational schools, and the remaining two were taken in the Board schools. That state of things gave liberty Df choice to the people of the town. If a parent wished his child to go to a Church school, a Board school, a Roman Catholic school, or a Wesleyan school, he could send him there; but when Board schools had taken possession of the town that choice was lost, for he could not send his offspring to any but a Board school. What did that mean ? It meant that if he sent his child now to a Church of England school, if he did not object the child could be thoroughly in- structed as a member of the Church of England ; to a Wesleyan school as one of that body, and a Roman Catholic as a member of that particular church ; but if he sent the child to a Board school it would get some religious instruction, but not of any particular sort. 'rhus they would observe if the whole town was covered with Board schools, and he, as a strong Churchman, Wesleyan, or Roman Catholic, had a boy and wanted him to be educated in his faith. he could not secure his desire. It was an impossibility, because they had taken away his liberty. Even if a School Board consisted exclu- sively of Churchmen, and they wished the education to be in accordance with their particular faith, that could not be done. The same remark applied to other denominations. They ought to clearly under- stand that; and that no amount of election or any- thing else could ever enable Board Schools to give any- thing but an undenominational education. Members of the Board in all cases in which he was acquainted very seldom mentioned the subject of religious teaching at all. It was too hot. They did not want to quarrel with one another ; the consequences was that the sub- ject of religious teaching was left to the master and mistress. It was a very good thing if they were good men and women, but when a master and mistress were appointed they were not asked a single question about religion—not a single question. They might be Roman Catholics, Unitarians, Wesleyans, Church people, or Nothingarians, they might have broad views or narrow views. As time went on they would not know what</p>
        <p>they were getting. These were not chimerical things but difficulties that would have to be met in the future. The National Union of Elementary teachers was already agitating that no questions should be asked teachers as to their religious views. He dared say what went on at their Board schools in Runcorn was very good. He was not speaking about that. He desired to emphasize the the fact that if there were only Board schools,- there would be no certainty whatever as to who would be the person giving the iostruction ; and as a Churchman, he should object very strongly to his boy being taught by a man of whom he did not know anything. Other de- nominations would also object, and the result would be that no religious teaching at all 'would be given. The difficulty would come that they would have to nay for it, whether they liked it or not. He should consider it extremely hard that his money should be taken from him by force, to compel him to give aeligious instruc- tion to the children to which he objected. He would say it was a violation of the rights of conscience. It was the tyranny of the majority that he should be com- pelled, as a Churchman, to put his hand into his pocket to pay for religious teaching of which he did not approve. It was what their Nonconformist friends said about the church rates. They con- sidered it was unfair ; that it was not religious liberty. It was tyranny, and the Nonconformists very properly insisted that they should not be taxed to sup- port the teaching of a religion with which they dis- agreed. That was his position. He said it was not fair that Board schools should wipe out of the country all other schools, and then that they should come down upon religious people, and say tbey must pay for the teaching of a religion of which they did not approve. He thought they ought to have various schools in a town, and the people should be able to chose one to which to send their children, and thereby enjoy religi- ous freedom. He did not think the present Govern- ment, or any administration for some time to come, would pass any law which would abolish the element- ary schools as they were now. What he desired was that they as Church people could not allow their eta mentary schools, which were doing admirable work, which wars educating half the town, and in which religion was taught by competent teachers, to suffer. Their first duty was to see that their Church schools— and be would say the same to other denom- inational managers—were maintained, not merely in efficiency, but in admirable order and condition. He wished to indure everyone present so feel that if he had not done anything for their school he would in the future. Be might intimate, as manager of the school, that they had a plan which had received the Govern- ment mark for the enlargement of the girls' school. There . had long been a demand for that. Their plans were authorised, and they were hoping they would be able to carry out the scheme very shortly. That meant a bazaar. (Applause.) Be expected that round of applause meant that everyone was going to work for it. They intended to have it in November' and it was for one of the best possible objects. They had got to do their best to keep those old schools, in which they gathered, in first rate order, and he should rely upon all those present to do their duty best in that matter. (Applause.) During the evening an interesting programme waa submitted, in which the church choir gave several glees. Songs were contributed by Miss Littler, and Miss Sun- kin; Messrs. Pownall, Clare, Caygill, and Branch ; and Master Newton. Duets were given by Messrs. Littler, and Branch and Litter and Gates. Mr. R. W. Pierpoint played a cornet solo, and • a selection was given by Mr. Pierpoint (cornet), Mr. Outhwaite (euphonium), and Mr. Le Couteur (piano.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN FREE PUBLIC LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE PUBLIC LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this committee took place on Wednesday, in the Board-room, Town Hall. Mr. Thomas Riley was in the chair, and there were also present—the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, Messrs. H. J. Grice and J. D. Jones (librarian). The issue of books from the library tor the month of December was 1,925, being an increase over the corres- ponding month of last year of 101. It was reported that Mr. Samuel Taylor had given to The Committee an oil painting by an unknown artist, being a view of Runcorn in 1835, taken from the river. The picture, which is more of a curiosity than a work of art, shows a couple of steam packets which are drawn in a spirited manner, and also several smaller craft, making quite a lively scene. The names of the packets were the Duke and the Eclipse. The old Parish Church, Castle Rock, the Baths, Johnson's chimney, the old Windmill, Norton Priory, and Halton Castle are conspicuous objects in the picture, the artist's desire seeming to be to crowd in Interesting features, regardless ot the law of perspective. The picture has an interest to old inhabitants who remember the town when it was a health resort, long before the construction of the Manchester Ship Canal destroyed the natural features-of the south bank of the Mersey. THE ALLEGED STABBING OF A BOY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The boy Thomas Fisher of Queen-st. surrendered to his bail at Runcorn Police C3urt, on Wednesday, to answer a charge of unlawfully wounding Arthur Royle, aged ten years, with intent to do him gre- vious bodily harm by stabbing him with a knife.— The injured boys' deposition was read over by Mr.. Johnton deputy magiatrates' clerk and Superintendent Atherton applied for a further remand of a week. Mr. Knowles contended that the prisoner ought not to be remanded on the uncorroborated testimony of one boy, and said he could call a number of witnesses who would tell a totally different tale to that given in the deposi- tion.—Dr. MacLennan said he was sorry to say there was very serious risk of blood poisoning being set up, owing to the fact that the water from the left kidney passed through the wound. The boy was very feverish. and very much worse in every respect that morning.— Mr. Brandrit thought the evidence produced was quite sufficient to justify a remand and he remanded the pri- soner until next Wednesday. The amount of bail was rais- ed from two sureties in £5 to two sureties of £lO each. The sureties are Samuel Webster and Thomas Roach. It was intended to. take Royle to Liverpool Infirmary on Wednesday afternoon but his condition was so serious that It was thought inadvisable to remove him.</p>
        <p>AGED PERSON'S TREAT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The annual treat to the aged people of Runcorn, took place in Runcorn Public Hall, on Wednesday evening, and proved as highly enjoyable as in former years. The arrangements had been carried out by a large and representative committee, of which Mr. F. Wiswall was chairman,and every pro- vision was made for the comfort and convenience of the aged guests. 550 invitations had been issued, but through sickness and other causes, nearly 150 were pre- vented from attending. Of 417 who made an appear- ance about two thirds were wpmen. The 'females also carried off the honours as regards age. One old lady named Mary Robinson, of Lord-street, had attained the age of 85 years, and she was closely followed by Martha Dugdale, Hatton-ropd, 84 ; Ann Whitley, Clarence Ter- race, 81 ; and Mary Jones, Chaucer-street ; Mary Pugh, Princess-street. 82 ; and Betsy Hewitt, OFeenway-pad, 140. The oldest male guests were Wm. Williams, Storm-street, and Thos. Barker, Union-street, each of whom had seen 80 winters pass by. There were numerous guests who had passed the allotted span of three-score years and ten; Francis Edwards, of Rutland-street, aged 89 years, was too .111 to atten 1. The "treat "consisted of a " hot pot" dinner followed by sandwiches, fancy Dreads, mince tarts. &amp;c., and the wants of the hungry donors were attended to by a num- ber of ladies, wives of the members of the Codimittee, and numerous gentlemen helpers, and the whole passed off satisfactorily. Tea was also served, and afterwards the old gentlemen were provided with packets of tobacco, and the old ladies with packets of tea. Oranges &amp;c., were also distributed. When the tables were cleared, an entertainment was given under the presid- ency of Mr. Wiswall, who opened the proceedings wits a brief and appropriate speech. To his mind that meeting was unique, proper, sensible, and right. Be was glad to notice that throughout the country, and even among the members of the Government, the question of how to deal with the aged persons was being discussed. The question of how to give pensions to aged persons would soon come under the consideration of Parliament. A sign of the improve- me..t of the times was that prisons were being emptied while others were being pulled down, and he next wished to see the Workhouses empty. (Applause.) He had a perfect horror of Workhouses, and he was sure most of those present had. Nor did he like the name pauper. In France he believed there were no such places ; at any rate the local authorities kept their own poor, and to a large extent within their own homes. Why should they In England no do the same ? He was sure they would excuse his introducing the word poor and pauper, but he did not employ the term as having any reference to that gathering. rTb behalf They el fewopecar the ht party, Committee. pleased to tlel e n l t leadership theirhauipse. 0 guests, and he desired to say 'how happy he was to welcome The P of Mr. James Littler, contributed several glees; songs were rendered by Mrs. Temperley, and Messrs. Branch, J. Leathwuod, and R. Morris, and Mr. Cyril Chantler gave a recitation. Professor Miller gave a clever yen- . tnloquoil Punch and Judy entertainrnent,which delighted , the crowded audience. During the evening the volun- teer band, under the conductorship of Mr. Duthwaite„ ) performed selections of music.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>HELSBY.</head>
        <p>HELSBY.</p>
        <p>--- ONCERT AT THE WESLEYAN SCHOOL.-011 behalf of this school a very enjoyable concert was given on Friday night, last week, by the friends and members of the band connected with the Seamen's Institute, Runcorn. There was a good attendance. The band gave five selec- tions, all of which were greatly appreciated. Songs were contributed by Misses Leathwood and Douglas, Messrs. Burton and Fearnett. Recitations were given by Miss Rogers and Mr. Shaw. A duet on the flute was played in an excellent manner by Messrs. Mack and Clays ; Mr. W. Byfield gave two readings ; and a sketch, "The Stupid Servant," by Misses Douglas and Waterworth4 created amusement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WIDNES PETIT SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES PETIT SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TauasDAY..—Before Messrs. F. H. Gossage and 3. Birehall.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS. - John Hughes, Choi:7loodeley-at.; Owen Mularky, New-st. ; and Thomas O'Neil, Peel-st., were fined ft,r this offence.</p>
        <p>CIIINNEYS ON FlRlL—Joseph Holland, Victoria-at., and Thomas Raton, Mersey-road, were fined is. and costs each for allowing the chimneys of their dwellings to be on fire.</p>
        <p>THEFT OF CoAL.—John Dunnigan Sankey-at., was charged with stealing a quantity of coal, valued at 2d., the property of the United Alkali Company.—The mother of the boy stated that she had sent her boy to gather cinders. He had never brought coal home.—Mr. Birchall said the parents were more to blame than the children.— Defendant was ordered to receive six etrokes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>CONVICTION OF A ST. HELENS PITBLICAN QUASHED.</head>
        <p>CONVICTION OF A ST. HELENS PITBLI- CAN QUASHED.</p>
        <p>APPEAL CASE AT THE LIVERPOOL QUARTER SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool County Quarter sessions, on Thursday, Mr. Hugh Perkins presiding, the appeal of James Woolfall, licensed victualler, of the Prince of Wales Hotel, Ormskirk-st., VVindle, St. Ilelens, against a conviction under the licensing acts by the borough magistrates, came on for hearing. Mr. M'Connell, appeared for the respondent magistrates Messrs. F. IL Dixon-Nuttall and others, and the appellant was represented by Mr. Mulholland and Mr. Swift. Mr. M'Connell opened for the respondents, and said they were five in number. On the lfth October, they convicted the appellant of having on the 12th of that month permitted drunkenness to take place on his licensed premises, and fined him 40s. and costs, but did not endorse his licence. The facts on which they con- victed were these. A few minutes after ten o'clock in the evening of Wednesday, 12th October, a police-con- stable saw a man named Patrick Henebery enter the hotel in a drunken condition, and informed Sergeant Strong and another Sergeant of the fact. These two Officers entered the house and found Henebery in the parlour with several others. Henebery was in a palpa- Ve ARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. ble state of drunkenness. The landlord was standing SarcanaT.—Before Mr. Burgess (in the chair), and Mr. close by, laughing with the others at something Hens- Voisey. bery had been saying. Before Henebery was a glass Ass INCORRIGMLB YOUNG Girm.—Ada Williams,a young containing gin, and while the landlord denied that it girl, daughter of a Ship Canal labourer living in Warbur- was his, Henebery claimed it. Henebery after leaving ton-street, was charged with having stolen, on the 10th the hotel went to the police station to complain about Inst., a waistcoat, valued at 55., and on the 12th a pair the matter, and the officer who there saw him, believed of slippers, valued at 25., both from the shop of Joseph him to be drunk, and subsequently he was seen by Dr. Bullock, Winwick-street.—The complainant state