      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>_14,W5</head>
        <p>_14,W5</p>
        <p>PROCLAMATION.</p>
        <p>£2O Reward.—Whereas it has come to our know- ledge that certain unscrupulous person or persons have lately bees offering for sale and passing off Quinine Bitters not ot our manufacture, and have endeavoured when GwILTM EVANS' QUININE Brrrens has been asked for, to substitute and pass off other preparations.</p>
        <p>The above Reward will be paid to anyone who will give such information as will enable us to ensure conviction in any case of passing off or substitut- ing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WARNING!</head>
        <p>WARNING!</p>
        <p>We also desire to warn the Public to be on their guard,and see the name "GWILYM Evass' Brrrzes " on each bottle and label.</p>
        <p>For further PROTECTION each bottle has the Government Stamp, wit', the name " GWILYM EVANS, F.C.S., MPS., LLANELLY," engraved thereon,—to imitate which is FORGERY.</p>
        <p>Signed, QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING</p>
        <p>CO., LD., LLANELLY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>INSOLENT VAGRANTS.</p>
        <p>Three strong stalwart men, named Patrick Linch, Thomas Burnes, and Henry Brown, were charged before the Bench on Wednesday, with vagrancy. Constable Barlow and another officer had observed the men the previous evening entering shops in Buttermarket-street. They carried a few laces in their hands an made a pre- tence at hawking, but in reality they were begging, and when refused assistance by either the shopkeepers or people in the streets they swore and used abusive lan- guage, especially to females. The Chief Constable said that when the men were brought to the police station they conducted themselve9 insolently.—Prisoners were eemmitted to jail for seven days each.</p>
        <p>HE WANTED TO DROWN HIMSELF FOR.BRAVADO</p>
        <p>At the Borough Magistrates' Court, on Monday morn- ing, Alfred Brown, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in St. James-st., on Saturday night. A con- stable said he had his coat and hat off on the bridge and wanted to throw himself cver and drown himself. He was mad with drink and very violent. The constable said he believed he would have drowned himself if he had not been prevented.—The Chairman said he passed at the time and thovight someone was daring the man to do something. He was very drunk and said "Will you dare me to do it ? "—The prisoner's wife said her husband was dared by somebody to throw himself over. His hat went over Arst.—The prisoner promised to start a new life, and was let off on the payment of the lock- up fee 25. - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>HUSBAND WHO DIDN'T _WANT TO GO</head>
        <p>A HUSBAND WHO DIDN'T _WANT TO GO</p>
        <p>_ John Plunkett, a labourer, residing at 36 Old Road,thd model lodging-house, was summoned to show cause why Ise should not be sent to prison in default of paying the sum of £2 10s., to his wife which had become due to her an a maintenance order. "He has not given me a penny since he went to jail last November" said the woman "and he goes mooching about lodging-houses and public-houses instead of working." Defendant said he could not show much cause why he should not be sent to jail unless it was because he didn't want to go and bad no money. Be considered, however, that as he and his wife wore both young it was a kind of shame that they couldn't live together.—The Chairman said he thought so too, but as the defendant had not got the money he had better go and live by himself for a month is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>SANGUINARY INCIDENT IN THE CELLS.</head>
        <p>A SANGUINARY INCIDENT IN THE CELLS.</p>
        <p>Catherine Brown was charged at the borpugh police court, on Monday, with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-st. on Sunday night. She appeared with her head bandaged, and she stated that she had fallen and hurt her head before she was brought to tho station.— Inspector Spinks, however, said he found her in the cell where she was confined, lying on the floor, with her head in a corner as if she had fallen against it, and a pool of blood beside her. If she had been bleeding be- fore she was brought in, there would have been blood OG the flags, but there was none.—The constable con- Corned in the case said he had examined the shawl she had on her head when brought in, and there was no blood on it.—The prisoner The shawl is full of blood. I fell before I was locked up at all.—She was sent to jail for 12 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MIN 0 R CASES.</head>
        <p>MIN 0 R CASES.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>SATUNDAT.—Before Colonel McCorquodale (chairman), and Messrs. J. H. Gillespie, F. Gooch, F. W. Pearson and T. D. Grimke-Drayton. DRUNKIINITESS.—Patrick Nealy, and James Ashall, were fined ss. and costs, and Thos. Bailey 2s. 6d. and costs, for being drunk at Newton. CNIUNEY ON Fimg.—John Watkinson, Newton, for having his chimney on fire on the 23rd ult., was fined 38. 86.</p>
        <p>1 and James Runcorn, of the Liverpool Militia, was sent I to gaol for one month's imprisonment, for making false attestation on enlisting. DRUNICENNESS.—Henry Hall, a tanner, of Mersey-st., was fined ss. and costa for drunkenness, on the previous day. IMPRISONED FOk THREEPHNNY-WORTH OF COAL. Joseph Granger, an ironworker of Bewsey, was charged with entering doe works at Bewsey Forge, and stealing a quantity of coal, value 3d. Pearson and Knowles's private policeman, Lawrence, apprehended the prisoner as lie was leaving the company's yard with the coal in his pockets. He said the man had been discharged for laziness.—Fined ss. and costs, or seven days' imprison- ment. He went to gaol. THIIRSDAY.—Before Mr. James Fairclough (in the chair) and Captain Ridgway. WAITING FOR MILITARY EsconT.—Henry Jackson, a deserter from H.M. Loyal North Lancashire Regiment, stationed at Londonderry who was apprehended at Orford Barracks on Wednesday, was sent to Strange- ways to await military escort. A TRULY "ROUGH CIISTOMBR."—CharIes Brown. a Ship Canal navvy, of Acton Grange, was charged with refusing to quit the licensed premises of Mr. J. Critch- ley. landlord of the Higher Angel Inn, Buttermarket-st., end he was further charged with assaulting Miss Susan Critchley.—Mr. A. Browne appeared for the prosecution and stated that both offences were committed on the previous night. The prisoner went into the tap room of the Higher Angel in an intoxicated condition, and whilst Miss Critchley was serving beer to some- men there, prisoner used a considerable amount of obscene and abusive language to her. She ordered him out ; but he refused to go. She said" If you don't go out I shall call someone to put you out." Thereupon prisoner ran at her and knocked ger down. She got up and prisoner ran at her again and felled her to the ground with a blow on the chest. She would have I.een farther abused had not atian named William Moran come to 'the rescue. A constable was called and Brown was given into custody.—Prisoner pleaded guilty, but said he did not know what he was doing.--,-Mr. Critchley said he did not think prisoner did it " out of spite ;" it was the result of his " bringings up."—A fine of ss. for each offence was inflicted. Two WELL-KNOWN CHARACTERS.—EIIen Royle, and Ellen Davies, two well-known characters were sent to jail for one month for indecent behaviour in Academy- street the previous night. FRlDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Alderman Wright, Councillor H. Roberts, and Mr.J.W.Hayward. EMBEZZLEMENT.—Themas Dale, a young man,of Earles- town, was charged with embezzling various sums of money to the extent of about £6O. from Mr. James Hallwood, grocer, Wilderspool-road. Toe evidence showed that prisoner was a carter in the employ of Mr. Hallwood. He had to deliver goods, receive the pay- ments, and receipt them, and enter them in a book at the shop every evening. On Christmas day he did not go to feed the horse as he ought to have done, but when he did not turn up the next two or three days Mr. Hallwood instituted inquiries and found the prisoner had been receiving payments from a large num- ber of customers and systematically avoiding entering them. The prisoner was found to hive left the town. Information was given to the police and D.S. Monaghan traced prisoner to Manchester and other towns' but missed him. It was found out that he had gone to Lon- don, and the Scotland-yard authorities were apprised of the matter and the prisoner was arrested whilst trying to enlist.—The prisoner, who pleaded guilty to the chargetwas sent to jail for two months and fourteen days,</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. Mortnarßefore Mr.. C. Reynolds and Dr. Steele.</p>
        <p>FURIOUS DRIVING.—John ArChur Clough, son of 'rhos. Clough, butcher, Church-st., was charged with having on the 7th January furiously driven a horse and carriage along Church-st., endangering the lives and limbs of the passengers thereon. P.C.'s 190 and 105 proved the case. They stated the defendant. was, driving at from ten to twelve miles an hour, and three children had a narrow escape from being run over.—The defendant said he had only started from the Farmers' Association and he was only travelling from seven to eight miles ad hour. There was snow on the ground. —The father said the pace was not more than five to six miles an hour.—Mr.Reynolds said this was a very daring practice, and the defendant was fined is. and costs 12s:6d.</p>
        <p>SLEEPING Orrr.—Geo. Davies, Thos Philips,and Joseph Arnuth, were charged with sleeping in a hut, on the Ship Canal Works. Davies, an idler, was sent to seven days' hard labour, and the others, who were navvies, were discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 21, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 21, 1893.</p>
        <p>spectators was raised to a high pitch, especially on the call of time, when the smart pair of half backs (Bate and Lally) passed finely. and Barnes finally throwing across to Pats the latter got in near the corner, Turner failing at a difficult place. ilalf-time score—Warrington, 0-1-2; Mossley, 0-0-0. Barnes responded to the restart in capital style, and. Warrington had the best of the opening items. Gradually the visitors worked their way into Warrington territory, and after some feeble attempts to negotiate something tangible, Barnes exe- cuted one of his old-time runs along the popular touch. Bate followed with another. whilst Lawless, after clear- ing all opposition, slipped, and a capital chance was lost. The game was of a give-and-take nature, each goal being in turn assailed, hut a sensational run by Lally near the close almost had a desired effect, the safe tackling of Byran accounting for Lally's inability to score. Final—Warrington, 0-1-3; Mosaic'', 0-0-1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>RUGBY.</head>
        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>Si. Hamm Y. ASPIII.L.-At St. Helens, before about 3,000 spectators. The Saints won the toss, and at once began to press. Aspull was penalised for handling in a scrimmage, but Foulkes. failed at the place kick. Splen- .. . .</p>
        <p>did pass:Tug' between Gill, Cross, and g-udlow resulted in the latter scoring. Foulkes failed to convert. Brown- bill and Cross each scored tries for the Saints. Foulkes failing on both occasions. Half-time score :—St. Helens three tries. Aspull nil. In the second half Cross again distinguished himself, gaining 2 tries. Gill and Johnson also got over. Final result :—St. Helens 7 tries, Aspull nil. ORFORD CRESCINTS v. PAIN:WM INSTITITTE.-011 Satur- day, for the second time this season, the Orford Cres- cents succeeded in defeating Padgate Institute, the match being played on the ground of the latter. The game was evenly contested, but in the first half the visitors gained a strong lead, two goals being kicked by J. Thompson, who played a fine game, and one by P. Ball. In the second portion of the game, the homasters rushed the ball through and the game ended—Orford Crescents, three goals ; Padgate Institute, one goal. The victorious team was as follows :—Goal, R. Jones ; full- backs, J. Jones and F. Trigger; half backs, F. Hogan, W. &amp;stern, and E. Whitham ; forwards, W. Ball, P. Ball, R. Higham, J. Thompson, and C. Lane.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS RECREATION V. BROUGHTON RANGERS.— At Broughton, before a moderate attendance. PiMing- ton wag away from the Recreation side, whilst Hopkins was off for the Rangers. The home captain won the toss, and Seddon kicked off, but the ball came back. Ellison missed getting tho ball, but Mees saved. The Rangers now pressed, and forced the first minor point. The home team had the advantage of a free kick, but failed to improve their position. Ellison should have placed a goal from a fair catch, but did not. Then Seddon and Matthews were instrumental in taking play to the Rangers' lities, Fielden scoring. Seddon misted at goal. Hacking, the Rangers' full back, next got hold, and with the aid of the wind drepped a clever goal. Soon after Thomas scored, a goal resulting. Half- time score :—Rangers 2 goals, Rezreation 1 try. Final result :—St. Helens Recreation 1 goal, 2 tries, Brough- ton Rangers 2 goals (one dropped).</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD T. WARRINGTON RANGERS.—PIayed at Latchford, on Saturday. Deakin kicked off for the home team against the wind, Clarke got away, and passed to Ashton, who ran well up the field, but was collared by Starkey near the use. Play con- tinued at the home 25, where Massey dropped at goal, scoring a minor. Thornton dropped out, putting the ball in touch well in the visitors' half, but the ball was soon taken to the home quarters again. A free kick was given to Latchford, but the ball was soon back under their goal, and after some give-and-take play Massey ran in fbr the Rangers, the kick at goal failing. Latchford now improved and rushed the ball into the Rangers' 25, where it remained some little time ; eventu- ally, the Rangers took the ball under the Latchford goal, where they were awarded a free kick, but Clarke failed at the easy place. Latchford now bad another turn, and took the ball to the Rangers' 25, where Deakin se- cured from a line out, and dashing through his oppo- nents scored a try, but from some cause or other the whistle went, and the ball had to be scrimmaged at the Rangers' 25. Shortly after the whistle sounded for half time, the score being—Rangers, one try ; Latchford, nil. The Rangers kicked off, and play settled down at half way, but with a grand rush Latchford took the ball well into the Rangers' half and kept them busy defend- ing their line. Eventually Hughes secured,and dodging through his opponents in fine style, placed the ball over the line near goal, Starkey failing at the place. Both teams played a very determined game after this, but try as they would, neither side could score again, and when the whistle sounded the result of a hard-fought game was a draw—Latchford, one try ; Rangers, one try.</p>
        <p>I WARRINGTON BREWERY AND THE PARISH CHURCH.— These teams met on Saturday, on the Brewery ground at Arpley, before a good number of spectators. The game opened with the homesters facing the breeze, and by smart following up from the kick-off they began to press. The leather was not allowed to remain in the visitors' quarters long, for some combined play by the Parish backs, abed by neat dribbling on the part of the forwards, saw the game taken to the other end. Try as they would the home team could not make headway, and it was not long before Mason broke through the defence,and scored. Carey and Welsby were prominent immediately after, and the latter was only just pushed into touch on the line, after a brilliant run half the length of the field. Not to be denied, the Churchites attacked in strong fashion, and Gallop scored the second try, Lawton placing his side further ahead by landing a goal. Welsbv was next to score after some pretty passing by Mason, Garrity, and Carey, and at the call of half-time the visitors led by a goal and two tries to nil. The second half was a little more even, the Churchites evidently easing up a little. Carey was very prominent, and Daniels had hard lines after a sprigt along the touch line. Bolesworth was called on for 'al- most the first time, and was not found wanting, and from a scrimmage in the home half Garrity got away, and,. baffling Noon, he transferred to Mason, and the movement being well assisted by Carey and Welsby, the latter succeeded in dashing over. The Brewery played up in desperate fashion after this, but their attacks , were soon staved off. Mason had to retire close on time ' owing to an injury, but, despite this, the Churchites more than held their own,and when the whistle blew for time the score stood—Parish Church. 1-3-2 ; Brewery, 0-0-2, or 11 points to nil. Referee. Mr. S. Wright. Lmfaroof. V. RuNconfs.—These teams met at Aig- burth on Saturday, before a moderate company of spectators. The sides were not at their best as far as representation was concerned, for Melly's services had ' to be dispensed with, and on the other side 0. Hughes was an absentee ; but a capital substitute was found in the person of Morris, who wa3 lately identified with the Tuebrook fifteen. The ground was not in good con- dition. The Runcornians opened the play, and follow- ing the return Houghton was tested, but he easily cleared, following which the visitors fairly took up the running, and Morris by smart following up crossed the home lines, but the kick at goal was a failure. A tem- porary stay in the Runcorn quarters was the next item, when the visitors again made tracks to the line, and registered try No. 2, which was also uncinverted. Fol- lowing this point the play was fairly even, but the visi- tors again got under weigh, and taking the ball on the loose, severely tested the Liverpool defence, Allen being a conspicuous figure in stemminc, the raid. Eventually Crompton ran in a try, but failed to convert. Bracing themselves up, the Aigburthians got well down, and from a smart pass by Owen, W. B. Stoddart dropped , successfully at goal. Half time was called shortly after- I wards,when the score stood—Runcorn,3 tries (6 points); Liverpool, 1 dropped goal (4 points). Central play characterised the opening stages of the second portion of the game. Houghton was called upon, but was not I to be trifled with ; and then the Runcorn forwards got away in a line, and but for the smart play of Allen would undoubtedly have scored. Keeping up a steady pressure,Crompfon at length got possession and dropped a capital goal. Restarting, the home forwards put in some neat foot work and reached the visitors' 25, but fine running by Crompton sent them back to their quar- ters • and after a minor point had been recorded,Walton crossed the line without a resulting goal. This was the last point scored, Runcorn winning a moderate game by 1 goal and 4 tries (12 points) to 1 goal (4 points).</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A " T. MOSSLRY " A."—With dis- appointment attending the senior Warrington club in their first-class engagement is ith Mossley, at Mossley, additional interest was lent to the" A" teams match at Wilderspool enclosure when it became known that several of the most prominent players from both clubs would officiate in the junior match. There would be over 2,500 persons present, and when the teams emerged from the pavilion and" Smack "Bate was seen doing duty for the homesters a ringing cheer welcomed this clever half-back. The ground was in capital order and Davies commenced hostilities with a good breeze at his hack. Faulty play by Ingham missed a capital chance, a mull by Pickford not being taken advantage of. Superior combination in the forward ranks gave the Mossleyites a footing well within the home quar- ters, and all Boscow's resources were required to keep his lines intact. Passing by Bate to Lally, and indivi- dual runs by Barnes and Potts changed the venue, and Ashworth; with all his old skill, just managed to keep his opponents at bay. Again the Mossley forwards headed by Cunningham and Marshall, showed their supremacy, and reached the home 25, but Boscow was not found wanting, and, getting the better of some exchange kicking with A.shworth, the home custodian reached the Mossley quarter-flag. Hampson came through a pack in clever style, and with Davies, Turner and Monks, as attendants, the outlook was indeed ominous, but luckily Bryan cleared in fine style. Ashworth and Pickford improved Moss- ley's position with judicious kicking, and Boscow and Barnes each in turn were applauded for i smart play, which resulted n another raid on the visitors' goal. Flampson failed at the finishing touch, and from this mistake Pickford got clear, and Warring- ton fell back on their lines. Barnes and Potts passed well, and once more the home star was in the ascen- dency, and following this came some exciting transfers on-the Warrington line, but still the coveted rut could not be crossed. Eventually Cunningham cleared, and the running being taken up by Ashworth and Wrigley, the homesters had an anxious time of it. Bate got beautifully away from a pack, and, passing to Barnes, the latter dallied a trifle too long, and Lawless had but little chance afterwards. A minor next followed, and the homesters maintaining the attack, thrice Barnes, Bate, and Potts were within an ace of scoring. The game did not lack in pace, whilst the excitement of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET CLUB V. OWENS COLLEGR.— Played at Manchester, on Saturday. Both teams were fairly strong, and a very close and exciting game re- sulted. Owens College kicked off against the Wind, but the Cricket Club pressed, and about ten minutes from the start Reid scored the first goal for them. F. William- son shortly after added a second. The College then got away, and their right wing made a good run and scored. Again the Cricket Club pressed, and Reid scored, and in return the College scored again, a mistake ou the part of one of the Cricket Club full backs having let them in. Each side scored another goal before half- time was called. After restarting the College went off with a rush, and scored three times in quick succes- sion. They tried, however, after this, and the Cricket Club, playing up well, scored twice, G. Williamson, and F. Williamson kicking the goals. Nothing further was scored, so the game ended in a draw-6 each. RUNCORN V. WARRINGTON Sr. ELPIIIN'S.This match was played on the Runcorn ground before a good num- ber of spectators. The teams turned out as follows : Runcorn : Mainwaring, goalkeeper ; Hughes and Fisher, full back ; Coombes, Richards, and Bate half backs; Limb, A. Limb, Pie, Wright, and Blower, forwards. St. Elphin's : Bates, goalkeeper ; Marshall and Taylor,full backs ; Whittle, Pheenix, and Clayton, half backs; Colley, Hickin, Gotham, Scarisbrick, Williams,, and Hatton forwards. The ground proved hard and slippery, and the ball frequently went into touch, which some- what spoiled the combination, though otherwise the teams were very evenly matched,and a fast and interest- ing game resulted. For some time from the kick-off the visitors pressed, but their effort e were fruitless, and the ball travelled back to the other end, where some lively play was seen. he visitors, however, came away with a rush,and Williams scored with a somewhat lucky shot, out of Mainwaring's roach. Nothing further was scored before half time. After restarting the homesters made tracks towards their opponents' goal, and Limb I centreing well, Pie put the ball promptly between the posts. The home defence was then sorely tried, but Hughes and Richards cleared finely, and Blewer carried the ball along the touch line at a tremendous pace. Numerous corners resulted, from which two further goals were added to the home score by Pie and Blower respectively, Limb placing the corner kicks most credit- ally. The visitors tried hard to draw level, their for- wards playing finely. but no further score accrued, and the gameended—Runcorn, three goals; Warrington St. Elphin's, one. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>CHANGE OF MINISTRY IN EGYPT.</head>
        <p>THE CHANGE OF MINISTRY IN EGYPT.</p>
        <p>PROTEST BY GREAT BRITAIN.</p>
        <p>CAIRO, Turanev.—The excitement regarding the re- construction of the Ministry and the appointment of Fakhri Pasha as Premier continues unabated, both in Cairo and the provinces. Lord Cromer, British Minister visited the Khedive, and Informed his Highness that the British Government expected to be consulted by the Egyptian Government on all important steps, and especially in regard to changes in the Ministry. His Excellency further declared that the British Government were quite unable to sanction the appointment as Premier of Fakhri Pasha, which they regarded as most undesirable. Lord Cromer will pay a second visit to- morrow to the Khedive, who held consultations to-day with Nubar Pasha and Riaz Pasha. With regard to the international aspect of the present situation, it is be- lieved that neither Russia nor France is giving or has promised any support to the Khedive in connection with the action taken by his Highness.</p>
        <p>The difficulty with Egypt is at an end. The Khedive on Wednesday, informed Lord Cromer, the British re- presentative, that he was prepared to appoint Riaz Pasha Premier, instead of Fakhri Pasha, and his Lord- ship assented to this proposal. M. Develle, the French Minister or Foreign affairs, was questioned on this matter in the Chamber, on Wednesday. He said that nothing which affected the independence of the Egypt- ian Government could be matter of indifference to France, or indeed to the whole of Europe, and M. Wad- dington had been instructed to see the British Minister for Foreign affairs on the subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>, – A WOMAN IN MALE ATTIRE.</head>
        <p>, - A WOMAN IN MALE ATTIRE.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning, in Vienna, a policeman ar- rested a" young man " who could not give a satisfac- tory explanation as to where he got a cask that he was rolling along. At the police station it was discovered that the thief was really a woman—Mary Gundinger by name, agea thirty-four. She confessed that when her brother died, fourteen years ago, she changed clothes with him, gave her own documents up as those of the dead, and with his papers, calling herself Joseph, managed to get more work and a better living than would have been possible had she continued a woman. At her lodgings was a girl who called herself the pri- soner's sweetheart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>REMARKABLE CASE OF ORGANIC MIS-</head>
        <p>REMARKABLE CASE OF ORGANIC MIS-</p>
        <p>PLACEMENT.</p>
        <p>When MoHere made his physician say "We have changed all that," because his attention was called to the fact that he had placed the human heart on the right-hand side of the body, it was thought he was 'Jok- ing fun at a statement in a learned publication of the period which dealt with a remarkable case of the kind alleged ,to have been discovered. Quite recently, it seems, a genuine case has cropped up. A gentleman of independent means, named De la Saviniere„ died the other day quite suddenly in Paris, in the Rue Saint Louis-en Pile. Dr. Descouts, who performed a post- mortem examination, was astounded to find the heart, liver, and other organs on the wrong side, according to all accepted notions on the subject. The news got spread abroad, and the "Temps," the gravest, perhaps of all the French papers, sent an interviewer to question the doctor upon the subjoct, The latter fully confirmed the truth of the story. The dead man, he said, in spite of his little peculiarities of construction, had lived to the good old age of eighty-five years.</p>
        <p>Fran I FISH! FISH I—Cheat) baskets of fresh fish or oysters sent anywhere, carriage paid, direct from the landing stage. at 2s. 6d. 33, 43, 5s and upwards. Try them! All orders and remittances to the Co-Operative Fish Supply Co. Ltd., Pontoon, Grimsby. Thousands of testimonials from all parts of the oountry.—Ado.</p>
        <p>CLAIM FOR ,65,000 AGAINST THE SALT</p>
        <p>UNION.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. Justice Bruce on Thursday, in the Queen's Bench Division, the case of Delamere v. Salt Union came on for hearing. This was an application by the Right Hon. Augustus Emily Baroness Delamere and Leopold Richard Sevmonr, owners of land in Over, Cheshire, to claim £85,000 damages for breaches of covenant and for acts of trespass. The plaintiff's land was leased to the defendants,who agreed to fence off the land from other lands belonging to the plaintiffs,a.nd also to maintain and repair all building and erections on the land. Mr. Nicoll, Q.C., who appeared for the plaintiff, said that he agreed with counsel for the decendants (Mr. Waiton, Q.C.). to leave the matter to arbitration. His lordship consented to this.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NATIONAL LIBERAL FEDERATION.</head>
        <p>THE NATIONAL LIBERAL FEDERATION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting in connection with the National Liberal Federation commenced at Liverpool on Thurs- day, when the fifteenth report of the Committee was presented to the Council. The report-commences with a reference to the satisfactory results of the general elec- tion, especially in Wales, where the overwhelming Liberal majority has clearly justified the action of the Federation in putting Disestablishment for Wales in the forefront of the Liberal programme, and constitutes an unanswerable argument in favour of attention to the question at the earliest possible moment. Referring to the results of the election petitions, the Committee state that in some constituencies offences were passed over in a manner which surprised many, and seems to call for a revision of the Corrupt Practices Act or a different n3ethocl of procedure in the trial of petitions. The Com- mittee regard the administrative reforms whieh have been carried out since Parliament rose as in thorough accordance with Liberal principles. As to the appoint- ment of magistrates, the Committee regard with satis- faction the course pursued, which has resulted in a num- ber of Liberals, and especially working men, being placed on the borough commissions .of the peace. The appointments recently made clearly indicate the anamalous distinction between the urban and county magistracy-. robe a Liberal and a Nmconformist is at present an effective bar to participation in the adminis- tration of justice in the county districts. In the ap- pointment of county magistrates two reforms are urgently needed—the abolition of the present qualifica- tions, and the selection of justices by the Lord Chan- cellor on his own initiative. The Committee also strongly urge the necessity of reform in registration law, and conclude by saying that the Liberal party being united and having faith in its leaders, all that re- mains now is to secure that those reforms which the Federation has during the last few years demanded re- peatedly. and of which Mr. Gladstone categorically , expressed his approval at Newcastle, shall be forthwith carried into execution.</p>
        <p>The annual conference of the National Liberal Federa- tion was opened in the Picton Lecture Hall in Liverpool, on Thursday, under the presidency of Dr. Spence Wat- son, of Newcastle, who was re-elected president. The attendance of delegates and members of Parliament was large. Dr. Spence Watson said the party had remitted to Ministers the measures which it was necessary to carry,and they had every confidence that the Newcastle programme would be embodied in the Government pro- posals. Home Rule was the first point, and he had no doubt the measure to be introduced would be a broad, generous, and honpurable attempt to solve the great question on lines which would be sasisfactory not only to Irishmen,but to Englishmen as well. Mr. T.E. Ellis, M.P., said the duty of the Federation was to keep the Govern- ment up to the mark, and bring the House of Lords to its knees. A resolution expressing confidence in Mr. Gladstone and the Government was unanimously carried Among the speakers was the Rev. C. A. Berry, Mr. Wm. Harris, Mr. W. H. Paul. M.P., Mr. W. Smith, M.P., Mr. J. Williams Benn, M.P., Mi. J. Lawrence Gane, M.P., and Mr. Naoroji, M.P. In the evening a banquet was given at the Reform Club by the Chairman, Mr. 0. I. Van Wart, when Dr. Spence Watson, replying to the toast of the Liberal Federation, expressed the hope that all would live to see Liverpool as Liberal in politics as in many other things. A crowded demonstration "vas also held in Hope Hall, under the presidency of Mr. Thomas Gee, J.P., Den- bigh. The Chairman, Mr. T. E. Ellis, M.P., Mr. Lloyd George, M.P., and others delivered energetic speeches against the continued existence of the State Establish- ment of the English Church in Wales, and a resolution in this sense was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>AGED PERSONS' TREAT AT ESTON.</head>
        <p>AGED PERSONS' TREAT AT ESTON.</p>
        <p>This treat was given in the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Friday evening. The treat was promoted by a few of those who are connected with the Weston Bible and Mutual Improvement Class, and is the first of the kind which has been lield in the village. Over 60 aged persons sat down to tea, the tables being presided over by Miss Ford, Mrs. D. Kirkham, Mrs. John Griffiths, Mrs. John Smith, Mrs. Onions, Mrs. Thomas Ford, and Mrs. Joseph Lunt. In- vitations to take tea with the old people were sent to the Rev. Mr. and Mrs. Arrowsmith, Rev. G. FL Kingston, the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi. Mr. and Mrs. Handley, Mr. and Mrs. Sill, Mrs. Bankes. Mr. and Mrs. James Ford, Mrs. Wesley Kirkham, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Ford (Caven- dish Farm), Mr. and Mrs. Wm. Griffiths. After tea, tobacco was distributed to the men, and tea to the women. Some of the old folk who would otherwise have been unable to be present, on account of old age and infirmity, were conveyed to and from the treat in a brake, supplied by Messrs. Yarwood, of Runcorn. At the concert held after tea Mr. T. T. Sill occupied the chair, and was supported on the platform by the Rev. W. Arrowsmith, Rev. G. H. Kingston, and Mr. Jas- Ford (chairman of the committee). The Rev. W. Arrow- smith, in a few explanatory remarks,informed the audi- ence how the tea and concert had been promoted, and expressed his pleasure at being present —The following ladies and gentlemen took part in the concert: Miss Branch, Miss Simkin, Misses Timperley, Mr. F. L. Scott, Mr. H. Branch, Mr. T. Clegg, Mr. J. Leathwood, Mr. Rhodes, Mr. J. W. Somerville (Willenhall), and Mr. James Tudor, jun. Mr. R. Birkett Musgrove, accom- panied. The old folk very much appreciated the dia- logue, entitled "Grandmother," by five children, the principal part being taken by Miss Ethel Matthews; a musical drill by nine children, under the direction of Miss B. Smith; and the song "I'm the very worst girl in school," by Miss Edith Smith. No encores were allowed, on account of the long programme. The audience, however, showed their appreciation of the efforts made to give them an enjoyable evening in a marked and enthusiastic manner. In responding to a vote of thanks proposed by the Rev. W. Arrowsmith, and seconded by the Rev. G. U. Kingston, Mr. Sill said that he was very pleased to be present, and hoped that the treat would be an annual one, now that it had once been given. In the course of the evening, the committee having charge of the treat made the pleasing announcement that their appeal for funds in aid of thetas had been liberally res- ponded to, handsome donations having being given by Mr. J. T. Brunner, M.P., and Mr. W. R. Earn, C.C. They took that opportunity of publicly expressing their thanks to all who so willingly subscribed. On the fol- lowing day what was left over from the tea was distri- buted among the old people who were unable to be I present at the treat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
