      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>HUSBAND WITH GUNS AND REVOLVERS.</head>
        <p>A HUSBAND WITH GUNS AND REVOLVERS.</p>
        <p>HIS WIFE TELLS OF HER TERRORS.</p>
        <p>George Davies Perry, a gardener, of Liverpool- road, said he had nothing to say in his defence when charged at the Borough Police Court on Thursday, with threatening to do his wife grevious bodily harm. The story of those threats, his wife,.a very respectable looking woman, told at the invitation of the. Magistrates Clerk. She said :</p>
        <p>" George Perry, there,is my husband, and through his drunkenness we both lead an unhappy life. He does not contribute one penny towards the maintenance of his family, and we have to keep ourselves. Some time ago.l he told me to get a house for wiyself and he would allow me my maintenance. I got a cottage but he would not allow me take any furniture there, and said if I did' he would murder me.</p>
        <p>"But are you afraid of his threats 2" asked the Maaistrates Clerk.</p>
        <p>"-Yes, indeed I am. He keeps two revolvers and two guns, and walks about the house with them, point- ing them at us. I don't know whether they are loaded or not, but they frighten me. The other night he came up to my bedroom and bending over he placed a re- volver at my ear and said," you are drawing</p>
        <p>YOUR LAST BREATH." The screlins of the children seemed to frighten him and he left the room. On Saturday week, he locked me in a bedroom by myself and said he would set fire to the house. He does these things when he is drunk cer- tainly, but he is never sober. He is always drunk ! drunk ! drunk !"</p>
        <p>Miss Perry spoke of her father's generally abusive conduct to her mother, and then the defendant was asked if he wanted to say anything. " No," he answered "I have nothing to say." After considering the case the Bench bound defendant over to ker.') the peace for six months in a surety of £5, and one of £lO, and ordered him to pay lls. cost</p>
        <p>THE SPECIALIST AND MRS. MAYBRICK</p>
        <p>The " Central News "learns that, at the request of the Baroness de Rogues, Sir Spencer Wells, the eminent specialist, has consented to visit Mrs. Maybrick in Wok- ing Prison, and to report and advise upon her case. A. visit of this character will require a special permit from the authorities, which it is hoped will not be withheld.</p>
        <p>THE NEW EVENING PAPER.</p>
        <p>It is stated that it has been finally decided that the new evening paper to be carried on for Mr. Newnes by Mr. Cook and other members of the former staff of the " Pall Mall Gazette " shall be called the " Westminster Gazette." The weekly paper in connection with it will be named the" Westminster Budget." The first nurnber of the " Westminster Gazette" will appear on the day that Parliament meets, the 31st inst. Mr. Gould will be the Parliamentary caricaturist. The paper will ultim- ately be produced in much the same form as the present "Pall Mall Gazette," but as the premises and machinery eannot be ready by the date named, it will be produced at first in temporary premises, and in a different form from that which it is intended ultimately to present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>HEAVY RAILWAY RATES.</head>
        <p>THE HEAVY RAILWAY RATES.</p>
        <p>TRADES UP IN ARMS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of representatives of the engineering and boiler-making trades in Manchester and district was held on Tuesday for the purpose of discussing the new railway rates as they affect those trades. Mr. C. J. Galloway, who presided, said that in the carriage of boilers the railway rates were increased by from 25 to 40 per cent, and in the case of engines the advance was from 20 to 50 per cent. Other makers bore out this statement. A resolution was passed ssrongly protesting against the new rates, on the ground that they would act most injuriously. on the engineering trades, and place those trades in a further disadvantageous position as compared with foreign competitors. The wholesale tea dealers of Manchester also met and passed a resolution protesting against the increased charges. It was urged by several gentlemen that advantage should be taken of the Ship Canal for the carrage of goods to and from London. The cattle dealers attending the Salford market on Tues- day resolved to recommend the members of their associa- tions to walk their cattle by road in preference to send- ing them by rail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>SHOCKING ACCIDENT ON A RUSSIAN: RAILWAY. 49 SOLDIERS BURNED TO DEATH.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING ACCIDENT ON A RUSSIAN: RAILWAY. 49 SOLDIERS BURNED TO DEATH.</p>
        <p>Sr. PETHRSBLTRO, TITEBDAT.—A frightful disaster occurred on the 11th inst., on the Slatoust-Samara rail- way. A train in which some 200 recruits were travelling on their way to join their regiments caught fire as it as going at full speed. The flames burst forth first in the front part of a long car in which the soldiers were riding, and, fanned into fury by the rush of the train, spread from one end of the car to the other with terrific rapidity. The train was brought to a standstillfas quickly as possible and the car, which was enveloped in fire, was uncoupled from the rest of the train. The young soldiers immediately made a frantic rush for the door and windows, and most of them suoceedecl in escaping from the burning car •, but in the haste and confusion a number of them got wedged tightly together in a passage leading to the door, and nothingcoalfl move them. Seven were dragged out of the mass of struggling human beings, but not until they had sus- tained severe burns, from the effects of which one died later in the day. The rest, to the number of fortymere simply roastod to death. Eight others met with a lass painful death, being suffocated by sisoke.</p>
        <p>L BRINGS JOY •</p>
        <p>TB E GLASGOW IRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>EXECUTION GF M'KEOWM. HORRIBLE SCENE ON TuE SCAFFOLD. At eight on Wednesday morning William M'Keowne who murdered Elizabeth Connor in Wt-st Lodge,' Pollokshields, Glasgow, on October 11, and thereafter cut her body up into bits and buried the remains in the' garden adjoining the house, was executed within Was: gow (Duke-street) Gaol. An angry wind moaned al' the morning. As day dawned a crowd of 6,000 r.mrsons collected outside the prison walls, the experience. of previous executions telling them that if the trafhc with' out was quiet at the hour when the dread sentence WO being carried out, the bang of the gallows iner givmfl way could be distinctly heard. However, fifty police- men kept the thoroughfare clear, while an equal number within the precincts of the gaol guarded the various passes, so that no one would witness the death scene b 0 the two magistrates, Raillies Alexander and Paline, the Catholic priest in attendance upon the condemned man. the joiner, master of works, and the two town officers. The last-named carried their halberds.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>The reporters were not admitted to witness the ere' cution, but were accommodated in the wa.tehroonv ,within the gate. The policemen who were on guard inside stated that in the dark they could see the roP6. dangling on the scaffold. i‘Plieown was restless all night ; indeed, since Sir James Marwick, Town-clerk,. informed him on Tuesday that the law ,vas ti take ite course, the condemned man was excited. Me ordr . remark he passed was that he had more time to prepare for death than the poor woman whose life he took. At five minutes to eight a turnkey came to a little room at the front gate, aad, having opened it. showea Billing,ton, the executioner, out. He worea small Ye% , vet peaked cap. He was led away to the condemned' cell, where he pinioned M'Keown. The sad processioll' a few minutes after eight o'clock started along the Or; ridor, and at eight minutes past the hour the noise 0, the platform giving way was distinctly beard in the reporters room. At the same instant the black flag Wag hoisted. The crowd outside began to get anxious 010 the sign of the capital punishment having been carried . out was not seen sooner. They thought something vi'as wrong, as it was known that M'Keown's neck was tea- der through his having when caught attempted to WO' mit suicide. Immediately thereafter the jr 'cession wended its way back to the governor's room, he officer° holding their halberds down. Dr. Sutherland stated that M'Keown walked .calrill/ to his doom, but when the platform gave way the roPe burst his neck; but the head did not come off. There . was no blood, the wound which the culprit had inflicted .• on himself at his capture being readily opened up. 0° did not sleep till three o'clock.in the morning, and the.,ll his rest was only fitful for an hour. He was attend by Fathers Clifford and M'Graw, who administered the last rites of the Church to the condemned man. Whet) pinioned he never spoke a word to the ma,gistrates or the executioner. As he walked to his doom be made the responses in an undertone to the service read by the- reverend fathers. M'Keown, who was 11t., received a drop of 71t. The Governor of Glasgow tDuke-st.) Prison states that before M'Keown had the white cap .drawn over his face he kissed the crucifix. He was on his knees prayini when Billington entered his cell. He had severe paroxysms of grief throughout the morning, and wirti he walked to the scaffold his eyes were suffused Wial tears.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>RETURN OF UNIONISTS TO TIIE LIBERAL PARTY.</head>
        <p>RETURN OF UNIONISTS TO TIIE LIBERAL PARTY.</p>
        <p>--- A Leicester correspondent telegraphs :—The event of the Liberal demonstration at Leicester on Wednesda/ night was the public return of several Liberal Unionists to the Liberal parry. These included Alderman Wrg ex-Mayor, who had been invited to centest the borougn in the Usio4ist interest at the last election, but declined and was now received most enthusiastically. Seconding a vote of thanks, he explained that the discussions ce the past few years had removed a good tnanyrnisconcer tions and fallacies on Home Rule. Six years ago Liberal Unionists merely exercised the right of privaw judgment. Honestly their objects had been to bring about a better state of things in Ireland and to exter local government, but the latter object had been scarril. attempted by the late Government. "Indeed, they not shown the smallest intention of carrying AO/ That being so, everybody who laid claim to pstriodr', must think that Liberal Unionists could now Mc° hands with their old political friends. The Irish Clclt- tion ought no longer to be the sport of parties or to bandied about from one side of the House of Comine°' to the other. There should be some reasonable WIC, to settle it, because it was the stumbling bleck way of other legislation. The discussion of the lastM., years had removed from the bill of 1886 some defeenM provisions and modified the views of some Libe!= Unionists, therefore those who belonged of right tn__lfi Liberal party thought they might support the Government in their attempt to settle the Irish quo.,'"'" The speech was received with the greatest enthusiely and Alderman Wright and others were cordiallf owned back to the party.</p>
