      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>letters to the Cutor.</head>
        <p>letters to the Cutor.</p>
        <p>BUDDHISM AND CHRISTIANITY.—SOME POINTS OF BESEMBLANCE. Sir,—lt was with much pleasure that I read in your last impression the letter of Harry Esmond, showing the remarkably close resemblance which exists between Buddhism and Christianity, and between Christ and Chrishna, the Hindoo god. Al, however, Mr. Esmond has net entered much into detail, in order to show the parallelisms between the Christian and Buddhist, or rather Brahmin. Saviours, perhaps I might be per- mitted to amplify these a little. In the Hindoo sacred writings it is recorded of Chrishna that he descended from a royal family. Christ is said to have descended from the royal house of David. Chrishna was called the shepherd god, and Christ the God Shepherd. Christina was an incarnation of Vishnu, the second per- son of the Trimurti (Trinity), Christ, of the Holy Ghost, then the second Person of the Trinity, now the third. Both Chrishaa and Christ are said to have per- formed miracles and washed the feet of their disciples. Both died on a cross, and rose agaia at the end of three days after burial. Both descended into hell and returned to psradise. Both are declared to have been the incar- nation of the Divine Being. Even in the minor details of ' the lives of Christhila and Christ, as given in the Hindoo gospels and the New Testament, the parallel still holds , good. For instance, both had an interview with a woman at a well, and described to her all her past I life; both had their heads anointed by a woman on a public occasion. Many Christians assert that that portion of the Bha- gavat Ghita, or Hindoo Bible, which gives the account of Chrishna, is simply a copy of the New Testament, and the Brahmins claim that he lived 7,00 q years ago, and there is no reason to doubt, that assuming his existence' snch antiquity is not too great to assign to him. Several Christian authorities have been compelled to admit that the record of Chrishna must have ante-dated that of, Christ by thousands of years, while Sir Wm. Hamiltons I an eminent Oriental scholar, and a Christian, concede 1 that the Hindoo preceded the Christian gospels by a I thousand years. Some Christians assert that the extra- 1 ordinary likeness between these is but mere coincidence, but such a contention is manifestly absurd. Others, and I once heard a well-known Wesleyan preacher take this line of argument, contend that though the Hindoo, were earlier than the Christian, sacred books, the similarities between them were an ordination of Divine Providence as a prefigurement in Chrishna of the life of Christ. This again is absurd sophistry, and can impose upon none except upon a class of minds some- thing like those which once accepted the argument that the fossils which have disproved the flood theory were placed in the earth by the Deity. to be produced some time as a trial of Christian faith. Those of my readers who would like to study further the question of the remarkable parallel between Chrishna and Christ I would refer to an English translation of the IThagavat Ghita, which I believe can be obtained, and also to Peebles's "Seers of the Ages" and Madame Bla- vatsky's " Iris Unveiled."—Yours truly, ECLECTIC.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>"MORALITY UP IN A BALLOON."</head>
        <p>"MORALITY UP IN A BALLOON."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Taking as a text the Bishop of Edinburgh's sneer, that Bishop aloorhouee's noble defence of animal rights, at the Church Congress was "Morality up in a balloon," / should like to give some reasons for regarding Mae Edinburgh ethics as morality down in the gutter The snugly placed gentleman who signs himself 'Edinburgh" does not seem to have reiected that such words are very inappropriate as coming from that carious anomaly, a Christian dignitary ; that all moral reforms—all attempts to raise the plane of morality to a higher level—have been regarded by people like himself whose morality is that of the man in the street—as being" morality up in a balloon." Even the system of morality which he is so well paid to teach was so held by many who were content, like himself, with the ethics of the via eovsmusis. This is the old, old plea :—What was good enough for our fathers ought to suffice for us. The rest-and-be-thankful frame of mind marks the sum- mit of moral ascension and the beginning of the fall, as the upward cast projectile hangs for one infinitesimal moment balanced in mid air b 7 the two forces of exhausted projection and conquering attraction, prepar- atory to the downward rush. In ethics, as in physics,to cease to go upward is to commence to go down. bike all good things in this world morality must be wrought for and sought for, and to be kept must be fought for, There is little hope of the moral advancement of the man who thinks the present is good enough for him. Such a is no better than his surroundings—of which indeed . ---m0,04^4 hv his generation_ trjWWl, the ereatu.- ; which he i 4 no natter if tie worse. alb eeough to think that Vre ae A nation adVincing morally, that there is a process of ethical *Volution going on amongst tts, which, If slow, is, perhaps, all the More likely to be stable. We are dis- tinctly better in this repect than were our forefathers of the I.6th eentuiy. To them our 'node of ethics would have been "morality up in a balloon." But still I think I may venture to say, without fear of being torn to pieces,that we are not perfection yet—that when the 22nd century weighs us we shall be found wanting in this matter. Men are very apt to regard what they are accustomed to see as being right. In this way many evils are uncon- sciously condoned. Take, for example, that veterinary iniquity so playfully alluded to as" making him into a sergeant." This" humane" operation consists of sear- ing the legs of a lame horse in bands by means of a red het iron. The stripes so produced are the cause of the aforesaid facetiousness. What is the excuse for a practice that in my estimation should result in a con- viction by aid of the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals P To cure the horse for its own good Scarcely! The poor horse has been reduced to lameness by abuse—overwork—and it is subjected to , this torture in order that it may be still further abused by overwork. Now this is an immorality which has been handed down to us from the bad old times when might was right, when to be able todo a thing, however wrong in itself, conferred the right to do it. Such plots as these are often assigned as excuses for the more modern iniquity of vivisection. A bad and weak cause is characterised by bad and weak arguments offered in support of it. Surely the presence of one evil of long standing can in no way be a sanction for the introduction of another ! But it is indeed singular how people, who are so fond of arrogating to themselves the title, par excellence, of scientific, can so far stultify themselves as to use such arguments, many of which are good only at first sight. But, as I have pointed out in another place vivisection is not itself scientific, and never will he till man himself is the subject. As well might the chemist, if interdicted trom experimentation upon one metal experiment upon another and apply the results and reasonings to the case of the tabooed one! The practice is morally and scientifically wrong, an in this light our successors will see it—that is if moral evolu- tion is a living principle.—Yours, ftc. JOHN PEERS. ranks* January 18, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>FORMATION OF A LABOUR DEPART.</head>
        <p>FORMATION OF A LABOUR DEPART.</p>
        <p>MENT.</p>
        <p>The Cabinet has, it is understood, approved the for- mation of a Department of Labour in connection with the Board of Trade. "Department." not" bureau," will be the official designation. As President of the Board of Trade, Mr. Mundella is, of course practically responsible for the scheme. At the same time it is understood that in the undertaking he has had the warm support of the Prime Minister and the other members of the Cabinet. Not only has an apportionment to meet the expenses of the department been made.but premises have been fixed upon for offices. What the apportionment is cannot as yet be stated, but there is some reason to suppose that it will be found to be fairly substantial. Both Mr. Mun- della. and Mr. Thomas Burt are engaged on the scheme i from day to day. It s not contemplated to make the Labour Department an executive one. Factory inspec- tion is to remain under the control of the Home Secre- tary, only the relations between the Home Secretary and the Labour Department in this respect will be very close. Primarily, and mainly, the functions of the La bour Department will be to collect, sift, and distribute the amplest information on the labour market. Corres- pondents are to be planted at the various trade and com- mercial centres of the country, and the department will have representatives in foreign countries—oftenest, no doubt, the English Consuls. The department will, it Is thought, be furnished with some machinery of concilia- tion in the case of disputes and strikes.</p>
        <p>TOWN TOPICS IN GENERAL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>GENERALLY DISCUSSED.</head>
        <p>GENERALLY DISCUSSED.</p>
        <p>BY LUKE BLUNT.</p>
        <p>It was a point of business which had thrown us all together. The business had been discussed and settled. A few general remarks about that old, old, subject the weather had been passed; and as often happens when nothing more fertile than the climate is on hand for comment, conversation wags its weary tale of "the farmers won't like it," and falls into silence. Thus it happened with our conversa- tion ; and there we sat, as the versatile novelist would say, " each occupied with his own thoughts." Although,maybe,one cir two of us didn't occupy our- selves in this way, because thinking is but an occa- sional dissipation with us anyhow.We eat and stared silently, and looked thoughttul. It was a gathering which I knew would, soon or late provide material for debate. All Warrington's political ideas were represented. There was a promirent member ot the Primrose League party, a well-known Con- servative, a Liberal-Socialist, and a Home Ruler, with myself thrown in,representing that section of Warrington which has no ideas of its own, but mix on s diversity of other people's opinions. By- and-by a large piece of coal fell from the lire into the iron fender pith a bang. It acted as a rude awakener to the Liberal's thoughts. He flicked the white ashes from his cigar and said : , "By the way, I see this gift of Mr. Parr's for a new Public Hall, has thrown the Council into a flutter. They don't know what to do with it; for it isn't enough to build what is required. I'm glad they intend to talk the matter over with him because—."</p>
        <p>The Primrose Leaguer's eyes had sparkled in a minuts ;he gazed fiercely at the Liberal and bang- ing his hand on the table as an emphasis, inter- rupted with "In my opinion it is a most indeli- cate,ungracious thing for the Council to do. Why, they are practically going to tell Mr. Parr, after giving FIVE—THOUSAND—POUNDS, that he has not offered enough. The sublime impertinence of ths thing dazzles me,coming as it does from a generally impecunious Town Council. It strikes me that they are used to squandering such large sums that a trifle like .£5,000 isn't worthy of their all-fired spending proclivities."</p>
        <p>We all laughed at this sudden outburst of righteous indignation. When the Liberal had finished his gavvfing, and wiped the tears from his eyes which the laughing had created, he said: Well, may be I'm right • may be I'm wrong; but if my memory is not playing the.cheat with me, I can safely say you are the first member of that glorious institution of the tea and bun de- molishing ladies that ever I found with such a ten- der regard for delicacy in " inviting " monetary help. Your fi iends—the immaculate dames and associates—would not scorn, I think, to ask Mr. Parr to help your party in its pettifogging matters, and why should the town refuse an offered gift from him, especially when that gift is a benefit to everyone in Warring- ton? It is a noble piece of generosity, and Mr. Parr offers it as a gentleman of Warrington, not as a partizan. It is absurd for you to speak about the Council going and begging more, for it is well known that Mr. Parr has not tied himself to give £5,000. He is desirous to give us a new Publi c Hall, and was advised by some one who thought he knew that £5,000 would be sufficient.</p>
        <p>"Humph" ejaculated the Primrose Leaguer. Then he lapsed into a silence which was accom- panied with a tatoo upon the table. He seemed annoyed, and there was an aspect in his eyes when he looked at the Liberal which seemed to say : You've clinched me this time. But wait till we cross swords again Mr. Clever. To debate with a man like me who speaks in public! Pooh! For the honour of my oratorical name I'll make you " spread" yourself.</p>
        <p>Quite unaware of this vindictive intention,the Liberal ejaculated placidly "Yes we do want a Public Hall." "Where do you think it will be built" said the Tory, who now spoke for the first time. He is a man not overdone with verbosity, and whose head is not grow- ing bald from the internal eruptions of many ideas. He says he is a Conservative because his father was one; he believes in monarchy, and the Establishment and likes to he comfortable, and there is one thing he shrinks from as an ass shrinks from running 200 yards at a spell, and that is arguing. The Liberal knew this man, and so answered him with a certain amount of compassion in his voice. He said :</p>
        <p>din% know exactly. They tried to get a separate site but it would cost £l,OOO, so they abandoned that idea in favour of the scheme for including it in the block of centralized municipal buildings. Municipal buildings centralized would be good. But the idea of centralising, them where the present Town Hall is ,vould be a bad thing. It would entirely destroy the present beauty of the park. They ought to build the whole of the offices, including the Town Hall, somewhere else. Now. I'll tell you what always strikes me as a good site for that purpose ; and that is the house where the Miss Garvens live, Ind the Rev. Mr. Carleton's house. What do you think." "Ah !" said the Tory musingly. "Perhaps it would. It would improve the general appearance of Sankey-et., I anyhow." "That's no use" said the Home Ruler. " Because I know there is a kind of agreement by which the Miss Garvens' house cannot be touched whilst they tenant it. And another thing. I believe it is intended to make a new road over that site when the property can be bought. Its my opinion that if they centralized the bui;dings at the Town Hall, and pulled down the Park wall, applendid block—a credit to the town—could 'OB built,"</p>
        <p>Our fries A the Primrose Leagtter kad igatti ioitotton his .eticeece. He saw the chance of a side feint at the Liberal, and with a benevolent nod Of tbe head in the direction of the Home Ruler he said " Hight again, old boy—quite agree with you. And notkeng would be better than a wide sweep of green refreshing lawn, lead- ing from the street, if the wall was pulled down." A smile of self-assured superiority flickered about the Liberal's mouth, and gazing deeply into the fire he murmured audibly : "No—no ! Nothing could be better—as a perspective for the Conservative Club. The guinea members could sit at their tables and look across that greei refreshing sweep of lawn." "Look here, drop that, my friend !" said the Primrose Leaguer angrily " you never credit a body with any motive except the promotion of the small devices et his own party. You are too cynical—too spiteful—too- -00 everything, dash it. Why,didn't Sir Gilbert offer to pull the wall down and erect a fountain on the lawn before the Conservative Club was built ?" "Now, my boy,"said the Liberal placidly "don't make an ass of yourself over trifles." The Home Ruler smiled at this, and turning to the Liberal he said soothingly:—" Don't be too hard on him. He isn't making an ass of himself." " No !" said the Primrose Leaguer still more satisfied at the Home Ruler's support. " It is impossible," continued the Hone Ruler " for a maa to convert himself into what nature has already made him." The Primrose Leaguer appeared to feet hurt; a look of seeming to feel that his best friend had proved false enter- ed his eves, but he maintained his composure, and bore through all the subsequent laoghing with a supreme curl of contempt upon his upper lip. That by the way was all be had on that part of his face. "Let me see," said the Tory meditatively " how was it Sir Gilbert's offer wasn't accepted II" "The Corporation hadn't acquired the water works then and they refused to meet the expense of supplying the fountain with water," answered the Liberal. " By the way," put in the Home Ruler quietly, "I believe that when that happened a deputation was about to wait upon Sir Gilbert asking him to lay a main from the brewery to the park, and work the fountain by a supply of beer. But I'm told that the temperance party threasened to oppose it, and it dropped."</p>
        <p>cil meeting to protest against the high price and the low quality of the gas. " Well, ejaculated the Tory with the laugh. "we shall get plenty of schemes for its reduction towards next November. Don't you know Who is coming out then. There's Councillors Roberts, Fleming, Browne, Shaw, Garner, and, let me see—" "Well, are you going to light this year r asked the Liberal.</p>
        <p>It was a loud outbreak of laughter which greeted this sally of the Irishman's ; but the Primrose Leaguer never smiled. He said in offended tone : " Wit in its proper place is always very acceptable ; but ridiculous comments cannot claim to be considered wit. However you may endeavour to depreciate Sir Gilbert's benevol- ence, the fact still remains that he has done many phil- anthropic things for Warrington." " Indeed," asked the Liberal in a bland playful tone, looking like a spider who sees the innocent fly becoming entaneled in his web " What has he done for Warring- ton ?" " Well he's built churches and—er—er—he's done other things." The Liberal smiled some more and said " Yes, he has been very benevolent to the churches ; but for all that what beneficial effect will a dozen new churches have upon the hundreds of poor wretches in this town, who have helped to maintain Sir Gilbert's revenue by drink- ing his beer. Of course every man has his own style of dispensing charity. The church certainly needs help, and one cannot cast an aspersion upon the baronet for bestowing his gifts in that direction. Still considering his profits COM@ chiefly from a class of people who are poor and ignorant you can't deny that they have a cer- tain moral claim upon his assistance." "That is so " admitted the Tory musingly, and the subject dropped.</p>
        <p>" Rather ! But I think Roberts, Fleming, and Browne will be safe. As for the others we intend to give them gip—we'll make it a Tory Council." "Shaw won't go in anyhow," said the Liberal. "I think we shall get the Irish vote in a proper way this time, and then it will be all U P for Mr. Shaw."</p>
        <p>"But don't you feel afraid of the Labour party," ven- tured the Primrose Leaguer, as though he was saving a point which might have been overlookcd. "Don't you remember the time when they met, disgusted with your compromise,and they and the Labour party agreed to combine this next election, in running candidates ?" "To a cattails extent "said the Home Ruler, "the Irish vote had a right to threaten the Liberals with such an action. But, one of their fiery generals has gone ; so has their unwillingness to compromise. That part of the Labour party which entered the alliance with them consisted of Mr. John Price. And as he doesn't happen to represent those Ship Canal navvies on the Trades Council any more, why I don't think there is much to fear from him. The legitimate Labour Party of this town has never separated from the Liberals." "Referring to the gas, and Councillor Fleming" sad the Tory."' don't think we are being fairly done by. You see the Corporation put on high prices for the ratepayer, and the only people who are favoured with low terms are the manufacturers. They get special reductions." "Yes." said the Liberal, " and they're entitled to it. The enormous amount of gas which they consume, even when supplied at a slightly reduced price—like large quantities of anything else—helps to enrich the rate- payers of this town." "If they didn't get suited that way" said the Home Ruler," they could make their own gas." "No, not without a special Act of Parliament. And they wouldn't get that because, a Corporation al- ways has a prior claim to the monopoly of gas. They would oppose it." " By the way," said the Tory, "getting away from gas to smallpox what do you think of the proposition which the Health Committee made last week, to peti- tion the County Council to erect permanent central infectious diseases hospitals." ." Grand thing—fine!" said the Liberal enthusiastic- ally. 'But do you know I've an idea that that scheme first originated from a legal Corporation official. That's some time ago, and he was laughed at then, but, mark my words, we shall have the honour of seeing that em- bodied into a special Act of Parliament, and carried out universally in all the counties." The conversation flagged again, and as some one exclaimed "gracious me it's half-past eleven," we glided swiftly to cur various homes, for, not being bachelors, we are not privileged to keep very late hours.</p>
        <p>" What do you think of the gas," I said, after watch- ing the two candle strength gas light flicker and jump. My friend, the Liberal replied that he didn't think much of our gas, for it was bad, very bad. HP said he was also surprised at the way in which Councillor Fleming was cried down when he rose at the last Cowl-</p>
