      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>A fHLETICS.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Again has the Warrington first team been fated to meet with disappointment. After being some weeks without a match, they were looking forward to Satur- day last, when they were down to meet Mossley, with feelings of expectation. But, alas, when the popular pay day came, there arrived with it a telegram from M...ss- ley, stating that the match would have to be postponed in consequence of the frozen condition of their field ; and lam told that they didn't like to come over here, because they thought it was necessary for them to be re- vaccinated first.</p>
        <p>It has been suggested that the team gat up a "Charity match," during the continuance of the bard frost,for the benefit ef the poor. Well, probably the team will get up a "Charity match," but as their funds are getting low, I have a kind of a presentiment that they will exemplify the old adage that "Charity begins at home.' So, under the circumstances, the poor are not likely to benefit much from such a match.</p>
        <p>The effect of the postponement of the first team match had at least one good point, and that was to place a good team on the field at Wilderspool where the home A and the Mossley A met. The visitors brought with them five first team men, and Warrington went on a similar tack, and were lucky enough to get Boscow with them.</p>
        <p>The match which resulted in a fair victory for the homesters, was not particularly noteworthy as a uni- form game. True, there was an extraordinary amount of individual effort, but this is a characteristic of some of our younger A team men, which we would warn them against. Hampscn, for instance, has got plenty of splendid" go "in him, but he will persist in being too eager, and thus lost several chances that might hare been utilised had he shown a little coolness and discre- tion. It was a treat to see Tommy Barnes showing some of his grand old form, and once or twice he quite reminded me of the old days, when he broke away into some of his inimitable dodging tactics. " Smack" Bate did very well, whilst Boscow played a game whict, won him many a cheer. The result was one try for the homesters to nil.</p>
        <p>A little more " contest" was done in the direction of the Junior League Championship on Saturday. The Parish Church team beat the Brewery team by 11 points to nil, and Latchford drew with the Rangers on a try. This leaves the result in this competition thus :</p>
        <p>For Agst. Plyd. Won. Lost. Drn. G.T. G.T.Pts. Parish Church 5 . 4 . 1 . 0 Rangers 5 .. 3 ... 1 . 1 St. Mary's 3 ... 2 ... 1 .~ 0 Latchford Stockton Heath 2 ... 1 ... 1 ... 0 Walker's Brawn.? ;„u . 2 0 OlO ... 2 4 ...0 reatellf°lrt -SC ele 8 1 0 1 0 Juniors</p>
        <p>The Lancashire etip Competition is going apace, but Mmewhat blowly. Broughton Rangers and St. Helens Recreatioit mot on Saturday. Success is said to lead to a dangerous kind of elation ; and after the match between these two teams I am inclined to believe it. As the old saving goe.s the Recs, played in that " cocksure" kind of way whicla showed plainly their unlimited belief in their own ability to annihilate the Rangers. But they didn't dolt. By half time they were being well beaten, and it was only by a considerable amount of exertion in the latter half of the game that they were able to get on level terms witti their antagonists. By the end of the game however, they were able to do this, both teams Inishing with 11 points each.</p>
        <p>There was a great contrast to this, however, in thei match between Broughton and Swinton. Swinton's play has been described as a" regular riddling of the Broughton ranks." Swinton won by 33 points to nil— one of the finest victories this season. Valentine, after a rather severe illness, appeared again, and played in his old, old, style.</p>
        <p>The results in this competition stand thus t-- For. Agst. Won. Lost. Drn. UT. G.T. Pts. Swinton 9 . 8 . 0 ... 1 ...12 20... 1 2 ...17 Bt. Helens Recreation 8 ... 6 ... 2 ... 1 ... 9 11... 4 6 ...11 Warrington Tyldesley Salford Oldham lO .. 2 . 5 ... 3 ... 4 9 ... 5 7 ... 7 Rochdale Hornets Broughton Rangers Wivan Broughton ll . 1 ... 8 ... 2 ... 3 2 ...12 24... 4</p>
        <p>WAItRINGTON ASSOCIATION CLUB, ON THE LITERPOOL JUNIOR CUP.</p>
        <p>Come, cheer up, lads, yo needn't shame O'er loysing th' Challeng cup ; Yo'y work'd reyght hard to win the same I'th way yo've follow'd up. A deaol o' clubs yo'v hed to meet— It'pleaoses me to tell— He s getten lick'd, tho' they hey bin Clubs owder than yorsel. Yo'r nobbut yung cempared wi some That's bin agate for years ; But tho yo've lost the junior cup The Printers had their fears. Tak this advise, just try it on, Yo'll find it not amiss ; Stick well together—mon to mon Then next yeor yo'll mend this. J. E. C.</p>
        <p>THE FRIVOLITY OF AMERICAN LADIES. An original kind of luncheon was recently given by a young American lady, who arranged that each of her guests should be dressed in a costume representing some well-known novel. A priz,. was awarded to the best costume, and another was given to the guest who should most correctly guess the names of the books from the dresses representing them. 011 e lady wore a network of twisted cords all askew, and tied here and there with cords and tassels. She was " Oliver Twist" (all-of-a- twist). Another, all in brightest red from head to foot, w"Study :n Scarlet." Aalas easily guessdeyasinanornaemd eansteal°n7grlitToYwien.Bwith a ribbon round the waist, from which was suspended a bag full of socks and stockings with yarn and needles was the exponent Too Late to Mend." The eo af sCi ehsat re) eosst if: me aed ey,v'sa s" tNheavte Never ggested by Miss Alrott's novel "A Rose in Bloom." It consisted merely of a magnificent rose fastened On the bodice of an ordinary toilet. A lady with a shield ripresenting a knight in armour, surrounded by kneeling captives, represented .4 One of our Conquerors," by George Meredith.</p>
