      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The distress in Warrington has abated consider- ably this week, as most of the outdoor workpeople have been able to resume their vocations. The hot- pot dinners to poor children, provided by public subscription got up by the Mayor and Mayoress, have been continued during the week. The dinners will be discontinued to-day (Saturday). Many thanks are due to the following persons who have daily assisted in dispensing the food :—Mrs. Whittle, the Misses Fleming, Miss Kiernan, Miss Cavanagh,Mr. .1. L. Whittle, town clerk,Mr. Stanley Wilson, Alderman Wright, Councillor Stnethuret, Mr. Peter Chrimes, Mr. G. Riley, and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>BROTHERHOOD OF FOBTEL</head>
        <p>A BROTHERHOOD OF FOBTEL</p>
        <p>A correspondent sends us the following:— Although Warrington is so much immersed in its purely material and commercial pursuits, and Lan- cashire is considered a county which is pre-emin- ently remarkable for this devotion, yet it is some consolation to find that in our town, as in other parts of the country, there are men who are con- staatly aiming at directing the minds of the con:I- I:trinity in the higher way, and are the salt that keep us from moral and mental putrefaction. We ' have our poets in Warrington as well as in other , parts of Lancashire, and it is pleasing to have to record that in this supposed materialistic county has sprung into existence a national Brotherhood of Poets, and that one of the Council of the society is a Warringtonian. The secretary of the society is Mr. H. Berkeley Score, F.R.G.S., F.R.H.S., &amp;c., of Lathom Perk Schools,Ormskirk, a man of consider- able culture and learning, as his titles imply. Professor Blackie is among the honorary members of the society. One of the objects of the society is to bring to light excellent writers of verse who would other- wise remain in obscurity; so our Warrington poets who fancy their productions not duly appreciated should take the hint from this and join. The society is very comprehensive, including all who love poetry, whether they have written it or not, hilt those elected Fellows of the Brotherhood or members of the Council must have published a " fair amount of sensible verse," have contributed freely to magazines or journals, or have written a sufficient quantity of M.S.S. worthy of merit. A quarterly magazine is to be commenced shortly in connection with the society, in which the contri- butions of members will be published. I commend the society to aspiring riders of Pegasus in War- rington as presenting to them abundant chances to win their spurs. Warrington has produced famous men. Who can tell that it may not cherish in its bosom a future Tennyson, whom the new society rimy bring to light?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>-.4.- A TOWN BESIEGED BY WOLVES.</head>
        <p>-.4.- A TOWN BESIEGED BY WOLVES.</p>
        <p>According to the Vienna papers, the Russian town of Tikhvin, in Novgorod, is virtually besieged by wolves. The hungry beasts run round the city in large packs, and sometimes enter the street to seize whatever pre- sents itself—cats, dogs, and even small children. The women of Tikhvin stay indoors, and the men only go out armed to the teeth. The Governor of the province has sent a battalion of infantry,a sotnia of Cossacks,and 300 Chasseurs to kill the wolves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATHS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATHS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>TWO INQUESTS IN AN HOUR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, held two inquests at iv)on on Tuesday. The first one was conducted at the Parade Room at the Police station. The death investi- gated was that of a Mr. Robert Jones, Egerton-st., who died on Saturday. Miss Jones daughter of deceased, said that her father had suffered from heart disease far about ten years. He was often attacked with this com- plaint and never sent for a doctor. He seemed to be attacked with it on Saturday afternoon when he died. Inspector Cox who had known the deceased 40 years corroborated this, and after medical evidence had been given a verdict of " Death from natural causes" was returned. The deceased, who was much respected, was the author of two Educational books, "Hand railing " and "Carpentry," the latter having reached its 38th edition.</p>
        <p>The second inquest was at the Horse and Jockey, Winwick-road, and was held within the same hour. The inquiry was into the cause of the death of a six year old boy named Wm. Wareing, son of Sarah Jane Pownall,of Winwick-road. The child "had died suddenly on Saturday morning, and as no outward signs of the cause of death were discovered by an ordinary exapination,Dr.Starkey Smith had held a post mortens examination on the body. The mother of the deceased said her child had shown no particular signs of illness. During the early part of the week be had gone to the Drill Hall for hot-pot dinner, and had probably, she said, in answer to the Coroner, been compelled to stand in the cold for an hour or more before getting served. On Friday morning he com- plained of being thirsty and vomited once. Ile had a feverish kind of thirst on him all day, and on Saturday morning he commenced to vomit again, and the doctor was sent for, but the child died before he arrived.—Dr. Starkey Smith who had made the pest mortem examination,gave evidence,from which it seemed that the child was suffering from an exceptionally small and diseased heart, and from the results of advanced pneumomia, which probably had been brought on by the chill the child would catch in standing in the cold whilst waiting for the hotpot. The verdict was " Death from natural causes."</p>
        <p>IA MM. LYMNI PETTY SESSIONS. FIIIDAY:—Before Messrs. T. P. Norris (chairman), and : -</p>
        <p>C. Lister and Dr. Brabazon. DRUNK.—John Philips was charged with being drunk at Lymm, on the 13th ult., and dismissed.—Hannah Barnsley, at Lymm, 103. and costs, or 14 days' impri- son ment.</p>
        <p>DEFAULTING LOCOMOTIVE OWNERS.—James Challers, of Statham, was charged with allowing a locomotive to pass over the highway at Warburton, at an hour other than that prescribed by the bye-laws of the Cheshire County Council.—The defendant said' he had met with an accident, and had to stop with the locomo- tive, which caused him to be late. Dismissed.—Thos. Swain, hay and straw dealer, Warrington, was charged with being the owner of a locomotive, on which he had not his name painted, and was fined ss.</p>
        <p>ON SALE.-END OP PICNIC.BI3ASON. MAGINN BROS.</p>
        <p>Have several USEFUL HORSES which they will sell ellen p to clear before the winter, suitable for Farmers no Reasonable offer refused. Also several well matched PAIRS of COBS and CARRIAGE HORSES worthy of the notice of Gentlemen and others. Park Mews. Warrington.</p>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED EMBEZZLEMENT OF [2,000.</p>
        <p>John Hewett, accountant, was remanded at Bristol yesterday, on a charge of embezzling £2,000.</p>
        <p>SIR ANDREW WALKER.</p>
        <p>The bulletin from Gatacre was that Sir Andrew Walker had spent a bad night,and that yesterday morn- ing he was not so well.</p>
        <p>REDUCTION IN SCOTCH COAL.</p>
        <p>The Fife coalmasters were offering to sell coals in the Glasgow market yesterday for shipment to the Mediter- ranean ports at 6s. 3d., which shows a reduction of 6d. per ton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>LIBERATOR PROSECUTION.</head>
        <p>THE LIBERATOR PROSECUTION.</p>
        <p>At Bow-street Police Court, London, yesterday, Henry Granville Wright, solicitor, and James William Hobbs, builder, were again charged with frauds in connection with the Liberator Building Society and J. W. Hobbs, Limited. Further evidence was given as to the payment of cheques. The prisoners were remanded until Thurs- day, bail being refused, the magistrate remarking that the evidence of yesterday and to-day had materially strengthened the case against them.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY IRISH ASSAULT 'CASE</p>
        <p>A NEWSPAPER PROPRIETOR'S WIFE AND THE</p>
        <p>NEWSAGENT.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Anestatia Fisher, wife of the proprietor of the "Munster Express," was charged at Waterford, yester- day, with assaulting Miss Hawaii, newsagent, and damaging her property. The evidence called showed that there was a quarrel between Mrs. Fisher and her husband, and that the assault was committed because the prosecutrix declined to stop selling Mr. Fisher's paper in her shop. The magistrates ordered Mrs. Fisher to be imprisoned for two months and to find sureties to keep the peace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>MR. JOHN BURNS, M.P., AND A LABOUR</head>
        <p>MR. JOHN BURNS, M.P., AND A LABOUR</p>
        <p>MINISTRY.</p>
        <p>Interviewed with respect to the new State Labour Department, Mr. J. Burns, M.P., the representative of the Battersea working-men, said he would like to see a Ministry of Labour established, its chief to have a seat in the Cabinet. This Ministry should deal with such subjects as the cost of living of workmen and their families, the cost of production, the efficiency of labour strikes and lock-outs, giving the views of both sides. It should insist on a return from all trades and callings on a much improved plan to that which the Govern- ment now apply to railway companies of all accidents, fatal or otherwise, and their causes. If this were done we should not hear as we did over the Manchester Ship Canal of such discrepancies in the several returns of the men killed during its construction. "We ought," he added, "to know the amount of English capital invested in foreign manufactures, and in the production of goods produced in competition with the goods made by Eng- lish labour. If this were accurately obtairucl it would throw interesting side-lights on the causes of the Lanca- shite cotton struggle, and certainly, in my opinion, it would produce overwhelming arguments in favour of the men who are now on strike."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>NATIONAL LIBERAL FEDERATION. (Continued from page 6) .</head>
        <p>THE NATIONAL LIBERAL FEDERATION. (Continued from page 6) .</p>
        <p>The Conference of the National Liberal Fedmaticin was resumed yesterday morning at Liverpool, being again attended by a large number of delegates and M.P.'s. Mr. Waddy, M.P., moved a resolution confirming a series of resolutions known as the Newcastle programme. He said the policy which it propounded was not a new one. They did not want any new policy, for they believed the old one was for the welfare of the people, and that the Government would promptly carry it out. Mr. Lloyd George, M.P., in seconding the resolution, said Wales had done so well at the last election that she deserved the first place in the Government's con- sideration. However, they would be satisfied with a complete measure of disestablishment and disendow- ment of the Church after the passing of Home Rule. They were threatened with difficulties from the House of Lords, that Constitutional Jack the Ripper, but he thought thatiWales would regard with satisfaction even the postponement of disestablishment to join in the pursuit and lynching of that culprit. The House of Lords was the key to the situation, and the future de- pended upas- how Liberal statesmen dealt with it. If they went forward, regardless of all obstruction, with their policy of sheltering the weak and protecting the op- pressed, then the Newcastle programme would be a programme no loner, but the law of the free land and a truly united empire. Mr. Fozall, unsuccessful candidate for Eassetlaw division, urged the necessity of the Government paying attention to the wants of the working classes. Mr. Skinner, of Sheffield, spoke as to the pressing im- portance of reform in the registration laws. Mr. E. J. C. Morton, M.P., advocated the payment of membus and triennial Parliaments. Lord Salisbury had threatened them with the House of Lords, but that. was the upas tree which would have to be laid low (Cheers.) 1 The Rev. Mr. Tuckwell, who described himself as the Radical parson, said the Newcastle programme, sympa- thetically understood, and courageously administered, meantland reform, leading ultimately to land nationa- lisation; abolition of unpaid magistrates, unification of London, disestablishment and disendpwment in Wales and Scotland first and then in England, manhood and womanhood suffrage payment of members, abolition of House of Lords, fiscal reform, and the reduction of the hours of labour to eight per day in dangerous employ- ments and where the majority of the trade demanded it. These measures might be too numerous to pass in one session,but not too many for the lifetime of a Parlia- ment of resolute energy and average duration. Mr. Conybeare, M.P., wished for the inclusion in the resolu- tion of an expression of opinion in favour of the policy of the further development of Central Africa, but the Chinaman ruled it out of order. The resolution was then unanimously adopted, and the proceedings ter- minated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSEUM.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>RECENT ADDITIONS.</p>
        <p>The annual change in the loan from the Science and Art Department has been made during the time that the museum has been closed and the new loan is now on view. It consists chiefly of specimens of Indian art manufactures, including a case of pottery and porcelain and glass-ware, one of metal work in brass and copper, cast, engraved, chased, and inlaid with lacquer and other metals; one of wood and ivory carvings, with some specimens of painted lacquer-work ; and one of textiles —woven and embroidered kincobs, dresses, and other pieces. An interesting Series of " fictile " ivories, that is, casts of ivory carvings, 70 in number, is hung on the west end of the Art Gallery. The examples chosen are chiefly of the early Christian and Medieval periods. An oil painting, " Liston as Paul Pry," by Clint, forms a part of the loan. LOCAL VIEWS AND PORTRAITS.</p>
        <p>The number of prints included in the local collection of the Reference Library having been very largely increased by the purchase of the cellections of the late Dr. Kendrick, the opportunity has been taken of making known the existence of a department necessarily little , known to the public, by the exhibition of a portion for a short time in the Museum. About 140 prints and drawings are shown, some of which are of very rare occurrence, and a few are probably the unique records of local conditions now passed away. In particular there is a series of views of the river at different points, including six views of Warrington Bridge at various dates from 1748 to 1830, and a view of Mersey Mills. etc., in which appears the spire of Old St. Jamcs's, on the Kuutsford-road. The Parish Church, the Market- place, with the old Court-house and the Meidfeather, the Academy, " Cromwell's lode.ings," " The Earl's lodgings," the 3ld workhouse, and many other build- ings now extant are also represented. Bewsev Hal in a variety of aspects, Orford and Newton Halls, Win. wick Church, and the Broad Oak, are the subject of other views. Portraits of about forty War- rington worthies are also exhibited, including Dr. Priestley, Gilbert Wakefield, Hamlet Winstanlev, Joseph Warrington, R. A., Mrs. Barbauld, and Dr. Barnes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>DISTRESSING FATE.</head>
        <p>A DISTRESSING FATE.</p>
        <p>Archduchess Isabella, a Vienna correspondent states, has given birth to a little Princess, her eighth daughter. The bulletin given out by the doctor says that the Archduchees is as well as can be expected. The new- born Princess is hale and hearty. There is a gipsy pro- phecy that Archduke Frederick and Archduchess Isabelfa will have twelve daughters before the son and heir is born. Archduke Frederick is the brother of the Queen of Spain, and heir to the enormous landed property of Archduke Albrecht, which his girls cannot inherit.</p>
        <p>BANK QUAY STATION, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>INTENDED EXTENSIVE ALTERATIONS.</p>
        <p>As we announced a abort time ago, the London and North-Western Railway Co.sare contemplating the execution of a radical change in the construc- tion of Bank Quay Station. From further particu- lerSto hand, it appears that they intend to expend a sum of .£50,000 upon making what , is known as an island platform of the present high level station. This has become a neceisity in consequence of the increased goods and passenger traffic. Since the station was built the general traffic passing through and using the station has more than trebled. Such is the congested condi- , tion of the lines there at certain parts of the day, that only by extraordinary vigilance on the part of the officials, and a keenly regulated train system is it possible to avoid blockages. The , , company have bad all this in consideration for some time, and now that the Ship Canal bids fair Ito create more traffic they have concluded to considerably extend the station. This forming of an island platform is the beat means that can be devised for the people. It will be effected by lay- ing a double set of lines on each aide of the plat" form. To do this the old zinc works lying to the left of the station will have to be encroached upon —and at least probably demolished. Then the ground will be banked up to the present level' lof the station. The set of lines that will be Ifarthest away from the present platform will be used for goods trains coming from the 'direction el Chester; and the set nearest the platform will be used for slow trains coming from the same direc- tion. This will entail the erecting of more platforni accommodation at the rear of the present waiting rooms and offices. Behind the east side of the platform a similar set of lines will be laid. Those farthest away again will be for goods traihs,and the nearest set to the platform will be for slow trains from the direction of St. Helens or anywhere in that direction. To make the extension on this side of the present platform means that an arch have to be erected to convey the trains right over where the booking offices now are. Then the lines on the central platform—those used now—will he for through trains and also fast trains. In addition to this the shunting arrangements are to be altered in such a way, that much of the noise now caused with the sidings opposite Parker-street will be done away with. The station offices and yards will be extended in the direction of Arley station, and right through where the present Slutcher's-lane Crossings are will be a goods yard. By these rearrangements the Company think they will be able to centralize the whole of the traffic, now , divided between the two stations.</p>
